/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T1 · NEW Lesson TC1G Business Trusts & REITs (added Oct 2026 audit).
   Source: SG Code Intro §2 (scope); Defns 18–19 and their Notes; Defn 1, Notes 6–7; Defn 2(g)–(i). Previously untested. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc1x-120", lesson:"TC1G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is within the Code's scope?",
  options:[
    {t:"An unlisted registered business trust with 120 unitholders and net tangible assets of $9 million", correct:true,
     why:"Section 2 of the Introduction says the Code 'applies to corporations with a primary listing of their equity securities, business trusts with a primary listing of their units in Singapore and REITs'. It then extends to 'unlisted public companies and unlisted registered business trusts with more than 50 shareholders or unitholders, as the case may be, and net tangible assets of $5 million or more', which 'must also observe the letter and spirit of the General Principles and Rules, wherever this is possible and appropriate'.\n\nThe unlisted registered business trust has 120 unitholders (more than 50) and NTA of $9 million ($5 million or more), so it is in scope. The Code then applies to it with the adaptations in the Note on Defn 18: units for shares, unitholders for shareholders, and the trustee-manager for the board. The Code 'does not apply to take-overs or mergers of other unlisted public companies and unlisted business trusts, or private companies'."},
    {t:"An unlisted business trust that isn't registered, with 120 unitholders and NTA of $9 million", correct:false,
     why:"The extension in section 2 covers 'unlisted registered business trusts' that meet the thresholds. The Code says it 'does not apply to take-overs or mergers of other ... unlisted business trusts'. An unlisted trust that isn't registered falls into that excluded group, whatever its size."},
    {t:"An unlisted registered business trust with 40 unitholders and NTA of $50 million", correct:false,
     why:"The unitholder test requires more than 50 unitholders. Forty fails, and both tests must be met, so the large NTA doesn't help. This trust falls among the 'other unlisted ... business trusts' outside the Code."},
    {t:"An unlisted registered business trust with 200 unitholders and NTA of $3 million", correct:false,
     why:"NTA must be $5 million or more. At $3 million, the trust fails that test, even though it has more than enough unitholders. It is outside the Code."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Intro §2; Defn 18", takeaway:"Unlisted RBT: >50 unitholders and NTA ≥ $5m." },

{ id:"tc1x-121", lesson:"TC1G", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each company-based term to how the Code reads it for a business trust or a REIT.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"'Board' of a business trust", r:"The trustee-manager"},
    {l:"'Board' of a REIT", r:"The manager"},
    {l:"Assets of a REIT", r:"Held by the trustee, including through any SPV"},
    {l:"Assets of a business trust", r:"Held by the trustee-manager, including through any SPV"},
    {l:"'Shares' and 'shareholders'", r:"Units and unitholders"}
  ],
  why:"The Notes on Defns 18 and 19 say references to a company 'should be taken as a reference to a registered business trust or business trust [or a REIT] and/or a company as the context requires'.\n\nBusiness trusts (Note on Defn 18): shares, shareholders and board refer, where appropriate, to 'units, unitholders and the trustee-manager'. Actions of the company are actions of the trustee-manager and/or its directors; voting rights held by the company are those held 'whether directly or indirectly, by the trustee-manager and/or any of its directors'; and assets are those 'owned, controlled or held by the trustee-manager', including 'through any special purpose vehicle'.\n\nREITs (Note on Defn 19): shares, shareholders and board refer to 'units, unitholders and manager'. Actions of the company are those of 'the trustee (in its capacity as trustee of a REIT) or the manager and/or any of its directors'. Voting rights are those held by the trustee (as trustee) or the manager and/or its directors. Assets are those held 'by the trustee (in its capacity as trustee of a REIT)', including through any SPV.\n\nThe split for REITs follows how they work: the manager runs the REIT, while the trustee holds its property. Each Note ends: 'In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'",
  cite:"SG Code Defns 18–19 and Notes", takeaway:"BT: trustee-manager. REIT: manager runs, trustee holds." },

{ id:"tc1x-122", lesson:"TC1G", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In applying the Code to a REIT, references to the 'board' of a company normally refer to the REIT's trustee.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Defn 19 says: 'In the context of a REIT, references to shares, shareholders and board of a company would, where appropriate refer to units, unitholders and manager.' The board corresponds to the manager, not the trustee.\n\nThis reflects how a REIT is structured. The manager makes the decisions that a board would make for a company: strategy, acquisitions and disposals, and the response to an offer. The trustee holds the REIT's assets on trust for unitholders and oversees the manager. So the Note treats assets held by the REIT as assets held by the trustee (in its capacity as trustee), including through SPVs, while the 'board' functions sit with the manager. In practice, this means it is the manager's directors who carry obligations like GP 7's restraint on frustrating action and GP 13's duty to advise unitholders as a whole. Actions 'of the company' can be taken by either the trustee (as trustee) or the manager and/or its directors."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The Note on Defn 19 maps 'board' to the manager. The trustee's role is different: it holds the REIT's assets, which is why the Note says the REIT's assets are those held by the trustee. A trustee isn't the REIT's decision-making board."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 19, Note", takeaway:"REIT 'board' = manager, not trustee." },

{ id:"tc1x-123", lesson:"TC1G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lotus REIT is subject to an offer. Its manager proposes selling Lotus's largest shopping mall during the offer. The mall is held by a special purpose vehicle wholly owned by Lotus's trustee, in its capacity as trustee of Lotus. The manager argues that the mall belongs to the SPV, not to Lotus.",
  prompt:"How does the Code treat the mall?",
  options:[
    {t:"As Lotus's asset, since REIT assets include those held via an SPV; frustration rules apply", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Defn 19 says: 'Where a reference to a company should refer to a REIT, assets owned, controlled or held by the company should refer to assets owned, controlled or held by the trustee (in its capacity as trustee of a REIT). This would include assets owned, controlled or held by the trustee through any special purpose vehicle.'\n\nThe SPV structure doesn't take the mall outside the REIT for Code purposes. It is an asset of Lotus. The manager is the REIT's 'board' under the same Note, so GP 7 applies to it: once Lotus has received a bona fide offer, the manager must not, without unitholder approval in general meeting, take action that could effectively frustrate the offer or deny unitholders the chance to decide on its merits. Selling the largest mall during the offer is the kind of action GP 7 and Rule 5 target.\n\nThe Note on Defn 18 contains the equivalent provision for business trusts, where assets held by the trustee-manager through any SPV count as the trust's assets. In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted."},
    {t:"As the SPV's asset, outside the REIT, so the manager may sell freely", correct:false,
     why:"The Note on Defn 19 expressly includes assets held by the trustee 'through any special purpose vehicle'. Without that, a REIT could avoid GP 7 by holding everything through SPVs. The mall is treated as Lotus's asset."},
    {t:"As the manager's own asset, since the manager runs the REIT and controls the SPV", correct:false,
     why:"The manager is the decision-maker, the REIT's 'board'. But the Note says the REIT's assets are those held by the trustee, in its capacity as trustee, including through SPVs. The mall is Lotus's asset held through the trustee, not the manager's."},
    {t:"As an asset of Lotus, but GP 7 and Rule 5 don't apply to REITs in any case", correct:false,
     why:"The Code applies to REITs (Introduction §2), and the Notes on Defn 19 adapt company-based references so that the General Principles work for them. GP 7 applies to the manager, as the REIT's board, in relation to the REIT's assets."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 19, Note; GP 7", takeaway:"Trustee's SPV assets are REIT assets." },

{ id:"tc1x-124", lesson:"TC1G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under Note 6 on acting in concert, where a reference to a company is read as a registered business trust or business trust, with whom is the concert party relationship?",
  options:[
    {t:"The trustee-manager; consult the Council if it acts for more than one side at once", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 says: 'Where a reference to a company should be taken as a reference to a registered business trust or a business trust, the concert party relationship is with the trustee-manager of the registered business trust or business trust.' This follows from the trustee-manager's role: it both holds the trust's assets and manages its business, so it stands in the company's place.\n\nThe Note continues: 'The Council should be consulted if the trustee-manager acts at the same time for more than one of the following: (a) offeror or possible offeror; (b) competing offeror or possible competing offeror; and (c) offeree registered business trust or business trust.' Trustee-managers often belong to a sponsor group that runs several vehicles. If the same trustee-manager is on two sides of the same deal, the usual presumptions don't map neatly, and the Council decides how to treat the position. Associate category (g) separately makes the trustee-manager, together with its group, an associate."},
    {t:"The unitholders collectively, since they are the beneficial owners of the trust's assets", correct:false,
     why:"Unitholders are the people protected. Note 6 places the concert relationship with the trustee-manager, which acts for the trust in the way a company's board and company together would."},
    {t:"The trust's auditors, who sign off its accounts each year", correct:false,
     why:"Auditors have no role in Note 6. The concert relationship is with the trustee-manager, the entity that holds and manages the trust's assets."},
    {t:"There's none, because a business trust isn't a company under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"The Code applies to business trusts with a primary listing in Singapore and to in-scope unlisted registered business trusts. Its company references are adapted, and Note 6 specifies that the concert relationship is with the trustee-manager."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 1, Note 6", takeaway:"BT concert link: trustee-manager; dual roles → consult." },

{ id:"tc1x-125", lesson:"TC1G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Magnolia Trust Co is trustee of Orchid REIT, which is making an offer for Peony REIT. Orchid REIT and its concert parties hold 27% of Peony's units. Separately, Magnolia Trust Co holds a further 5% of Peony's units as trustee of several unrelated private trusts.",
  prompt:"For calculating the concert group's voting rights in Peony, what is the normal position?",
  options:[
    {t:"27%: Magnolia's holdings as trustee of unrelated trusts aren't normally counted", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 on acting in concert says a REIT's concert relationship is with its manager and its trustee. 'In relation to the trustee, the concert party relationship is normally limited to the trustee (including its directors) acting in the capacity as trustee of the REIT.' So Magnolia is in Orchid's concert group only as trustee of Orchid REIT.\n\nThe Note then says: 'For the purposes of calculating the voting rights held by a group acting in concert, the voting rights held by a trustee of unrelated trusts will not normally be counted. In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.' Magnolia's 5% held for unrelated private trusts isn't counted. The group stays at 27%, below the 30% threshold.\n\nThe reasoning is practical. Trust companies act for many unconnected trusts and don't run them as a bloc. Counting all their holdings would push concert groups over thresholds artificially. The word 'normally' leaves room: if the unrelated trusts' votes were in fact being used to support Orchid's offer, the Council could decide differently."},
    {t:"32%: everything Magnolia holds, in whatever capacity, is counted as part of the concert group", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 limits the trustee's concert relationship to its capacity 'as trustee of the REIT', and says voting rights held by a trustee of unrelated trusts 'will not normally be counted'. Adding the 5% would push the group to 32%, artificially crossing the 30% line."},
    {t:"5%: only Magnolia's own holdings count", correct:false,
     why:"This turns the rule upside down. The group's 27% (Orchid REIT and its concert parties) counts. What is normally left out is the 5% Magnolia holds as trustee of unrelated trusts."},
    {t:"It can't be decided; the trustee is never a concert party", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 expressly says a REIT's concert relationship is with its manager and its trustee. The trustee is a concert party, but normally only in its capacity as trustee of that REIT, and its unrelated trust holdings aren't normally counted."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 1, Note 7", takeaway:"REIT trustee: concert as trustee only; unrelated trusts not counted." },

{ id:"tc1x-126", lesson:"TC1G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Saffron Trustees acts as trustee of Orchid REIT, which is preparing an offer for Peony REIT. Saffron is also trustee of Peony REIT.",
  prompt:"What does Note 7 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult the Council: as REIT trustee, Saffron acts for both offeror and offeree at once", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 says: 'The Council should be consulted if a manager or a trustee, in its capacity as trustee of a REIT, acts at the same time for more than one of the following: (a) offeror or possible offeror; (b) competing offeror or possible competing offeror; and (c) offeree REIT.' Saffron is trustee of the offeror (Orchid) and of the offeree (Peony), which is two of the listed roles.\n\nThe default rules would give an awkward result here. A REIT's concert relationship is with its trustee, so on a literal reading Saffron would be in concert with both sides of the same offer. Saffron would also be holding assets for both, which raises questions about information flows and conflicts. Rather than apply the presumptions mechanically, the Code sends the question to the Council. The same rule applies to a manager that acts for more than one side, which can happen when a sponsor group runs several REITs. Note 6 has an equivalent rule for trustee-managers of business trusts."},
    {t:"Nothing: trustees are neutral, so acting for both sides at once is fine", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 specifically says the Council should be consulted when a trustee, in its capacity as trustee of a REIT, acts for more than one of the offeror, a competing offeror and the offeree REIT. Neutrality is exactly the question the Council needs to consider."},
    {t:"Saffron must resign as trustee of one of the two REITs before the offer", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't require resignation. It requires consultation, so the Council can decide how the concert-party and other Rules should apply to Saffron's dual role."},
    {t:"Saffron's units in Peony are automatically counted with Orchid's for Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 doesn't set out an automatic answer for this dual role. It requires consultation. Saffron, as Peony's trustee, holds Peony's assets, not units in Peony; the question is how its position should be treated, which the Council decides."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 1, Note 7", takeaway:"Trustee or manager on two sides → consult." },

{ id:"tc1x-127", lesson:"TC1G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How do the associate categories treat a REIT's trustee compared with its manager?",
  options:[
    {t:"The trustee only in its REIT capacity; the manager together with its whole corporate group", correct:true,
     why:"Associate category (h) covers 'any trustee (in its capacity as trustee of a REIT) of the offeror, the offeree or any REIT that relates to the offeror or offeree in any of the ways set out in (a) above'. The trustee comes in alone and only in its trustee capacity, for the same reason as Note 7 on acting in concert: trust companies act for many unconnected clients.\n\nCategory (i) covers 'any manager (together with its parent, subsidiaries, and fellow subsidiaries, and its associated companies and companies of which it is an associated company)' of the offeror, the offeree, or a related REIT. The manager brings its whole group, because a manager is usually part of a sponsor group whose other members may have interests in the outcome.\n\nCategory (g) treats a business trust's trustee-manager the same way as a REIT manager: it is an associate together with its group. These categories mean dealings by these entities during an offer must be disclosed under Rule 12."},
    {t:"Both are associates together with their whole corporate groups, whatever capacity they act in", correct:false,
     why:"That is true for the manager (category (i)) and the trustee-manager of a business trust (category (g)), but not for the REIT trustee. Category (h) limits the trustee to its capacity 'as trustee of a REIT', without its group."},
    {t:"Neither is an associate, because both act for the unitholders rather than for themselves", correct:false,
     why:"Both are expressly listed. Category (h) covers the trustee (as trustee) and category (i) covers the manager with its group. They have interests in the outcome beyond an ordinary unitholder's."},
    {t:"Only the trustee is an associate, because it holds the REIT's assets; the manager is not", correct:false,
     why:"The manager is an associate too, under category (i), and with a wider reach than the trustee, since its group is included. The manager is the REIT's decision-maker, so its interest in the outcome is central."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 2(g)–(i)", takeaway:"REIT trustee: capacity only. Manager/TM: with group." },

{ id:"tc1x-128", lesson:"TC1G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lantern Capital is the manager of Orchid REIT. Lantern's parent company and a fellow subsidiary of Lantern both buy units in Peony REIT, which is subject to an offer from Orchid REIT.",
  prompt:"Are the parent and the fellow subsidiary normally associates?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: category (i) covers the manager together with its corporate group", correct:true,
     why:"Category (i) makes an associate of 'any manager (together with its parent, subsidiaries, and fellow subsidiaries, and its associated companies and companies of which it is an associated company) of the offeror, the offeree or any REIT that relates to the offeror or offeree in any of the ways set out in (a) above'.\n\nLantern is the manager of Orchid REIT, the offeror. Its parent and its fellow subsidiary are both expressly included. Their dealings in Peony units during the offer must therefore be disclosed under Rule 12.\n\nWhy include the whole group? A REIT manager is typically owned by a sponsor that runs other businesses and often holds units in the REIT. Its group has a commercial interest in the offer succeeding: management fees grow with the REIT, and the sponsor may have its own plans for the combined portfolio. Compare category (h): a REIT trustee is an associate only in its capacity as trustee, without its group."},
    {t:"No: only the manager itself is an associate, not any of its group companies", correct:false,
     why:"Category (i) expressly includes the manager's 'parent, subsidiaries, and fellow subsidiaries, and its associated companies and companies of which it is an associated company'. Both the parent and the fellow subsidiary are within it."},
    {t:"No: they don't manage Orchid REIT, so they have no interest in it", correct:false,
     why:"They don't need to. Category (i) extends from the manager to its group. A sponsor group's interest in the outcome doesn't depend on which group company holds the management mandate."},
    {t:"Only if each of them holds 5% or more of Peony's units", correct:false,
     why:"The 5% test is a separate category, (f). Category (i) applies regardless of the size of the holding, because the interest comes from the group's connection with the offeror REIT's manager."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 2(i)", takeaway:"Manager's whole group = associates." },

{ id:"tc1x-129", lesson:"TC1G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ember Trust, a registered business trust listed in Singapore, is building a stake in Flint Ltd before a possible offer. Ember's trustee-manager holds Flint shares on Ember's behalf through a wholly owned subsidiary. Separately, one of the trustee-manager's directors buys Flint shares with his own money.",
  prompt:"How does the Note on Defn 18 treat the voting rights 'held by' Ember?",
  options:[
    {t:"The subsidiary's shares count, as held for the trust; the director's personal purchase doesn't", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Defn 18 says: 'Where a reference to a company should refer to a registered business trust or business trust, voting rights owned, controlled or held by the company should refer to voting rights owned, controlled or held, whether directly or indirectly, by the trustee-manager and/or any of its directors (in their respective capacity on behalf of the registered business trust or business trust).'\n\nThe Flint shares held through the trustee-manager's subsidiary for Ember are held indirectly by the trustee-manager on Ember's behalf, so they are Ember's voting rights. That matters for the 30% threshold in Rule 14 and for any price rules if Ember goes on to bid.\n\nThe director's own purchase is different. He isn't acting 'in [his] capacity on behalf of' Ember, so his shares aren't Ember's. But they aren't irrelevant. The trustee-manager stands in the place of Ember's board, so its directors are in a position like a company's directors, and the concert presumptions and associate categories that reach directors may catch his purchase. The Note also says that action by the 'company' means action by the trustee-manager and/or its directors on the trust's behalf. Each paragraph ends: 'In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'"},
    {t:"Only shares registered in the trustee-manager's own name count", correct:false,
     why:"The Note says 'whether directly or indirectly'. Shares the trustee-manager holds for Ember through a subsidiary are held indirectly, and they count as Ember's voting rights. Otherwise a stake could be hidden simply by holding it one level down."},
    {t:"Both holdings count as Ember's, because the director works for the trustee-manager", correct:false,
     why:"The Note limits the reading to holdings 'in their respective capacity on behalf of' the trust. A director buying with his own money isn't acting for Ember, so his shares aren't Ember's holding. They may still matter under the concert presumptions, which is a separate question."},
    {t:"Neither: a business trust can't hold voting rights in a company", correct:false,
     why:"A business trust holds assets, including shares, through its trustee-manager. The Note on Defn 18 exists precisely so that Code references to a company's voting rights work for a trust. The subsidiary's holding counts as Ember's."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 18, Note", takeaway:"BT holds through its trustee-manager, directly or indirectly." }

]);

