/* ---- data/t3p1/TC1E.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T1 · Lesson TC1E Schemes, Trust Schemes & Amalgamations (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code Defn 12 and Note on Defn 12, conditions (a)–(h) (new (e)–(f), relettered (g)–(h), Order 32 in 2026).
   Replaces TC1E in takeoverCode1.js. Previously untested: parent-for-subsidiary offers, application of (e) to dates, (f)(B),
   conditions satisfiable only on/after sanction, MBO abstention, merger-of-equals IFAs, creeper limb of (b), trust-scheme
   vote arithmetic, amalgamation date arithmetic. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc1-035", lesson:"TC1E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the Code treat schemes of arrangement, trust schemes and amalgamations?",
  options:[
    {t:"All are subject to the Code, though the Council may exempt them from some Rules", correct:true,
     why:"Defn 12 says 'offer' includes 'take-over and merger transactions, howsoever effected, including reverse take-overs, schemes of arrangement, trust schemes, amalgamations, partial offers and also offers by a parent company for shares in its subsidiary'. The Note on Defn 12 then says: 'All schemes of arrangement, trust schemes and amalgamations are subject to the provisions of the Code. However, the Council may, subject to conditions, exempt' them from listed Rules.\n\nThe reason for exemptions is that these transactions run on a different mechanism. A scheme binds all shareholders through a statutory vote and court sanction, rather than through individual acceptances of an offer. Rules built around acceptances, offer timetables and withdrawal rights don't fit. The conditions in (a) to (h) replace those protections with others: abstention by interested parties, disclosure, independent advice and, since 2026, time limits and pre-sanction confirmations. The General Principles still apply throughout. One exclusion from 'offer': offers for non-voting non-equity capital don't come within the Code."},
    {t:"They fall outside the Code because the court supervises each of them closely instead", correct:false,
     why:"Court involvement doesn't take a scheme outside the Code. The Note on Defn 12 says expressly that all schemes, trust schemes and amalgamations are subject to the Code. The court's role (sanction for schemes, approval for trust schemes) sits alongside the Code, and the Council's conditions for exemption ensure shareholders are protected before the court is asked to act."},
    {t:"Only amalgamations are subject to the Code; schemes are not", correct:false,
     why:"All three are subject to the Code. If anything, amalgamations get the narrowest exemptions, only from Rules 20.1, 22, 28 and 29, while schemes and trust schemes can be exempted from a longer list that includes Rule 14."},
    {t:"Only the General Principles apply to them, not the Rules", correct:false,
     why:"The Rules apply too, unless and to the extent the Council grants an exemption from particular Rules. Even then the exemption is conditional and specific: the Council exempts from named Rules, and everything else, including all the General Principles, continues to apply."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12 and Note", takeaway:"Schemes: in the Code, with conditional exemptions." },

{ id:"tc1x-080", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Olive Ltd owns 72% of its listed subsidiary, Pine Ltd. It proposes (1) an offer for the remaining 28% of Pine's ordinary shares and (2) a separate offer for Pine's redeemable preference shares, which carry no votes and a fixed dividend with no other right to participate in profits.",
  prompt:"How does the Code apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"The Code applies to the ordinary-share offer, not the non-voting, non-equity preference offer", correct:true,
     why:"Defn 12 says 'offer' includes, wherever appropriate, 'offers by a parent company for shares in its subsidiary'. So Olive's offer for Pine's minority ordinary shares is an offer under the Code, even though Olive already has statutory control. The minority still needs the Code's protection, perhaps more than usual, given the parent's dominance over the subsidiary's board and information.\n\nDefn 12 ends: 'But offers for non-voting non-equity capital do not come within the Code.' Pine's preference shares carry no votes and have only a fixed dividend with no further right to participate in profits, which makes them both non-voting and non-equity. Olive's offer for them is outside the Code. If the preference shares carried votes, or participated in profits beyond a fixed amount, the answer would be different."},
    {t:"Neither: a parent's offer for its own subsidiary is an internal reorganisation", correct:false,
     why:"Defn 12 expressly includes 'offers by a parent company for shares in its subsidiary'. The minority shareholders of a listed subsidiary are exactly the people the Code protects. Olive's 72% doesn't take the offer for the remaining ordinary shares outside the Code."},
    {t:"Both, because any offer for any class of share is within the Code", correct:false,
     why:"Defn 12 carves out 'offers for non-voting non-equity capital'. Pine's preference shares have no votes and only a fixed dividend, so the offer for them falls outside the Code."},
    {t:"Only the preference-share offer, since Olive already controls the ordinary shares of Pine as its parent", correct:false,
     why:"This gets both points backwards. Control by the parent doesn't remove the Code from an offer for the minority ordinary shares, and the preference shares, being non-voting and non-equity, are the ones that fall outside it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12", takeaway:"Parent-for-subsidiary: in. Non-voting non-equity: out." },

{ id:"tc1-036", lesson:"TC1E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which Rule can the Council exempt a scheme of arrangement from, but not an amalgamation?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rule 14 on mandatory offers", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Defn 12 gives two lists. A scheme of arrangement or trust scheme can be exempted from: Rule 14 (mandatory offers); Rule 15 (voluntary offers); Rule 16 (partial offers); Rule 17 (type of consideration); Note 1(b) on Rule 19 (appropriate offers to holders of convertibles); Rule 20.1 (keeping a revised offer open 14 days); Rule 21 (purchases above the offer price); Rule 22 (offer timetable); Rule 28 (acceptances); Rule 29 (withdrawal of acceptances); and Rule 33.2 (6-month delay before buying above the offer price).\n\nAn amalgamation can be exempted only from Rules 20.1, 22, 28 and 29.\n\nRules 20.1, 22, 28 and 29 are common to both lists because they are all built around the mechanics of an offer that shareholders accept individually, which neither a scheme nor an amalgamation uses. The scheme list adds the mandatory offer, voluntary and partial offer rules, consideration rules and price protections. For a scheme, condition (b) deals with control by an informed abstention vote instead."},
    {t:"Rule 22 on the offer timetable", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 22 is on both lists. A scheme and an amalgamation can each be exempted from the offer timetable, because neither runs on an acceptance-period timetable. Amalgamations have their own timetable in condition (h) instead: posting within 35 days and effectiveness by the 60th day after posting."},
    {t:"Rule 28 on acceptances", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 28 is on both lists. In a scheme or amalgamation, shareholders vote rather than accept, so the Rules on acceptances don't fit either transaction, and both can be exempted from them."},
    {t:"Rule 29 on withdrawing acceptances", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 29 is on both lists. Withdrawal rights only make sense where shareholders have accepted an offer. Since neither a scheme nor an amalgamation involves acceptances, both can be exempted."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note", takeaway:"Amalgamations: only 20.1, 22, 28, 29." },

{ id:"tc1x-081", lesson:"TC1E", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The Council can exempt an amalgamation from Rule 21 (purchases of voting rights at above the offer price).",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Defn 12 limits amalgamation exemptions to four Rules: 'Rule 20.1 to keep the offer open for 14 days after it is revised; Rule 22 on offer timetable; Rule 28 on acceptances; and Rule 29 on the right of acceptors to withdraw their acceptances.' Rule 21 isn't among them.\n\nRule 21 is on the list for schemes of arrangement and trust schemes ('Rule 21 on purchases of voting rights in any scheme company at above the offer price'), alongside Rule 33.2 on the 6-month delay before buying above the offer price. So the price protections in Rules 21 and 33.2 can be disapplied for a scheme, subject to conditions, but not for an amalgamation. When working out which Rules apply, begin with the transaction type and check its specific list: being a 'merger' doesn't unlock the scheme list."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 21 appears only on the list for schemes of arrangement and trust schemes. The amalgamation list is limited to Rules 20.1, 22, 28 and 29, so the Council's exemption power for amalgamations doesn't reach Rule 21."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note", takeaway:"Rule 21 exemption: schemes only." },

{ id:"tc1-037", lesson:"TC1E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the conditions for exemption, who must abstain from voting on the scheme?",
  options:[
    {t:"The offeror, its concert parties, and common holders of 5%+ in both companies", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (a): 'the offeror and its concert parties as well as the common substantial shareholders of the merging companies (i.e. those holding 5% or more interests in both the companies to be merged) abstain from voting on the scheme of arrangement, trust scheme or amalgamation'. The offeror side has an obvious conflict: it is the buyer. A 5%-plus holder in both companies benefits on both sides of the deal, so its vote doesn't reflect the interests of the target's independent shareholders.\n\nThe other conditions work alongside this. Condition (b) requires persons who will acquire or consolidate effective control through the scheme to abstain and be disclosed. Condition (c) requires directors on both boards, or acting in concert with the (a) or (b) persons, to abstain from recommending. Condition (d) requires the company that is in effect the offeree to appoint an independent financial adviser, and in a reverse take-over or merger of equals, each merging company must do so."},
    {t:"Only the offeror itself, and not its concert parties", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (a) extends to the offeror's concert parties and to common substantial shareholders with 5% or more in both merging companies. Limiting abstention to the offeror itself would let its allies, and holders who gain on both sides, decide the outcome for the independent shareholders."},
    {t:"Every director of the offeree company, whether interested or not", correct:false,
     why:"Directors aren't required to abstain from voting just because they are directors. Condition (c) requires conflicted directors (those on both boards, or acting in concert with the (a) or (b) persons) to abstain from making a recommendation. Whether a director must abstain from voting depends on whether he falls within (a) or (b), for example as a concert party of the offeror."},
    {t:"Nobody needs to: the court's sanction hearing itself protects the minority shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Abstention is a condition of the Council's exemption. The court's sanction process looks at whether the scheme is fair and properly approved, but the Code requires its own protections first. Without abstention by interested parties, the Council won't normally exempt the scheme from the Rules."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (a), (c), (d)", takeaway:"Offeror side + 5% common holders abstain." },

{ id:"tc1x-082", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A consortium is buying Quince Ltd by scheme of arrangement. Quince's CEO, Mr Lau, who is also a Quince director and holds 4% of its shares, is a member of the consortium and will hold equity in the bidding vehicle.",
  prompt:"What do the exemption conditions require of Mr Lau?",
  options:[
    {t:"Abstain from voting, as an offeror concert party, and from recommending, as a Quince director", correct:true,
     why:"As a member of the consortium, Mr Lau is acting in concert with the offeror. Condition (a) requires 'the offeror and its concert parties' to abstain from voting on the scheme. His 4% stake can't be voted.\n\nCondition (c) requires 'the directors of a merging company who are also directors of the other merging company or who are acting in concert with those persons in (a) or (b) above' to 'abstain from making a recommendation on the scheme'. Mr Lau is a Quince director acting in concert with the offeror, so he can't join the board's recommendation either.\n\nThis is the standard management-buyout structure. The insider is on the buyer's side, so both his vote and his advice are conflicted. The recommendation is left to the independent directors, advised by the independent financial adviser that condition (d) requires. GP 13 points the same way: directors advising shareholders must have regard to shareholders as a whole, not their own interests."},
    {t:"He may vote his 4% at the meeting but must not make any recommendation", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (a) requires the offeror's concert parties to abstain from voting. As a consortium member, Mr Lau is a concert party, so he can't vote his shares on the scheme. He must also abstain from recommending under condition (c)."},
    {t:"He may join the board's recommendation but must not vote his shares", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (c) bars directors acting in concert with the offeror from making a recommendation. Mr Lau's conflict affects his advice as much as his vote, so both are restricted."},
    {t:"Nothing more, because the independent financial adviser's advice already protects the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The IFA required by condition (d) is an additional protection, not a substitute for abstention. The conditions apply cumulatively: interested parties abstain from voting, conflicted directors abstain from recommending, and the offeree appoints an IFA."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (a), (c), (d); GP 13", takeaway:"MBO director: no vote, no recommendation." },

{ id:"tc1-038", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Alpha Ltd will acquire Beta Ltd by a scheme, paying in new Alpha shares. Mr Ong holds 12% of Alpha and a large stake in Beta. After the scheme, he and his concert parties will hold 34% of Alpha.",
  prompt:"What does the exemption require in relation to Mr Ong?",
  options:[
    {t:"They abstain, and the document warns that a yes vote lets them take control without an offer", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (b) applies to 'those persons and their concert parties who, as a result of the scheme ... would acquire 30% or more voting rights in a merging company or a new entity that holds one or both of the merging companies'. Mr Ong goes from 12% to 34% of Alpha, so he acquires effective control of a merging company. He and his concert parties must 'abstain from voting at the meeting of that merging company to approve the scheme'.\n\nAlpha's scheme document 'must contain advice to the effect that by voting for the scheme ... shareholders are agreeing to such persons and their concert parties acquiring or consolidating effective control in the merging company without having to make a general offer'. It must also disclose their names, their current voting rights, and their voting rights after the scheme. This works like a whitewash: independent Alpha shareholders knowingly give up the general offer they would otherwise get under GP 5. Since Mr Ong holds 5% or more in both companies, condition (a) also requires him to abstain as a common substantial shareholder."},
    {t:"He must make a general offer for all of Alpha's shares before the scheme", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (b) substitutes an informed vote of independent shareholders for the general offer. If Mr Ong and his concert parties abstain and the document contains the required advice and disclosure, the scheme can proceed without a separate offer for Alpha, provided the Council grants the exemption."},
    {t:"Nothing: condition (b) applies only to holders who end up above 50% of the votes", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (b) applies to anyone acquiring 30% or more of the voting rights in a merging company or a new holding entity. It also covers a 30–50% holder who would increase by more than 1% in 6 months. Statutory control (above 50%) isn't the test."},
    {t:"He may vote with everyone else, provided he discloses his holding clearly in the scheme document", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is required, but it doesn't replace abstention. Condition (b) requires both: he and his concert parties abstain at Alpha's meeting, and the document discloses their names and voting rights before and after the scheme."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (a)–(b)", takeaway:"Control via scheme: abstain, advise, disclose." },

{ id:"tc1x-083", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mrs Tan and her concert parties hold 40% of Rowan Ltd. Rowan is merging with another company by scheme. As a result of the scheme, the group's holding in Rowan will rise to 43%.",
  prompt:"Does condition (b) apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: a 30–50% group gaining over 1% must abstain, with the advice and disclosures given", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (b) has two limbs. The first catches persons who would acquire 30% or more. The second catches persons who, 'if they together already hold between 30% and 50% of the merging company's voting rights before the scheme ..., would increase their voting rights in such merging company by more than 1% in any period of 6 months'. This mirrors the creeper rule in Defn 11 and Rule 14.1(b).\n\nMrs Tan's group is at 40%, in the 30–50% band, and rises by 3%, more than 1%. So they 'abstain from voting at the meeting of that merging company'. Rowan's document must advise that voting for the scheme means agreeing to them 'acquiring or consolidating effective control ... without having to make a general offer', and must disclose their names and their voting rights before and after.\n\nIf the group were already above 50%, or the increase were 1% or less, this limb wouldn't apply. If the group started at 28% and finished at 31%, the first limb (acquiring 30% or more) would catch it instead."},
    {t:"No: the group already has effective control at 40%", correct:false,
     why:"Already holding effective control doesn't help. The second limb of condition (b) is specifically about groups between 30% and 50% that would increase by more than 1%, which is consolidating effective control. A 3% increase triggers it."},
    {t:"No: condition (b) only applies to persons crossing the 30% threshold", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (b) has a second limb for those already holding between 30% and 50% who would increase by more than 1% in any period of 6 months. Ignoring it would let a 30–50% holder use a scheme to creep up without the abstention and disclosure safeguards."},
    {t:"Only if the group's combined holding will be above 50% once the scheme has fully taken effect", correct:false,
     why:"The test is an increase of more than 1% from a starting point between 30% and 50%. Crossing 50% isn't required. A move from 40% to 43% is enough."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (b); Defn 11", takeaway:"Scheme creeper: 30–50% group adding >1% → abstain." },

{ id:"tc1x-084", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sycamore Ltd and Teak Ltd, two listed companies of similar size, agree a 'merger of equals' by scheme of arrangement.",
  prompt:"What does condition (d) require about independent advice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Each merging company appoints its own independent financial adviser", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (d) says: 'the merging company which is in effect the offeree company appoints an independent financial adviser to advise its shareholders on the scheme ... Where the scheme ... involves a reverse take-over or a \"merger of equals\", each of the merging companies must appoint an independent financial adviser to advise their respective shareholders. In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'\n\nIn an ordinary scheme, one company is clearly the target, and its shareholders need independent advice on whether to give up their shares. In a merger of equals, both sets of shareholders are giving up something substantial: each company's holders end up with a share of a combined business they no longer control alone. Either company could be regarded as 'in effect the offeree'. The same logic applies to a reverse take-over, where the nominal offeror's shareholders lose control. So each company appoints its own IFA. GP 8 reflects the same principle: a board approached about an offer should seek competent independent advice."},
    {t:"Only the smaller of the two merging companies needs to appoint an IFA", correct:false,
     why:"For an ordinary scheme, the company that is 'in effect the offeree' appoints an IFA. But condition (d) says that in a merger of equals each merging company must appoint one. Size doesn't decide it, and with similar-sized companies there may be no clear offeree anyway."},
    {t:"The two companies may share a single IFA to save cost", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (d) requires each company to appoint an IFA 'to advise their respective shareholders'. The two sets of shareholders have different interests in the terms of the merger, so a single adviser couldn't independently advise both."},
    {t:"No IFA is needed in a merger of equals between them", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (d) says the opposite. A merger of equals is one of the two cases where each merging company, not just one, must appoint an independent financial adviser."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (d); GP 8", takeaway:"Merger of equals / RTO: an IFA each." },

{ id:"tc1-039", lesson:"TC1E", type:"fill", amended:true,
  prompt:"Except with the Council's consent, the scheme meeting must be held within ____ months of the announcement of the scheme.",
  accept:["6","six","6 months","six months"], answer:"6 months",
  why:"Condition (e), added in 2026: 'Except with the Council's consent, the scheme meeting must be held within 6 months of the announcement of the scheme.' Through condition (g), which now requires trust schemes to meet '(a), (b), (c), (d), (e) and (f)', the same time limit applies to trust schemes. Amalgamations aren't subject to (e). They have their own timetable in condition (h): posting within 35 days of the announcement and effectiveness by the 60th day after posting.\n\nThe purpose is to stop schemes hanging over the offeree for too long. During the offer period, the offeree's board is restricted by GP 7 and Rule 5 from frustrating action, and its shareholders and the market are left in uncertainty. A 6-month limit on reaching the shareholder vote keeps the process moving, while 'Except with the Council's consent' leaves room for genuine delays, such as complex regulatory approvals before the meeting.",
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (e)", takeaway:"Scheme meeting within 6 months (new 2026)." },

{ id:"tc1x-085", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"A scheme for Walnut Ltd is announced on 1 March. Because of a complicated restructuring step, the parties plan to hold the scheme meeting on 15 October of the same year.",
  prompt:"What does the Code require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Council consent: the meeting is over 6 months after the announcement", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (e), added in 2026, says: 'Except with the Council's consent, the scheme meeting must be held within 6 months of the announcement of the scheme.' Six months from 1 March is 1 September. A meeting on 15 October is about six and a half months after the announcement, so it is outside the window.\n\nThe parties don't automatically lose the exemption. The words 'Except with the Council's consent' let them ask for more time. They should go to the Council early, explaining why the restructuring step makes the delay necessary. Without consent, the scheme wouldn't meet condition (e), and the Council wouldn't normally grant the exemption from the Rules that the scheme depends on.\n\nIf the 6-month period happened to end on a non-business day, Note 3 on the Definitions would extend it to the next business day, but that wouldn't help here."},
    {t:"Nothing: there's no time limit at all for holding a scheme meeting under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"That was the position before 2026. Condition (e) now requires the scheme meeting to be held within 6 months of the announcement, unless the Council consents. A meeting on 15 October, after a 1 March announcement, needs that consent."},
    {t:"The meeting must be held by 1 June, within 3 months of announcing", correct:false,
     why:"The limit in condition (e) is 6 months, not 3. The deadline here is 1 September, and the planned October meeting misses it, so consent is needed."},
    {t:"The court's permission, not the Council's", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (e) is a Code condition for exemption, and the exception is 'with the Council's consent'. The court's timetable for convening meetings is a separate matter; it doesn't relieve the parties of the Code's 6-month limit."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (e)", takeaway:"Announced 1 Mar → meet by 1 Sep, or get consent." },

{ id:"tc1-040", lesson:"TC1E", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Under condition (f), added in 2026, what must the offeror and offeree company normally do before the court sanction hearing?",
  options:[
    {t:"Confirm every condition is met or waived, except post-sanction ones, and undertake to be bound", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (f): 'Except with the Council's consent, prior to the court sanction hearing, the offeror and the offeree company must: (i) confirm to the Council that all of the conditions to the offer have been either satisfied or waived, other than any conditions which are capable of being satisfied only upon or following the scheme being sanctioned (which conditions should normally be specified in the scheme circular); and (ii) undertake to be bound by the terms of the scheme upon the sanction of the scheme by the Court.'\n\nThe problem it addresses is the scheme that shareholders have approved and the court is asked to sanction while the offeror still has conditions it could invoke to walk away. Shareholders would be left uncertain after voting. Condition (f) means that by the sanction hearing, the deal is effectively locked in: everything that can be satisfied or waived has been, and the parties are bound once the court sanctions. There's a carve-out for outstanding material regulatory clearances, and the Council can consent to a different approach."},
    {t:"File the scheme document with the Council for pre-clearance before it is sent out to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (f) doesn't concern pre-clearance of documents. Under section 3 of the Introduction, documents aren't normally submitted to the Council in advance. Condition (f) is about the status of the offer conditions and a binding undertaking, given just before the sanction hearing."},
    {t:"Obtain a second shareholder vote approving the scheme after the court hearing", correct:false,
     why:"No second vote is needed. The shareholders' vote at the scheme meeting stands. Condition (f) deals with the period between that vote and court sanction, making sure the deal is locked in before the court is asked to sanction it."},
    {t:"Nothing: offer conditions may stay open until after the court has sanctioned the scheme", correct:false,
     why:"That was effectively the position before 2026. Condition (f) now requires all conditions to be satisfied or waived before the sanction hearing, except those capable of being satisfied only upon or following sanction, unless the Council consents or the regulatory-clearance carve-out applies."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (f)", takeaway:"Pre-sanction: confirm conditions, undertake to be bound." },

{ id:"tc1x-086", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Shareholders have approved a scheme for Hazel Ltd. Before the sanction hearing, two conditions are open: (1) a minimum-cash condition on Hazel's balance sheet, which the offeror could waive, and (2) the scheme becoming effective once the court order is lodged with the registrar. The scheme circular listed (2) as a post-sanction condition.",
  prompt:"What must the parties confirm to the Council before the hearing?",
  options:[
    {t:"That (1) is met or waived; (2) may stay open, as it is only satisfiable on or after sanction", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (f)(i) requires confirmation 'that all of the conditions to the offer have been either satisfied or waived, other than any conditions which are capable of being satisfied only upon or following the scheme being sanctioned (which conditions should normally be specified in the scheme circular)'.\n\nCondition (1), the minimum-cash test, could be satisfied or waived before the hearing. It has to be dealt with: either it is met, or the offeror waives it. It can't be left open as a way out after shareholders have voted.\n\nCondition (2) is different by nature. The scheme can only become effective after the court sanctions it and the order is lodged, so it can't be satisfied beforehand. That's the type of condition the carve-out is for, and it was properly listed in the circular. The parties must also undertake, under (f)(ii), to be bound by the scheme once sanctioned. The aim is that, at the hearing, the only things left are steps that follow sanction itself."},
    {t:"Both conditions must be satisfied or waived before the hearing", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (f) excludes 'conditions which are capable of being satisfied only upon or following the scheme being sanctioned'. Condition (2) depends on the court order, so it can't be met before the hearing, and the Code doesn't require the impossible."},
    {t:"Neither needs to be dealt with, since both can be handled after sanction", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (1) is capable of being satisfied or waived before sanction, so it must be. Leaving it open would let the offeror walk away after shareholders have voted and the court has been asked to sanction, which is exactly what condition (f) prevents."},
    {t:"The parties need only undertake to be bound by the scheme; no confirmation of any kind is needed", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (f) has two parts: (i) confirming that conditions have been satisfied or waived, subject to the carve-out, and (ii) undertaking to be bound upon sanction. Both are required, unless the Council consents otherwise or the regulatory carve-out applies."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (f)", takeaway:"Waivable pre-sanction conditions must be closed out." },

{ id:"tc1-041", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Shareholders have approved a scheme and the sanction hearing is approaching. A material merger-control clearance is still outstanding, and it isn't yet clear what the regulator will require to grant it.",
  prompt:"Must the parties give the condition (f) confirmation and undertaking before the hearing?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: not while a material regulatory clearance is outstanding and the action needed is unclear", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (f) says the confirmation and undertaking 'will not apply if a condition relating to a material official authorisation or regulatory clearance is outstanding, provided that either: (A) it is not sufficiently clear what action would be required to be taken in order for the authorisation or clearance to be obtained; or (B) if it is sufficiently clear what action would be required ..., the taking of that action would give rise to circumstances which are of material significance to the offer in the context of the offer (see Note 2 on Rule 15.1).'\n\nThis case fits limb (A): the clearance is material, outstanding, and its requirements are unclear. Locking the parties in before that is resolved would force them to commit blind. The Code adds a safeguard against drift: 'If a question as to whether the condition in (f) has been satisfied remains outstanding on the long-stop date, the parties to the offer will normally be required to agree an extension to the long-stop date pending the final determination of the issue.' So the offeror can't rely on running out the clock to escape."},
    {t:"Yes: they must first waive the clearance condition, and then give the confirmation in the usual way", correct:false,
     why:"The carve-out exists precisely for outstanding material regulatory clearances. Forcing a waiver could push the parties into breaching the law if the regulator later imposes requirements. Where limb (A) or (B) is met, the confirmation and undertaking requirements don't apply."},
    {t:"Yes, unless the court excuses them from the requirement at the hearing", correct:false,
     why:"The carve-out is in the Code itself, in condition (f). The court doesn't need to excuse anything. Separately, the Council can always consent to a different approach under 'Except with the Council's consent'."},
    {t:"No, and the scheme then lapses automatically at the long-stop date", correct:false,
     why:"The Code points the other way. If the question is still outstanding at the long-stop date, the parties 'will normally be required to agree an extension to the long-stop date pending the final determination of the issue'. This stops an offeror using a slow clearance as a way out."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (f)", takeaway:"Unclear clearance: carve-out; extend the long-stop." },

{ id:"tc1x-087", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Before the sanction hearing for a scheme, the competition regulator tells the parties it will clear the merger only if the offeror sells its largest business line, which accounts for 60% of its revenue.",
  prompt:"Does the condition (f) carve-out apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, under limb (B): the action needed is clear but would be materially significant to the offer", correct:true,
     why:"The carve-out has two alternative limbs. Limb (A) applies when it isn't sufficiently clear what action would be required to obtain the clearance. Here the regulator has been clear, so (A) doesn't apply.\n\nLimb (B) applies 'if it is sufficiently clear what action would be required to be taken in order for the authorisation or clearance to be obtained', but 'the taking of that action would give rise to circumstances which are of material significance to the offer in the context of the offer (see Note 2 on Rule 15.1)'. Selling a business line that produces 60% of the offeror's revenue would transform the deal it agreed to. That is of material significance.\n\nSo the confirmation and undertaking requirements in (f) don't apply while the clearance is outstanding. The offeror isn't forced to commit before deciding whether to accept the remedy. If the issue is still unresolved on the long-stop date, the parties will normally have to agree to extend it pending final determination. Whether a remedy is material enough is judged 'in the context of the offer', which is the test in Note 2 on Rule 15.1 (TC6A)."},
    {t:"No: once it is clear what action is needed, the carve-out can never apply to the parties", correct:false,
     why:"That reads only limb (A). Limb (B) expressly covers cases where the required action is clear but taking it would give rise to circumstances of material significance to the offer. A forced sale of 60% of the offeror's revenue is such a case."},
    {t:"No: the parties must give the confirmation and then seek to withdraw", correct:false,
     why:"Giving the confirmation and undertaking would bind the offeror to the scheme once sanctioned. The carve-out exists so it doesn't have to do that while a material clearance is outstanding on terms of material significance."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if the regulator's requirement was unexpected", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't ask whether the requirement was expected. Limb (B) asks whether the required action would give rise to circumstances of material significance to the offer, in the context of the offer. Expected or not, a forced sale on this scale meets that test."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (f)(B); r 15.1, Note 2", takeaway:"Clear but material remedy → carve-out limb (B)." },

{ id:"tc1-042", lesson:"TC1E", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Which conditions must a trust scheme meet to be exempted, compared with an amalgamation?",
  options:[
    {t:"Trust schemes: (a)–(f), unitholder and court approval. Amalgamations: (a)–(d) and set deadlines", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (g), relettered from (e) in 2026, applies to trust schemes 'in addition to (a), (b), (c), (d), (e) and (f)'. The words '(e) and (f)' were added in 2026, so trust schemes are now subject to the 6-month meeting limit and the pre-sanction confirmation and undertaking. On top of those, (g) requires (i) approval 'by a majority in number representing three-fourths in value of unitholders or class of unitholders present and voting either in person or by proxy'; and (ii) Court approval for the trust scheme, now 'under Order 32 of the Rules of Court', replacing the reference to Order 80.\n\nCondition (h), relettered from (f), applies to amalgamations 'in addition to (a), (b), (c) and (d)', so not (e) or (f). It requires the amalgamation document to be posted within 35 days of the announcement, and the amalgamation to be effective by 5:30 pm on the 60th day after posting."},
    {t:"Both need only conditions (a) to (d), with no extra approval or timing conditions", correct:false,
     why:"That was the position before 2026. Since then, trust schemes must also satisfy new conditions (e) and (f). Only amalgamations remain on (a) to (d), plus their own deadlines in (h)."},
    {t:"Both must meet conditions (a) to (f), plus court approval of the scheme or amalgamation", correct:false,
     why:"The 2026 amendment extended (e) and (f) to trust schemes but not to amalgamations. Condition (h) still refers only to '(a), (b), (c) and (d)' for amalgamations, which instead have their own posting and effectiveness deadlines."},
    {t:"Trust schemes are exempted automatically; amalgamations must meet (a) to (h)", correct:false,
     why:"No exemption is automatic. The Council 'may, subject to conditions, exempt', and it 'will normally grant such exemption if' the listed conditions are met. A trust scheme must meet (a) to (f) and the voting and court approval requirements in (g)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (g)–(h)", takeaway:"Trust schemes: (a)–(f), 75% in value, court (O 32)." },

{ id:"tc1x-088", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"At the meeting to approve a trust scheme for Acacia Trust, 120 unitholders vote in person or by proxy, casting 100 million units in total. 70 unitholders, holding 72 million units, vote for. 50 unitholders, holding 28 million units, vote against.",
  prompt:"Is the approval requirement in condition (g) met?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: a majority in number (70 of 120), but 72% in value is short of ¾", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (g)(i) requires the trust scheme to be 'approved by a majority in number representing three-fourths in value of unitholders or class of unitholders present and voting either in person or by proxy'. That is two tests, and both must be met.\n\nNumber: 70 of the 120 unitholders voting are in favour, which is a majority. Value: those 70 hold 72 million of the 100 million units voted, which is 72%. Three-fourths is 75%, so the value test fails, and the trust scheme isn't approved.\n\nTwo details matter. The base is unitholders 'present and voting', so unitholders who don't attend, or abstain, aren't counted. And those required to abstain under conditions (a) and (b) (the offeror's concert parties, common substantial holders, and those gaining or consolidating control) aren't voting, so their units are left out of both the number and the value counts. Even with approval, the trust scheme would still need Court approval under Order 32 of the Rules of Court."},
    {t:"Yes: a majority in number voted for", correct:false,
     why:"The headcount majority is only half the test. Condition (g)(i) also requires that majority to represent three-fourths in value of those present and voting. At 72%, it doesn't."},
    {t:"Yes: 72% is more than two-thirds", correct:false,
     why:"The threshold is three-fourths (75%) in value, not two-thirds. 72% is below it, so the approval fails."},
    {t:"It can't be decided without counting the unitholders who didn't attend the meeting", correct:false,
     why:"The tests are measured against unitholders 'present and voting either in person or by proxy'. Non-attendees and abstainers don't form part of the base, so the result can be worked out from the votes cast."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (g)(i)", takeaway:"Majority in number AND 75% in value of those voting." },

{ id:"tc1-043", lesson:"TC1E", type:"fill",
  prompt:"For an amalgamation to be exempted, its document must be posted within 35 days of the announcement, and it must be effective by 5:30 pm on the ____ day after posting.",
  accept:["60","60th","sixtieth"], answer:"60th",
  why:"Condition (h): 'in the case of amalgamations, in addition to (a), (b), (c) and (d) above: (i) the amalgamation document must be posted within 35 days of the announcement ...; and (ii) the amalgamation must be effective by 5:30 pm on the 60th day after the date of posting'.\n\nAn amalgamation can be exempted from Rule 22, the offer timetable, because it doesn't involve an acceptance period. Condition (h) gives it a replacement timetable, so shareholders aren't left waiting indefinitely. Note that amalgamations aren't subject to the new conditions (e) and (f) added in 2026. Their deadlines in (h) already serve a similar function. If either deadline falls on a non-business day, Note 3 on the Definitions extends it to the next business day.",
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (h)", takeaway:"Amalgamation: post in 35 days, effective by day 60." },

{ id:"tc1x-089", lesson:"TC1E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An amalgamation of two companies is announced on 2 May. The amalgamation document is posted on 30 May.",
  prompt:"Did posting meet condition (h), and by when must the amalgamation be effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, posting was in time (the deadline was 6 June); the amalgamation must be effective by 5:30 pm on 29 July", correct:true,
     why:"Condition (h)(i) requires posting 'within 35 days of the announcement'. Counting 35 days from 2 May: 3 May is day 1, 31 May is day 29, and 6 June is day 35. Posting on 30 May is well within the window.\n\nCondition (h)(ii) requires the amalgamation to be 'effective by 5:30 pm on the 60th day after the date of posting'. Counting from 30 May: 31 May is day 1, 30 June is day 31, and 29 July is day 60. So the deadline is 5:30 pm on 29 July.\n\nNotice that the second clock runs from posting, not from the announcement. Posting early (30 May rather than 6 June) brings the effective-date deadline forward too. If either deadline fell on a non-business day, Note 3 on the Definitions would extend it to the next business day."},
    {t:"Yes; the amalgamation must be effective by 5:30 pm on 1 July, 60 days after the announcement", correct:false,
     why:"The 60-day clock runs 'after the date of posting', not after the announcement. Posting was on 30 May, so the 60th day after posting is 29 July."},
    {t:"No, posting had to be within 14 days of the announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Condition (h)(i) allows 35 days from the announcement, giving a deadline of 6 June. Posting on 30 May is in time. (14 days appears elsewhere in the Code, for example in Rule 20.1 on keeping a revised offer open.)"},
    {t:"Yes, and the amalgamation must be effective by 5:30 pm on 5 August, which is 60 days after the posting deadline", correct:false,
     why:"The 60 days run from 'the date of posting', which was 30 May, not from the last date posting was allowed. The deadline is 5:30 pm on 29 July."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Defn 12, Note (h); Notes on Defns, Note 3", takeaway:"35 days from announcement; 60 days from actual posting." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t3p1/TC1F.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T1 · Lesson TC1F The General Principles (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code GPs 1–13; Introduction §4 (organisation of the Code). Replaces TC1F in takeoverCode1.js.
   Previously untested in application: GP 2, GP 3 (special price to one holder), GP 4, GP 6 (stake-building without funds),
   GP 7 timing, GP 8 trigger, GP 9 (selective briefing; bona fide potential offeror exception), GP 11 forecasts, GP 12
   misleading statements, GP 13 personal interests, 'normally' in GP 5, §4 notes. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p1v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc1-044", lesson:"TC1F", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each General Principle to what it requires.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"GP 3", r:"Treat all shareholders of the same class equally"},
    {l:"GP 5", r:"General offer when control is acquired or consolidated"},
    {l:"GP 7", r:"No frustrating action without shareholder approval"},
    {l:"GP 9", r:"No information to some shareholders only"},
    {l:"GP 12", r:"Prevent a false market"}
  ],
  why:"There are 13 General Principles:\n1 The spirit of the Code: observe spirit and wording; GPs apply where no Rule does.\n2 Limitations on directors' action: boards must accept limits on how they pursue shareholders' interests.\n3 Equality of treatment: all shareholders of the same class treated equally.\n4 Oppression of minority: control exercised in good faith; oppression wholly unacceptable.\n5 Acquisition or consolidation of effective control: a general offer is normally required.\n6 Announcements: careful consideration; be able to implement the offer in full.\n7 Frustration of an offer by offeree board: no frustrating action without shareholder approval.\n8 Competent independent advice for the offeree board.\n9 Information to all shareholders, with a bona fide potential offeror exception.\n10 Sufficient information, advice and time; nothing relevant withheld.\n11 Standards of care in documents: prospectus standard; profit forecasts need special care.\n12 Prevention of a false market: full and prompt disclosure; no misleading statements.\n13 Duties of directors with personal interests.\n\nSection 4 of the Introduction calls them 'essentially standards of good commercial conduct', expressed in broad terms and applied by the Council 'in accordance with their spirit to achieve their underlying purpose'.",
  cite:"SG Code GPs 1–13; Intro §4", takeaway:"13 GPs: standards of conduct, applied by spirit." },

{ id:"tc1x-100", lesson:"TC1F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does section 4 of the Introduction describe the relationship between the General Principles, the Rules and the Notes?",
  options:[
    {t:"GPs set broad standards, applied in spirit; Rules expand them or set procedure; Notes show normal use", correct:true,
     why:"Section 4 says the General Principles 'are essentially standards of good commercial conduct', that they 'apply to all transactions with which the Code is concerned', and that they are 'expressed in broad terms and the Code does not define the precise extent of, or limitations on, their application'. The Council applies them 'in accordance with their spirit to achieve their underlying purpose' and 'may modify the effect of their precise wording accordingly'.\n\nThe Rules come in two kinds: 'some are effectively expansions of the General Principles and examples of their application and others are provisions governing specific aspects of take-over procedure'. They aren't technical, and they too are interpreted 'to achieve their underlying purpose'. 'Their spirit must be observed as well as their letter', and the Council 'may modify the application of a Rule' if it would otherwise operate inappropriately.\n\nThe Notes under the Rules 'provide guidance as to how the Rules will normally be applied by the Council'. The word 'normally' runs right through the Notes, which signals that they describe the Council's usual practice and that departures are possible."},
    {t:"The Rules override the General Principles wherever the two conflict, and are applied to the letter", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't set up a hierarchy of this kind. Many Rules are 'expansions of the General Principles and examples of their application', and GP 1 says the General Principles and the spirit of the Code apply even where no Rule does. The Rules are read to achieve their purpose, which is usually a General Principle."},
    {t:"The Notes are binding Rules in their own right, applied literally and never modified by the Council", correct:false,
     why:"Section 4 says the Notes were inserted 'to assist with interpretation of the Rules' and 'provide guidance as to how the Rules will normally be applied'. They describe usual practice. They aren't applied literally any more than the Rules are, since everything is read for its purpose."},
    {t:"The General Principles apply only where a Rule expressly refers to them, and otherwise have no effect", correct:false,
     why:"The General Principles 'apply to all transactions with which the Code is concerned', and under GP 1 they apply 'in areas not explicitly covered by any Rule'. They don't need a Rule to activate them."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Intro §4", takeaway:"GPs = spirit; Rules = purpose; Notes = normal practice." },

{ id:"tc1-045", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An offeror's advisers find a structure that no Rule expressly prohibits. It would give one group of offeree shareholders a better deal than other holders of the same class.",
  prompt:"What does the Code say?",
  options:[
    {t:"The GPs and spirit of the Code apply where no Rule does; the gap doesn't help", correct:true,
     why:"GP 1: 'It is impracticable to devise rules in sufficient detail to cover all circumstances which can arise in take-over or merger transactions. Accordingly, persons engaged in such transactions must observe both the spirit and the precise wording of the General Principles and Rules. Moreover, the General Principles and the spirit of the Code will apply in areas not explicitly covered by any Rule.' Section 2 of the Introduction says the same.\n\nThe structure gives one group of a class a better deal than others of the same class, which engages GP 3: 'An offeror must treat all shareholders of the same class in an offeree company equally.' Finding a technical gap in the Rules doesn't help, because GP 3 applies directly. The proper course, if there's genuine doubt, is to consult the Secretariat in advance. The Council can sanction a breach of a General Principle just as it can a breach of a Rule, and can make compensation rulings so that disadvantaged holders receive what they should have."},
    {t:"It's permitted, because no Rule of the Code expressly prohibits it", correct:false,
     why:"GP 1 rules this out. The General Principles and the spirit of the Code 'will apply in areas not explicitly covered by any Rule'. The Code is deliberately not a list of prohibitions to be navigated around. GP 3 applies to the structure directly."},
    {t:"It's permitted, provided the structure is fully disclosed to all shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure doesn't cure unequal treatment. GP 3 requires equal treatment of all shareholders of the same class. Telling everyone that one group will get a better deal doesn't make the treatment equal."},
    {t:"Only a court can stop it, on an application by shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The Council, not a court, applies the General Principles. It can sanction a breach, require corrective steps and make compensation rulings. Court proceedings aren't needed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 1, GP 3", takeaway:"No Rule doesn't mean no restriction." },

{ id:"tc1x-101", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An offeror is making a general offer at $2.00 a share for Crane Ltd. To secure the founder's support, it privately agrees to buy her ordinary shares at $2.10. All other ordinary shareholders will receive $2.00.",
  prompt:"Which General Principle is most directly breached?",
  options:[
    {t:"GP 3: all shareholders of the same class must be treated equally", correct:true,
     why:"GP 3: 'An offeror must treat all shareholders of the same class in an offeree company equally.' The founder's shares are ordinary shares, the same class as everyone else's. Paying her 10 cents more is unequal treatment, whether it's done inside the offer or by a side agreement. The private nature of the deal makes things worse, because the other shareholders don't know they are getting less.\n\nThe Rules develop GP 3 in detail. The Rule on special deals (Rule 10, covered in TC3H) addresses favourable arrangements with particular shareholders. Rule 10 is also among the Rules the Introduction names for compensation rulings, so the Council could require the offeror to pay the other shareholders the difference, with interest.\n\nGP 3 speaks of 'the same class'. Different classes can be offered different prices, but holders within a class can't."},
    {t:"GP 7: no frustrating action by the offeree board without approval", correct:false,
     why:"GP 7 restrains the offeree board from frustrating an offer. Here the offeror is paying one shareholder more, which is a matter of equality between shareholders. The offeree board's conduct isn't in issue."},
    {t:"GP 10: sufficient information and time", correct:false,
     why:"The concealment does deprive other shareholders of relevant information, so GP 10 is engaged too. But the core wrong is the unequal price for the same class of share, which is what GP 3 prohibits. Even if the side deal were disclosed, it would still breach GP 3."},
    {t:"None, because the founder's support is worth paying for", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't allow an offeror to buy a key holder's support with a better price for the same class. That is the very thing GP 3 prohibits. The offeror is free to raise its offer price, but then every holder of the class must get it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 3; Intro §2", takeaway:"Same class, same deal: no premium for the founder." },

{ id:"tc1x-102", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before any offer, the board of Ibis Ltd, an offeror, plans to make an aggressive move: telling selected market participants about its intentions in order to drive up demand for Ibis shares before announcing a share-for-share offer. The directors say they owe a duty to act in the best interests of Ibis's own shareholders.",
  prompt:"How does GP 2 respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"Boards' primary duty is to shareholders, but the Code inevitably limits their freedom", correct:true,
     why:"GP 2: 'While the boards of an offeror and an offeree company and their respective advisers and associates have a primary duty to act in the best interests of their respective shareholders, the General Principles and Rules will inevitably impinge on the freedom of action of boards and persons involved in take-over and merger transactions. They must therefore accept that there are limitations on the manner in which those interests can be pursued in a take-over or merger transaction.'\n\nThe Ibis board is right that it owes duties to its own shareholders. But GP 2 says that duty doesn't license any tactic. Telling selected market participants about a forthcoming offer engages GP 9 (no information to some shareholders that isn't available to all, once a transaction is in contemplation) and GP 12 (prevent a false market; make full and prompt disclosure). The board's goal may be legitimate, but the means are restricted. GP 2 applies to offeror and offeree boards alike, and to their advisers and associates."},
    {t:"GP 2 lets a board do whatever benefits its own shareholders, without limit", correct:false,
     why:"GP 2 says the opposite. It acknowledges the board's primary duty but states that the General Principles and Rules 'will inevitably impinge' on boards' freedom of action. Boards 'must therefore accept that there are limitations' on how they pursue their shareholders' interests."},
    {t:"GP 2 applies only to offeree boards, not to the boards of offerors", correct:false,
     why:"GP 2 names 'the boards of an offeror and an offeree company and their respective advisers and associates'. It applies on both sides. The Ibis board, as offeror, is directly covered."},
    {t:"GP 2 replaces the directors' duties under company law for the duration of an offer", correct:false,
     why:"GP 2 accepts that directors have a primary duty to their shareholders and doesn't displace it. What it adds is that, in a take-over, the Code limits the methods by which that duty may be pursued."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 2; GPs 9, 12", takeaway:"Duty to shareholders, within Code limits." },

{ id:"tc1x-103", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After a successful offer, Mr Pang holds 78% of Egret Ltd. He then causes Egret to sell its main property to his private company at well below market value, over the objections of the minority.",
  prompt:"Which General Principle speaks directly to his conduct?",
  options:[
    {t:"GP 4: control must be used in good faith; oppressing minorities is unacceptable", correct:true,
     why:"GP 4: 'Rights of control must be exercised in good faith and oppression of the minority is wholly unacceptable.' Mr Pang has used his control of Egret to transfer value from the company, and therefore from its minority shareholders, to himself. A sale of the main asset to his own company at well below market value is a clear example of control used in bad faith against the minority.\n\nGP 4 is notable because it reaches beyond the offer itself to how control is used. The Code isn't only about getting into control fairly but also about not abusing it. The minority may have their own company-law remedies (for example, oppression claims under the Companies Act), but the Code adds its own standard of conduct, which the Council can enforce through its sanctions."},
    {t:"GP 5: a general offer is required on acquiring effective control", correct:false,
     why:"GP 5 is about the obligation to make a general offer when effective control is acquired or consolidated. Mr Pang's problem isn't how he acquired control but how he is using it. That is GP 4's territory."},
    {t:"GP 9: information must be given equally to all shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"GP 9 prevents selective disclosure during a transaction. Mr Pang's wrong is substantive: taking value from the minority through a below-market sale. Equal information wouldn't cure it."},
    {t:"None: the offer is over, so the Code no longer applies to his conduct as controller at all", correct:false,
     why:"GP 4 doesn't stop at the end of the offer. Its words cover the exercise of 'rights of control' generally. A controller who uses control oppressively breaches GP 4, whenever the offer closed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 4", takeaway:"Control in good faith; no oppression." },

{ id:"tc1x-104", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Foo holds 28% of Finch Ltd. Wanting to move first, he buys another 3% in the market, taking him to 31%. He has no financing arranged and tells his adviser he will 'sort out the money once the offer is under way'.",
  prompt:"What does GP 6 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"He and his advisers must be sure he can, and will still be able to, implement it in full", correct:true,
     why:"GP 6: 'An offeror should announce an offer only after the most careful consideration. Before taking any action which may lead to an obligation to make a general offer, a person and his financial advisers should be satisfied that he can and will continue to be able to implement the offer in full.'\n\nMr Foo's purchase from 28% to 31% takes him across the 30% threshold for effective control. Under Rule 14 that triggers an obligation to make a general offer to all other shareholders. GP 6 required him to be satisfied, before buying, that he could fund that offer in full and keep it funded throughout. Hoping to arrange money later fails that test.\n\nNote that GP 6 puts the burden on his financial advisers as well, who must also be satisfied. The reason is simple: shareholders who accept an offer must be paid. An offer that can't be implemented, or a mandatory offer that never gets made, defeats GP 5 and leaves the market disrupted. The Introduction's compensation power, which covers Rule 14, could also follow."},
    {t:"Nothing, as long as he finds financing before the offer document is posted to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"GP 6 bites earlier: 'Before taking any action which may lead to an obligation to make a general offer'. The purchase that took him across 30% was that action, so he had to be satisfied about funding before making it."},
    {t:"Only his financial adviser is responsible for checking that funding is in place", correct:false,
     why:"GP 6 says 'a person and his financial advisers should be satisfied'. Both share the responsibility. Mr Foo can't shift it entirely to his adviser."},
    {t:"GP 6 applies only to voluntary offers, not to mandatory offers under Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"GP 6 expressly covers 'any action which may lead to an obligation to make a general offer', which is the mandatory-offer case. It is especially important there, because a person crossing 30% has no choice about making the offer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 6; GP 5", takeaway:"Be able to pay in full, before you trigger." },

{ id:"tc1-048", lesson:"TC1F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Before taking any action that may lead to an obligation to make a general offer, what must a person and his financial advisers be satisfied of?",
  options:[
    {t:"That he can, and will continue to, implement the offer in full", correct:true,
     why:"GP 6: 'Before taking any action which may lead to an obligation to make a general offer, a person and his financial advisers should be satisfied that he can and will continue to be able to implement the offer in full.' It begins: 'An offeror should announce an offer only after the most careful consideration.'\n\nTwo points stand out. 'Can and will continue to be able to' means funding must be secure for the whole offer, not just at the outset. If financing could fall away mid-offer, the test isn't met. And 'in full' means for all the shares the offer could cover, not just the acceptances the offeror expects. The financial adviser's involvement is important. Its reputation is on the line, and the Council can require an adviser in breach to abstain from Code-related work for a stated period. The Rules on announcements (TC2D) build on this."},
    {t:"That the offeree board will recommend the offer", correct:false,
     why:"A recommendation isn't a precondition. Offers can be hostile. GP 6 is about the offeror's ability to pay for everything the offer could require, not about whether the offeree board supports the bid."},
    {t:"That holders of at least 50% of the shares will accept", correct:false,
     why:"Acceptance levels are a matter for the offer's conditions, not for GP 6. GP 6 requires the offeror to be able to implement the offer in full, meaning it could pay even if every holder accepted."},
    {t:"That the Council has approved the offer price in advance", correct:false,
     why:"The Council doesn't approve offer prices in advance. The Code sets price floors in some situations, but GP 6 is about the offeror's ability to implement the offer, which is something the offeror and its advisers must satisfy themselves about."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 6", takeaway:"Be able to pay in full, throughout." },

{ id:"tc1-046", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Arden Ltd's board has reason to believe a bona fide offer is imminent. The chairman proposes a quick sale of Arden's most valuable subsidiary to a friendly buyer.",
  prompt:"What does GP 7 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Without shareholder approval, the board mustn't act to frustrate a bona fide offer", correct:true,
     why:"GP 7: 'If the board of an offeree company has received a bona fide offer or has reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent, it must not, without the approval of its shareholders in general meeting, take any action on the affairs of the offeree company that could effectively result in any bona fide offer being frustrated or the shareholders being denied an opportunity to decide on its merits.'\n\nAll the elements are present. The board has reason to believe a bona fide offer is imminent. Selling the most valuable subsidiary could make the company far less attractive, or impossible, to bid for, and so could frustrate the offer. And the decision would be the board's alone. GP 7 doesn't ban the sale, but it moves the decision to shareholders. If they approve in general meeting, the board may proceed. The test is 'could effectively result in' frustration, so the board's motive doesn't matter: a sale made for good reasons is still restricted. Rule 5 applies GP 7 in detail, and since 2026 requires independent advice on a frustrating action put to shareholders (TC3A)."},
    {t:"The sale is fine if the board honestly believes it is in the company's best interests", correct:false,
     why:"The board's view doesn't displace GP 7. The test is whether the action 'could effectively result in' the offer being frustrated or shareholders being denied a chance to decide. Even a genuinely beneficial sale must go to shareholders in general meeting if it could have that effect."},
    {t:"The sale is fine until a firm offer has been announced, but not after that", correct:false,
     why:"GP 7 starts earlier, when the board 'has received a bona fide offer or has reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent'. A firm announcement isn't needed, and once the board has reason to believe an offer is imminent, it is already restricted."},
    {t:"The board needs the Council's approval for the sale, not the shareholders'", correct:false,
     why:"GP 7 gives the decision to 'shareholders in general meeting'. The Council's role is to apply the principle and the Rules, and parties should consult it when in doubt, but the approval needed is the shareholders'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 7", takeaway:"Frustrating action needs shareholder approval." },

{ id:"tc1x-105", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In January, after a year-long strategic review and with no approach from any bidder and no reason to expect one, the board of Gull Ltd agrees to sell a division. In March, a bidder approaches the board about a possible offer. The board then proposes a second, larger disposal.",
  prompt:"How does GP 7 apply to the two disposals?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not to the January sale; it applies to the March proposal, which may need shareholder approval", correct:true,
     why:"GP 7 applies only when the offeree board 'has received a bona fide offer or has reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent'. In January, Gull had no approach and no reason to expect one. GP 7 wasn't engaged, so the January sale was a matter for the board under its ordinary duties. The Code doesn't freeze a company merely because someone might bid one day.\n\nIn March, the bidder's approach gives the board reason to believe a bona fide offer may be imminent. From then on, GP 7 bites. The second disposal is restricted if it 'could effectively result in any bona fide offer being frustrated or the shareholders being denied an opportunity to decide on its merits'. A larger disposal, proposed just after an approach, is likely to be tested closely against that standard. If it could have that effect, it needs approval in general meeting. In practice the board should consult the Council. Presumption (f) also starts at the March approach, so the directors and their close relatives are now presumed to act in concert."},
    {t:"GP 7 applies to both sales, because the company was always a potential take-over target", correct:false,
     why:"GP 7 is triggered by a bona fide offer, or reason to believe one is imminent, not by the general possibility of a bid. In January, neither existed, so the January sale wasn't caught."},
    {t:"GP 7 applies to neither, because no offer had been announced", correct:false,
     why:"GP 7 doesn't wait for an announcement. A board that 'has reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent' is restricted. The March approach gives the board that reason."},
    {t:"GP 7 applies to the January sale only, because it was agreed first", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the timing. GP 7 attaches when the board receives an offer or has reason to believe one is imminent, which here was March. The January sale came before that and wasn't caught. The March proposal was."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 7; Defn 1(f)", takeaway:"GP 7 starts at the approach, not before." },

{ id:"tc1x-106", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The board of Heron Ltd receives a letter from a credible party proposing a possible offer at an indicative price. No announcement has yet been made. The chairman suggests the board wait until a firm offer arrives before taking any advice.",
  prompt:"What does GP 8 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"An offeree board receiving an offer or approach should seek competent independent advice", correct:true,
     why:"GP 8: 'An offeree board which receives an offer or is approached with a view to an offer being made, should, in the interests of its shareholders, seek competent independent advice.' The trigger is the approach, not a firm offer. A letter proposing a possible offer at an indicative price is an approach 'with a view to an offer being made'.\n\nWaiting is a poor idea for practical reasons too. Many decisions arise early: whether to engage with the bidder, whether to open the books, how to respond to leaks, whether a proposed corporate action could be frustrating action under GP 7. The board needs advice for all of these. GP 10 adds that shareholders must be given sufficient information, advice and time, and the independent adviser's view is central to the advice they receive. The Rules make independent advice a detailed requirement (TC3C)."},
    {t:"Advice is needed only once a firm offer has been announced, not on a mere approach to the board", correct:false,
     why:"GP 8 is triggered when the board 'receives an offer or is approached with a view to an offer being made'. The letter is such an approach. Waiting for a firm offer would leave the board without independent advice at the stage when many key decisions are made."},
    {t:"The board's own judgment is sufficient; outside advice is optional", correct:false,
     why:"GP 8 says the board 'should, in the interests of its shareholders, seek competent independent advice'. The emphasis on 'independent' matters, because directors may have personal interests in the outcome (GP 13). Their own judgment isn't a substitute."},
    {t:"The bidder's financial adviser can advise the offeree board", correct:false,
     why:"The offeror's adviser isn't independent from the offeree's point of view. It acts for the other side and is an associate of the offeror under Defn 2(c). GP 8 requires competent independent advice."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 8; GP 10", takeaway:"Approached? Get independent advice now." },

{ id:"tc1-047", lesson:"TC1F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which disclosure falls outside GP 9?",
  options:[
    {t:"Information given in confidence to a bona fide potential offeror, or vice versa", correct:true,
     why:"GP 9: 'In the course of a take-over or merger transaction, or when such transaction is in contemplation, the offeror, the offeree company and their respective advisers must not give information to some shareholders that is not made available to all shareholders. This principle does not apply to the provision of information in confidence by the offeree company to a bona fide potential offeror or vice versa.'\n\nThe exception exists because deals can't happen without it. A potential offeror needs due diligence to decide whether, and at what price, to bid, and an offeree may need information about the offeror, especially in a share-for-share deal. The safeguards are built into the wording: the recipient must be a 'bona fide potential offeror', and the information must be given 'in confidence'. GP 9 applies from the time a transaction is 'in contemplation', which is before any announcement. The Rules add requirements about giving equal information to competing offerors (TC3G)."},
    {t:"Briefing the largest institutional shareholders privately about the offer", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly what GP 9 prohibits. Giving information to some shareholders, however large, that isn't made available to all is selective disclosure. Institutional shareholders are still shareholders, and the exception covers only bona fide potential offerors."},
    {t:"Sharing forecasts with shareholders who have first signed a non-disclosure agreement", correct:false,
     why:"An NDA doesn't create an exception. GP 9's exception is limited to information given in confidence to a bona fide potential offeror, or by the offeror to the offeree. Shareholders who sign NDAs are still being given information that others don't have."},
    {t:"Answering one shareholder's questions on a private call", correct:false,
     why:"If the answers contain information not available to all shareholders, that is selective disclosure under GP 9. Directors can talk to shareholders, but they can't give some of them information others don't have."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 9", takeaway:"Equal information; confidential bidder talks excepted." },

{ id:"tc1x-107", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer period, Ibex Ltd's CEO hosts a lunch for its five largest institutional shareholders. He shares an updated internal valuation of Ibex's property portfolio that hasn't been published, to persuade them to reject the offer.",
  prompt:"What is the position under the General Principles?",
  options:[
    {t:"A breach of GP 9, as only some shareholders got the information; GP 10 and GP 12 too", correct:true,
     why:"GP 9 says the offeror, the offeree and their advisers 'must not give information to some shareholders that is not made available to all shareholders'. The unpublished valuation went to five institutions only. The CEO's motive, defending the company, doesn't matter.\n\nGP 10 is engaged too: 'Shareholders should be given sufficient information ... to enable them to reach an informed decision on an offer. No relevant information should be withheld from them.' If the valuation is relevant enough to persuade institutions, it is relevant to everyone. GP 12 requires 'full and prompt disclosure of all relevant information' and every endeavour 'to prevent the creation of a false market', and selective disclosure of price-sensitive information risks exactly that.\n\nThe usual remedy is to put the information out to all shareholders promptly, by announcement or circular. Any valuation published in a take-over must also meet the GP 11 standard of care (and the asset valuation Rules in TC7E)."},
    {t:"It's permitted because institutions are sophisticated investors", correct:false,
     why:"GP 9 doesn't distinguish between sophisticated and other shareholders. All shareholders must have access to the same information. The only exception is for information given in confidence to a bona fide potential offeror, or vice versa."},
    {t:"It's permitted, because the briefing was meant to help defend the company from the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Purpose doesn't matter. Whether the aim is to support or to resist the offer, giving information to some shareholders and not others breaches GP 9."},
    {t:"It's a matter only for SGX's disclosure rules, not for the Code", correct:false,
     why:"Listing rules may also apply, but the Code governs this directly. GP 9 applies 'in the course of a take-over or merger transaction', and the Council can act on it whatever the exchange does."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GPs 9, 10, 12", takeaway:"No private briefings: publish to all." },

{ id:"tc1x-108", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before any announcement, Jay Ltd's board gives due diligence information in confidence to Kite Holdings, a bona fide potential offeror that already owns 9% of Jay.",
  prompt:"Does this breach GP 9?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: confidential disclosure to a bona fide potential offeror is outside GP 9, shareholder or not", correct:true,
     why:"GP 9 ends: 'This principle does not apply to the provision of information in confidence by the offeree company to a bona fide potential offeror or vice versa.' Kite receives the information in its capacity as a bona fide potential offeror, under confidentiality, for the purpose of deciding whether to bid. That is the exception.\n\nKite's existing 9% holding doesn't take it out of the exception, but it does raise points the parties need to manage. Kite must keep the information confidential and use it for the potential offer, not for dealing in Jay shares. The Code's dealing restrictions and the general law on insider dealing both apply. If Kite proceeds, the offer document must give other shareholders the information they need under GP 10 and GP 11. If a competing offeror emerges, the Rules on information to competing offerors (TC3G) may require Jay to give it equivalent information."},
    {t:"Yes: Kite is a shareholder, so the information must also go to all other shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The exception concerns the recipient's role as a bona fide potential offeror, receiving information in confidence. Kite's shareholding doesn't remove it from the exception, although it must not use the information for dealing."},
    {t:"Yes: the exception applies only after a firm offer is announced", correct:false,
     why:"GP 9 doesn't set that timing. The exception refers to a 'bona fide potential offeror', which assumes that no offer has been made yet. Pre-announcement due diligence is the typical case."},
    {t:"No, and Kite may now buy more Jay shares freely using the information", correct:false,
     why:"The exception allows the information to be given in confidence for the potential offer. It doesn't free Kite to deal on it. Dealing on confidential, price-sensitive information would raise insider-dealing issues under the law and breach the Code's dealing restrictions."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 9", takeaway:"Confidential info to a bona fide bidder is excepted, even if he holds shares." },

{ id:"tc1-051", lesson:"TC1F", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Under GP 10, shareholders should be given sufficient information, advice and ____ to reach an informed decision on an offer.",
  accept:["time"], answer:"time",
  why:"GP 10: 'Shareholders should be given sufficient information, advice and time to enable them to reach an informed decision on an offer. No relevant information should be withheld from them.' The same three things appear in section 1 of the Introduction as 'a fundamental requirement'.\n\nEach element connects to other parts of the Code. Information: the offer document and offeree board circular Rules (TC7B, TC7C), and the ban on withholding anything relevant. Advice: GP 8 says an offeree board that receives or is approached about an offer 'should ... seek competent independent advice', and that advice is passed on to shareholders. Time: the offer timetable in Rule 22 (TC7A) gives shareholders minimum periods to consider the offer and any revision. Since 2026, the scheme meeting must also be held within 6 months of announcement, which addresses the opposite problem of a process taking too long.",
  cite:"SG Code GP 10, GP 8; Intro §1", takeaway:"Information, advice, time." },

{ id:"tc1-050", lesson:"TC1F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A document addressed to shareholders containing the offeree board's opinions must meet the highest standards of care and accuracy, as with a prospectus.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"GP 11: 'Any document or advertisement addressed to shareholders containing information, opinions or recommendations from the board of an offeror or offeree company or its advisers, should, as with a prospectus, meet the highest standards of care and accuracy. Profit forecasts require special care.'\n\nThe principle is broad. It covers 'any document or advertisement', not just formal offer documents, and opinions and recommendations as well as facts. It applies to both boards and to their advisers. The comparison with a prospectus is deliberate: shareholders deciding whether to sell or keep their shares rely on these documents as heavily as investors rely on a prospectus. Profit forecasts get special mention because they are among the most influential and least verifiable statements made in a bid. The Rules on forecasts (TC7D) set out the reporting requirements."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"GP 11 sets a prospectus-level standard for any document or advertisement addressed to shareholders that contains information, opinions or recommendations from either board or their advisers. Opinions aren't exempt, and the offeree board's circular is a central example."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 11", takeaway:"Prospectus-level care; forecasts need more." },

{ id:"tc1x-109", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Facing a hostile bid, the board of Lark Ltd wants to send shareholders a profit forecast showing a sharp rise in earnings. The finance team prepared it overnight from management's best guesses, without review.",
  prompt:"What does the Code require of the forecast?",
  options:[
    {t:"GP 11: prospectus-standard care and accuracy, with special care for profit forecasts", correct:true,
     why:"GP 11 applies to 'any document or advertisement addressed to shareholders containing information, opinions or recommendations from the board of an offeror or offeree company or its advisers'. These must 'as with a prospectus, meet the highest standards of care and accuracy'. It then adds: 'Profit forecasts require special care.'\n\nA forecast thrown together overnight from best guesses, without review, falls well short. A defending board has an obvious incentive to be optimistic, and an inflated forecast could lead shareholders to reject a good offer. GP 12 is engaged as well: parties 'must take care not to make statements which may mislead shareholders or the market'.\n\nThe board can still publish a forecast, but only one prepared with real care, on stated assumptions, and reported on in the way the Rules on profit forecasts require (TC7D). Since 2026, quantified expected cash proceeds from a competing asset sale are also treated as a profit forecast."},
    {t:"Nothing special: a forecast is only the board's opinion, so ordinary care is enough for it", correct:false,
     why:"GP 11 covers opinions as well as information. And it singles out profit forecasts as requiring special care, because shareholders rely on them heavily. A forecast is held to a higher standard than ordinary opinions, not a lower one."},
    {t:"Profit forecasts are banned altogether while an offer is in progress", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't ban forecasts. It regulates them. GP 11 requires special care, and the Rules on profit forecasts (TC7D) set out how they must be prepared and reported on. A properly prepared forecast can be published."},
    {t:"The forecast is acceptable as long as it is clearly labelled 'unaudited'", correct:false,
     why:"A label doesn't satisfy GP 11. The standard is the highest care and accuracy, with special care for forecasts. An unreviewed, overnight forecast doesn't meet it, however it is labelled."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 11; GP 12", takeaway:"Forecasts: special care, not overnight guesses." },

{ id:"tc1x-110", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During an offer, the offeror's CEO tells a newspaper that 'shareholders holding over 40% have already agreed to accept'. In fact, none has made any commitment.",
  prompt:"Which General Principle is most directly breached?",
  options:[
    {t:"GP 12: parties must prevent a false market and avoid misleading statements", correct:true,
     why:"GP 12: 'All parties to a take-over or merger transaction should make full and prompt disclosure of all relevant information and use every endeavour to prevent the creation of a false market in the shares of an offeror or offeree company. Parties to such transactions must take care not to make statements which may mislead shareholders or the market.'\n\nThe CEO's statement is false and plainly capable of misleading. Shareholders who believe 40% have already committed may accept out of fear of being left as a minority, and the market price may move on the claim. Both effects create a false market. Media statements are covered just as formal documents are, and the 2026 amendments to Rule 9.1 address media interviews and social media directly (TC3F).\n\nThe offeror should correct the statement immediately by announcement. Depending on how deliberate the conduct was, the Council could reprimand or censure, and in a flagrant case take action to deprive the offender of market facilities."},
    {t:"GP 3: all shareholders of the same class must be treated equally", correct:false,
     why:"GP 3 concerns equal treatment of shareholders of the same class, for example equal price. A false statement to the market doesn't treat holders unequally in that sense. It misleads all of them, which is GP 12's concern."},
    {t:"GP 6: an offer should be announced only after the most careful consideration by the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"GP 6 is about announcing an offer only after careful consideration and being able to implement it in full. The problem here is a false claim about acceptances, which is a misleading statement under GP 12."},
    {t:"None, because press comments aren't formal offer documents", correct:false,
     why:"GP 12 applies to 'statements', not only documents. Comments to the media are statements to the market, and they must not mislead. A party can't escape the Code by saying something in an interview that it couldn't put in a circular."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 12", takeaway:"No misleading statements, including to the press." },

{ id:"tc1-049", lesson:"TC1F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The directors effectively control their company and reject an offer that minority shareholders favour. What does GP 13 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Their attitude will be decisive, but they must examine their reasons and be ready to explain them", correct:true,
     why:"GP 13: 'Directors of an offeror or an offeree company should, in advising their shareholders, have regard to the interests of shareholders as a whole, and not to their own interests or those derived from personal or family relationships. Shareholders of companies which are effectively controlled by their directors must accept that the attitude of their board on any offer will be decisive. There may be good reasons for the board rejecting an offer or preferring the lower of two offers. The board must carefully examine its reasons for doing so and be prepared to explain its decision to shareholders.'\n\nGP 13 is realistic. If the directors hold control, no offer can succeed without them, and the Code doesn't pretend otherwise. What it requires instead is accountability. The board's advice must consider shareholders as a whole, not personal or family interests, and if it rejects an offer or prefers the lower of two, it must examine its reasons carefully and be ready to explain them."},
    {t:"The directors must accept any offer made at a premium to the market price", correct:false,
     why:"GP 13 expressly accepts that 'there may be good reasons for the board rejecting an offer or preferring the lower of two offers'. A premium doesn't oblige the directors to accept. What GP 13 requires is a careful examination of their reasons and readiness to explain them."},
    {t:"The minority can force a sale through an application to the Council", correct:false,
     why:"There's no such mechanism. GP 13 says shareholders of companies controlled by their directors 'must accept that the attitude of their board on any offer will be decisive'. The Council won't override a controlling holder's decision to sell or not."},
    {t:"Controlling directors may consider only their own interests as shareholders, and need explain nothing", correct:false,
     why:"GP 13 says directors advising shareholders must have regard to 'the interests of shareholders as a whole, and not to their own interests or those derived from personal or family relationships'. Control makes their view decisive; it doesn't license self-interested advice."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 13", takeaway:"Controlling board decides, and must explain." },

{ id:"tc1x-111", lesson:"TC1F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Neo, a director of Myna Ltd, has privately agreed a lucrative five-year consultancy with an offeror, starting if its offer succeeds. A rival bidder is offering more. At the board meeting, Mr Neo argues strongly that the board should recommend the lower offer.",
  prompt:"What does GP 13 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Directors must advise for shareholders as a whole, not their own interests, Mr Neo's included", correct:true,
     why:"GP 13 begins: 'Directors of an offeror or an offeree company should, in advising their shareholders, have regard to the interests of shareholders as a whole, and not to their own interests or those derived from personal or family relationships.' Mr Neo's consultancy gives him a personal interest in the lower offer succeeding. That is exactly the kind of interest GP 13 says must not drive advice to shareholders.\n\nGP 13 also accepts that 'there may be good reasons for the board ... preferring the lower of two offers', but the board 'must carefully examine its reasons ... and be prepared to explain its decision'. Where one director's support depends on a private benefit, the board can't credibly say its preference rests on shareholders' interests. In practice, Mr Neo's conflict must be disclosed, and he should stand aside from the recommendation. The independent adviser required by GP 8 matters even more here. Private benefits offered by an offeror to particular people can also raise issues under the special-deals Rule (TC3H) and GP 3."},
    {t:"He may advocate freely for his preferred offer, since every director is entitled to a view", correct:false,
     why:"Directors can hold views, but GP 13 requires them, when advising shareholders, to have regard to shareholders as a whole, not their own interests. A director with an undisclosed private benefit tied to one offer isn't giving that kind of advice."},
    {t:"The board must always recommend whichever offer is higher, whatever the terms", correct:false,
     why:"GP 13 recognises that there may be good reasons to prefer the lower of two offers, for instance if the higher one is less certain. The problem here isn't preferring the lower offer as such but the conflicted reason behind the preference."},
    {t:"GP 13 applies only to directors who personally hold shares in the offeree", correct:false,
     why:"GP 13 covers personal interests of any kind, 'their own interests or those derived from personal or family relationships'. A consultancy is a personal interest whether or not Mr Neo holds any shares."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 13; GP 8", takeaway:"Personal benefit mustn't steer the board's advice." },

{ id:"tc1x-112", lesson:"TC1F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because GP 5 says a general offer is 'normally' required when effective control is acquired or consolidated, there can be situations where no general offer is required.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"GP 5: 'Where effective control of a company is acquired or consolidated by a person, or persons acting in concert, a general offer to all other shareholders is normally required.' The word 'normally' signals that the principle allows exceptions.\n\nRule 14 shows how this works. It opens 'Except with the Council's consent', and its Notes set out dispensations, the best known being the whitewash waiver, where independent shareholders vote to waive their right to a general offer (TC5C, TC8D). You've also seen an example in this chapter: under condition (b) of the Note on Defn 12, a person who acquires or consolidates control through a scheme doesn't have to make a general offer, provided he and his concert parties abstain and the scheme document tells shareholders that voting for it means agreeing to that. In each case, the protection of GP 5 is replaced by an informed decision of the independent shareholders."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"GP 5 says a general offer is 'normally' required, not always. Rule 14 starts 'Except with the Council's consent' and provides dispensations such as the whitewash waiver. Control acquired through a scheme can also be exempt, subject to the abstention and disclosure conditions in the Note on Defn 12."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code GP 5; r 14.1; Defn 12, Note (b)", takeaway:"'Normally': dispensations and whitewashes exist." },

{ id:"tc1-052", lesson:"TC1F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The Council may modify how a Rule applies if, in the particular circumstances, it would otherwise operate inappropriately.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Section 4 of the Introduction says the Rules, like the General Principles, 'are to be interpreted in such a way as to achieve their underlying purpose. Therefore, their spirit must be observed as well as their letter and the Council may modify the application of a Rule if it considers that, in the particular circumstances of the case, it would otherwise operate in an inappropriate manner.' For the General Principles, the Council 'may modify the effect of their precise wording accordingly'.\n\nThis flexibility works in both directions. It lets the Council relax a Rule that would produce an unfair or pointless result in an unusual case. But it also means parties can't rely on a literal reading that defeats a Rule's purpose. That's why consulting the Secretariat in advance is the Code's recommended practice when a case looks unusual. You'll see the same idea throughout the Rules in phrases like 'except with the Council's consent' and 'in cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted'."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Section 4 gives the Council this power expressly. Rules are read for their purpose, and the Council may modify how a Rule applies where it would otherwise operate inappropriately. The Code is designed to be applied flexibly, not mechanically."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code Intro §4", takeaway:"Purpose over letter; Council may modify." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t3p2/TC2A.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T2 · Lesson TC2A Approach & Secrecy (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code (amended 2026) Rules 1–2, Note on Rule 2, r 3.4. Replaces TC2A in takeoverCode2.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc2-001", lesson:"TC2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"To whom should an offer be put forward in the first instance?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board of the offeree company or its advisers", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1.1: 'The offer should be put forward in the first instance to the board of the offeree company or to its advisers.' The board is the right first point of contact because it has to evaluate the approach, take independent advice (GP 8), decide whether to engage, and manage confidentiality.\n\nThe approach also changes who is responsible for announcements. Before an approach, the potential offeror normally carries that responsibility (Rule 3.1). After an approach, 'the primary responsibility for making an announcement will normally rest with the board of the offeree company' (Rule 3.2). From that moment the board must watch the share price and volume for signs of undue movement, and GP 7 restrains it from frustrating action if it has reason to believe a bona fide offer is imminent."},
    {t:"The offeree company's shareholders directly", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders learn about an offer through announcements and, later, the offer document. Approaching them privately first would bypass the board and would risk breaching GP 9, since only some shareholders would know. Rule 1.1 sends the offer to the board or its advisers first."},
    {t:"The Securities Industry Council, for clearance in advance", correct:false,
     why:"The Council doesn't clear approaches or offers in advance. Parties may consult it in confidence on how the Code applies, but that is different from putting the offer forward. Rule 1.1 directs the offer to the offeree board or its advisers."},
    {t:"The Securities Exchange", correct:false,
     why:"The exchange receives announcements once they are due under Rule 3 and can grant trading halts, but it isn't the recipient of an approach. Sending an approach to the exchange would effectively make it public before the board had considered it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 1.1; r 3.2", takeaway:"Board or its advisers first." },

{ id:"tc2-002", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ridgeway Partners, a corporate finance house, approaches the chairman of Calder Ltd about a possible offer on behalf of a client. The client, a family office, wants its name kept from Calder's board until a firm offer is ready.",
  prompt:"What does the Code require?",
  options:[
    {t:"The ultimate offeror's identity must be disclosed to Calder's board at the outset", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1.2: 'If the offer or an approach with a view to putting forward an offer is not made by the ultimate offeror or potential offeror, the identity of the ultimate or potential offeror must be disclosed at the outset to the board of the offeree company.' Ridgeway is an intermediary, so the family office's identity must go to Calder's board straight away.\n\nThe board needs to know who is behind the approach to do its job. It can't judge whether the approach is credible, whether the bidder can pay (Rule 1.3), whether there are conflicts, or how to respond, without knowing who the bidder is. This is about disclosure to the board, not the public. A holding announcement can say talks are taking place without naming the potential offeror (Note 5 on Rules 3.1–3.3). At the firm-intention stage, Rule 3.5(b) requires the announcement to identify the offeror and its ultimate offeror or ultimate controlling shareholder."},
    {t:"The client may stay anonymous to the board until a firm intention is announced", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.2 requires disclosure 'at the outset', meaning when the approach is made, not when a firm offer is ready. The client can stay anonymous to the public for a while, but not to the board it is approaching."},
    {t:"The client's identity need be given only to the Council, not to the board", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.2 requires disclosure 'to the board of the offeree company'. The Council isn't the recipient. The board needs to know so it can assess the approach and the bidder's ability to implement it."},
    {t:"Disclosure is needed only if Calder's board asks who is behind the approach", correct:false,
     why:"The obligation is unconditional: the identity 'must be disclosed at the outset'. The board doesn't have to ask, and the intermediary can't wait to be asked."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 1.2; r 3.5(b)", takeaway:"Board learns who's really behind it, at once." },

{ id:"tc2x-001", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Bidco Pte Ltd, a newly formed company, approaches the board of Delta Ltd about a possible offer. Bidco is wholly owned by Fund IV, a private equity fund managed by Summit Partners. Bidco's letter is signed by Bidco's directors and doesn't mention the fund.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 1.2 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"The ultimate offeror behind Bidco must be named to Delta's board at the outset", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1.2 applies whenever 'the offer or an approach with a view to putting forward an offer is not made by the ultimate offeror or potential offeror'. Bidco is a newly formed vehicle with no business of its own. The real potential offeror is the fund behind it. Delta's board must be told who that is at the outset.\n\nThe point of the rule is substance. A board can't assess an approach from a shell company: it needs to know who will fund the offer (Rule 1.3), whether that party has a record of completing deals, and whether there are conflicts with Delta's business or shareholders. When a firm intention is announced, Rule 3.5(b) goes further and requires the public announcement to state 'the identities of the offeror and the ultimate offeror or ultimate controlling shareholder of the offeror, where applicable'."},
    {t:"Nothing more: Bidco is the offeror and has already identified itself", correct:false,
     why:"Bidco is the nominal offeror, but Rule 1.2 asks about the 'ultimate offeror or potential offeror'. A vehicle company owned by a fund isn't the ultimate offeror. Identifying only the vehicle doesn't tell the board who is really bidding."},
    {t:"Only Bidco's directors need be named to Delta's board", correct:false,
     why:"The directors of a bid vehicle aren't the ultimate offeror. Rule 1.2 requires the identity of the ultimate or potential offeror, which here means the party that owns and directs Bidco."},
    {t:"The fund need be disclosed only in the eventual offer document, and not at any point before", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.2 requires disclosure 'at the outset' to the board. By the time of the offer document, the board would long since have had to decide how to engage. The public must also be told at the firm-intention stage under Rule 3.5(b)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 1.2; r 3.5(b)", takeaway:"Bid vehicle? Name who stands behind it." },

{ id:"tc2-003", lesson:"TC2A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A board that is approached is entitled to be satisfied that the offeror will be in a position to implement the offer in full.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1.3: 'A board which is approached is entitled to be satisfied that the offeror will be in a position to implement the offer in full.' This mirrors GP 6, which requires a person and his financial advisers to be satisfied, before taking any action that may lead to an obligation to make a general offer, that he 'can and will continue to be able to implement the offer in full'.\n\nGP 6 puts the duty on the offeror; Rule 1.3 gives the board a matching right to check it. In practice, a board may ask for evidence of committed financing before opening its books or recommending a deal. Disrupting a company with an offer that then fails for lack of funds harms shareholders. For a cash offer, the firm announcement must later contain an 'unconditional confirmation by the financial adviser or by another appropriate third party that the offeror has sufficient resources available to satisfy full acceptance of the offer' (Rule 3.5)."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.3 gives the approached board exactly this entitlement. The board doesn't have to take the offeror's word for it. It can ask for evidence of the offeror's ability to implement the offer in full, consistent with GP 6."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 1.3; GP 6", takeaway:"Board may test the offeror's ability to pay." },

{ id:"tc2x-002", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A potential offeror asks Eagle Ltd's board for access to confidential due diligence. The board asks the offeror to show how it would fund a cash offer for all of Eagle's shares. The offeror says that's none of the board's business until a firm offer is announced.",
  prompt:"Who is right?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board: Rule 1.3 lets it be satisfied the offer can be implemented in full", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 1.3 gives the board a right to be satisfied about the offeror's ability to implement the offer 'in full'. That right arises when the board 'is approached', not when a firm offer is announced. It is reasonable for the board to want this before handing over confidential information.\n\nThe board's interest is real. Opening the books exposes Eagle to commercial risk, and engaging with a bidder can unsettle staff, customers and the share price. If the bidder can't fund the deal, all of that is for nothing. GP 6 already requires the offeror and its financial advisers to be satisfied about funding before taking steps that may lead to an offer obligation. Rule 1.3 lets the board test that. At the firm-intention stage, a cash offer needs an unconditional third-party confirmation of resources for full acceptance, and the Council may require evidence supporting it (Rule 3.5 and Note 4)."},
    {t:"The offeror: funding only becomes relevant once a firm offer is announced", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.3 applies from the approach. The board is 'entitled to be satisfied' then, which is why it can ask before engaging. The firm-announcement confirmation of resources is an additional, later requirement."},
    {t:"Neither: only the Council can ask a potential offeror about funding", correct:false,
     why:"The Council can require evidence of resources (Note 4 on Rule 3.5), but Rule 1.3 gives the approached board its own right to be satisfied. The board doesn't need the Council to ask on its behalf."},
    {t:"The board, but only where the offer will be a securities exchange offer", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 1.3 applies to any offer. Funding is especially important for cash offers, which is why Rule 3.5 requires a cash confirmation, but the board's right to be satisfied about implementation isn't limited to any type of offer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 1.3; GP 6; r 3.5, Note 4", takeaway:"Board can ask for funding proof from the approach." },

{ id:"tc2-004", lesson:"TC2A", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Rule 2: There must be ____ secrecy before an announcement.",
  accept:["absolute"], answer:"absolute",
  why:"Rule 2 opens: 'There must be absolute secrecy before an announcement.' It goes on: 'All persons privy to confidential information, particularly relating to an offer or contemplated offer, must treat that information as secret and may pass it to another person only if it is necessary to do so and if that person is made aware of the need for secrecy. No person who is privy to such information should make any recommendation to any other person as to dealing in the relevant securities. All such persons must conduct themselves so as to minimise the risk of an accidental leak of information.'\n\nSecrecy matters because a leak creates a false market. Some investors trade on rumours while others don't know anything, which offends GP 9 and GP 12. A leak also triggers announcement duties under Rule 3, often before the parties are ready, and Rule 3.1(a) and Rule 3.3 can put that duty on whoever's 'inadequate security' caused it.",
  cite:"SG Code r 2", takeaway:"Absolute secrecy before announcement." },

{ id:"tc2-005", lesson:"TC2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When may a person privy to confidential information about a contemplated offer pass it on?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only if necessary, and the recipient is told of the need for secrecy", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 2 says a person privy to confidential information 'may pass it to another person only if it is necessary to do so and if that person is made aware of the need for secrecy'. Both conditions must be met. Necessity limits the circle to those who really need to know, such as advisers, financiers and the few staff working on the deal. Telling the recipient about the need for secrecy makes sure they know they are now bound by the same duty.\n\nThe recipient then becomes a person privy to the information, so all of Rule 2 applies to them too: they must keep it secret, mustn't recommend dealing, and must conduct themselves so as to minimise the risk of an accidental leak. A widening circle also has consequences under Rule 3.2(c). When talks are about to extend beyond a very restricted number of people, the offeree board must announce."},
    {t:"Whenever the recipient has signed a confidentiality agreement", correct:false,
     why:"A confidentiality agreement helps make the recipient aware of the need for secrecy, but it doesn't satisfy the necessity condition. If there's no need for that person to know, Rule 2 doesn't allow the disclosure, NDA or not."},
    {t:"Freely within the offeror's corporate group and its advisers", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 has no group exception. Telling colleagues in other group companies is allowed only if it is necessary and they are made aware of the need for secrecy. A wide internal circle is a common source of leaks."},
    {t:"Never, until the offer has been publicly announced", correct:false,
     why:"That would make deals impossible. Rule 2 allows information to be passed where necessary, provided the recipient is told of the need for secrecy. Advisers, financiers and key staff can be brought in on that basis."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2; r 3.2(c)", takeaway:"Need to know, and told it's secret." },

{ id:"tc2x-003", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An offeror's CFO is preparing a bid for Falcon Ltd. To build morale, she wants to brief all 40 staff in the finance department on 'Project Falcon' at a team meeting. Only two of them will work on the deal.",
  prompt:"Does Rule 2 allow this?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: it may be passed only where necessary, and 38 staff not on the deal don't need it", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 2 permits disclosure 'only if it is necessary to do so and if that person is made aware of the need for secrecy'. Morale isn't a necessity. The two staff who will work on the deal can be told, with a clear warning about secrecy. The other 38 have no need to know.\n\nThe rule also requires everyone privy to the information to 'conduct themselves so as to minimise the risk of an accidental leak'. Briefing 40 people does the opposite. If a leak followed and Falcon's shares moved, Rule 3.1(a) would require the offeror to announce if there were reasonable grounds for concluding that its 'inadequate security' had directly contributed. The offeror would have lost control of its timetable. Under the Note on Rule 2, the offeror's advisers should already have warned it about the importance of secrecy and security."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as she reminds them all to keep it secret", correct:false,
     why:"A reminder satisfies only one of Rule 2's two conditions. The other is necessity, and there's no need for 38 people who aren't working on the deal to know. Both conditions must be met."},
    {t:"Yes, because they are all employees of the offeror and are bound by duties of confidentiality", correct:false,
     why:"Being an employee doesn't make disclosure necessary. Rule 2 has no exception for staff. Necessity is assessed person by person."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as the target is referred to by a code name only", correct:false,
     why:"A code name helps reduce the risk of an accidental leak, but here she plans to explain the project, so the code name doesn't really hide anything. And it doesn't cure the lack of necessity for briefing people who don't need to know."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2; r 3.1(a)", takeaway:"Morale isn't necessity." },

{ id:"tc2-006", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An associate director at the offeror's financial adviser is working on a bid for Calder Ltd. Over dinner, without naming the target or the deal, she tells her brother he 'shouldn't sell his Calder shares this month'.",
  prompt:"Has she breached Rule 2?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: those privy must not recommend dealing in the relevant securities to anyone", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 2: 'No person who is privy to such information should make any recommendation to any other person as to dealing in the relevant securities.' A recommendation not to sell is a recommendation as to dealing. It doesn't matter that she gave no details of the offer. The tip itself carries the value of the information.\n\nThe breach is complete when she makes the recommendation, whether or not her brother acts on it. If he does hold on and benefits, he gains an advantage other Calder shareholders don't have, which is what GP 9 and Rule 2 aim to prevent. Separately, a recommendation based on inside information can be an offence under the insider-trading provisions of the Securities and Futures Act. If the Council found evidence of that, it would refer the matter to the appropriate authority. The adviser firm could also be sanctioned, including an order to abstain from Code work for a period."},
    {t:"No: she didn't disclose the offer itself to her brother", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 has a separate prohibition on recommendations. A tip about whether to deal is caught even if the offer itself isn't mentioned, because the tip is a way of using the confidential information."},
    {t:"Only if her brother then actually deals in the shares", correct:false,
     why:"The prohibition is on making the recommendation. Whether her brother acts on it doesn't affect whether Rule 2 was breached, though it may affect the seriousness of the consequences."},
    {t:"No: close relatives fall outside Rule 2, which covers only unconnected third parties", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 prohibits recommendations 'to any other person'. There's no exception for family. A tip to a sibling is a classic example of the conduct the rule targets."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2", takeaway:"No dealing tips, however vague." },

{ id:"tc2x-004", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two lawyers acting on a confidential bid for Harrier Ltd discuss pricing over lunch in a busy restaurant near the exchange, using the target's real name. One of them later leaves a draft announcement on a shared printer.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 2 say about this conduct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Those privy must act to minimise the risk of an accidental leak; this falls short", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 2 ends: 'All such persons must conduct themselves so as to minimise the risk of an accidental leak of information.' The duty isn't only about deliberate disclosure. It requires positive care: talking about the deal where others can overhear, using the target's real name in public, and leaving drafts where others can see them all increase the risk of a leak.\n\nThe consequences can be serious. If a leak leads to rumour or undue movement in Harrier's shares before any approach, Rule 3.1(a) requires the potential offeror to announce where there are reasonable grounds for concluding that its actions, including 'inadequate security', directly contributed. The offeror loses control of its timing. The Note on Rule 2 says advisers should warn clients of the importance of secrecy and security. Advisers are expected to model that care themselves."},
    {t:"No problem, as long as they didn't intend anyone to overhear them", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 targets accidental leaks as well as deliberate ones. The duty to 'minimise the risk of an accidental leak' is about care, not intention. Discussing the deal in a busy restaurant by name fails that standard."},
    {t:"Rule 2 applies only to the offeror's own directors, not to its lawyers or other advisers", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 applies to 'All persons privy to confidential information', which includes advisers of every kind: lawyers, bankers, accountants, printers. Lawyers on a deal are among those most likely to hold the information."},
    {t:"It matters only if the offeree's share price then moves", correct:false,
     why:"The duty to minimise the risk of a leak applies whatever happens next. A price move may trigger announcement duties under Rule 3, but the careless conduct is already a failure to comply with Rule 2."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2, and Note; r 3.1(a)", takeaway:"Minimise leak risk: no lunch-table deal talk." },

{ id:"tc2-007", lesson:"TC2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the Note on Rule 2, what should advisers tell their clients?",
  options:[
    {t:"Warn them about secrecy and security, citing Rule 2 and the dealing restrictions", correct:true,
     why:"The Note on Rule 2, headed 'Warning Clients', says: 'Advisers should warn clients of the importance of secrecy and security. Attention should be drawn to the Code, in particular this Rule and restrictions on dealings.'\n\nAdvisers are often more familiar with the Code than their clients, especially a first-time bidder or a family-controlled target. A client may not realise that telling a few friends, or buying a few shares, could trigger an early announcement or breach the dealing rules. Early warning protects the client, the offer timetable and the market. It also fits section 3 of the Introduction, which places primary responsibility for compliance on the parties and their advisers. An adviser who fails to warn a client, and whose client then causes a leak, may have to answer for that to the Council."},
    {t:"Advise them to buy offeree shares before the announcement is made", correct:false,
     why:"That is the opposite of what the Note requires. Rule 2 forbids anyone privy to the information from recommending dealing in the relevant securities, and the Code restricts dealings (TC4A). Advising a client to buy on confidential information could also breach insider-trading law."},
    {t:"Notify the Council of every client mandate as soon as it is signed", correct:false,
     why:"The Note doesn't require notification of mandates. The Council doesn't pre-vet deals. The Note is about advisers warning their own clients."},
    {t:"Nothing: secrecy is entirely the client's own responsibility, not something for the adviser", correct:false,
     why:"The Note puts a positive duty on advisers to warn. Clients are responsible for their conduct too, but advisers can't stay silent and leave clients to discover the rules for themselves."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2, Note; Intro §3", takeaway:"Advisers warn clients about secrecy." },

{ id:"tc2x-005", lesson:"TC2A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Rule 2's ban on recommending dealings applies to a financial printer's typesetter who reads a draft offer announcement while preparing it.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 2 applies to 'All persons privy to confidential information, particularly relating to an offer or contemplated offer'. And 'No person who is privy to such information should make any recommendation to any other person as to dealing in the relevant securities.' Nothing limits it to the offeror, the offeree or their principal advisers.\n\nA typesetter reading a draft announcement is privy to exactly the kind of information Rule 2 protects. The typesetter must keep it secret, must not recommend dealing to anyone, and must take care to minimise the risk of a leak. Service providers like printers, translators and IT staff often see deal documents, which is why advisers should make sure everyone in the chain is made aware of the need for secrecy. Separately, the typesetter would be subject to the insider-trading provisions of the general law."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 2 covers 'All persons privy to confidential information' and says 'No person who is privy' may recommend dealing. A printer's staff who see a draft announcement are privy to it. The rule isn't limited to the main parties or their advisers."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 2", takeaway:"Anyone privy is bound, including service providers." },

{ id:"tc2-008", lesson:"TC2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After an approach, Calder's shares jump on a rumour. Its board wants to ask the Exchange for a trading halt. The potential offeror, worried that a halt will draw attention, asks the board to wait until the weekend.",
  prompt:"What is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The offeror mustn't try to stop it; the board may announce or seek a halt when it sees fit", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.4: 'A potential offeror should not attempt to prevent the board of an offeree company from making an announcement or requesting the Securities Exchange to grant a temporary halt in dealings at any time the offeree board considers appropriate.' The decision belongs to the offeree board.\n\nThe board also has a duty here. After an approach, primary responsibility for announcements normally rests with the offeree board (Rule 3.2), and Rule 3.2(b) requires it to announce when, following an approach, the company 'is the subject of rumour or speculation about a possible offer, or there is undue movement in its share price'. Waiting until the weekend would let a false market run for days. Note 5 on Rules 3.1–3.3 recognises that 'a possible alternative to an immediate announcement may be to obtain a suspension of trading of the offeree company's shares to be followed shortly by an announcement'. That is what the board proposes."},
    {t:"The board needs the potential offeror's consent before it can request a halt in trading", correct:false,
     why:"No consent is needed. Rule 3.4 says the board may request a halt 'at any time the offeree board considers appropriate', and that the potential offeror shouldn't try to stop it. The offeror's interest in timing doesn't override the board's judgment or its own announcement duty."},
    {t:"Only the Council can request a trading halt in the offeree's shares", correct:false,
     why:"The offeree board can ask the exchange directly. Rule 3.4 refers to the board 'requesting the Securities Exchange to grant a temporary halt'. The Council should be consulted in cases of doubt, but the request is the board's."},
    {t:"The board must wait until a firm offer has been announced before acting", correct:false,
     why:"A firm offer isn't needed. After an approach, rumour or undue price movement triggers the board's duty to announce under Rule 3.2(b), 'whether or not there is a firm intention to make an offer'. A halt followed shortly by an announcement is a recognised way of dealing with it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.4; r 3.2(b); r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"Offeror mustn't block an offeree halt or announcement." }

]);

;
