/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T2 · Lesson TC2B When to Announce (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code r 3.1–3.3; Notes 1–4, 7 on Rules 3.1–3.3; Note on Rule 3 (timing). Replaces TC2B in takeoverCode2.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc2-009", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Fenwick Ltd has quietly bought 8% of Glenmore Ltd in the market but hasn't approached Glenmore's board. Glenmore's shares rise 25% on heavy volume and a newspaper reports a possible bid.",
  prompt:"Who must make an announcement?",
  options:[
    {t:"Fenwick: before an approach the offeror normally bears it, and its buying contributed", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.1 says: 'Before the board of the offeree company is approached, the responsibility for making an announcement will normally rest with the offeror or potential offeror.' It adds that the potential offeror 'should keep a close watch on the offeree company's share price and volume for signs of undue movement'.\n\nRule 3.1(a) requires an announcement when, before an approach, the offeree is the subject of rumour or speculation, or there is undue price movement or a significant increase in turnover, 'and there are reasonable grounds for concluding that it is the potential offeror's actions (whether through inadequate security, purchase of the offeree company's shares or otherwise) which have directly contributed to the situation'. All the elements are here: a newspaper report, a 25% rise on heavy volume, and Fenwick's own purchases of 8%. Glenmore's board hasn't been approached and may know nothing. Rule 3.1 ends: 'In all cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'"},
    {t:"Glenmore's board, as the board of the company whose shares moved", correct:false,
     why:"Primary responsibility moves to the offeree board only 'following an approach' (Rule 3.2). Fenwick hasn't approached Glenmore, and the board may not even know who is buying. The duty sits with the potential offeror whose purchases caused the movement."},
    {t:"No one, until Fenwick's holding reaches 30% of the voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.1(a) has no shareholding threshold. Rumour or undue movement, plus reasonable grounds for concluding the potential offeror's actions directly contributed, is enough. Rule 3.1(b), the immediate announcement on triggering a Rule 14 offer, is a separate trigger."},
    {t:"No one: market purchases stay confidential until Fenwick itself chooses to disclose them", correct:false,
     why:"Market purchases are expressly listed in Rule 3.1(a) as one of the ways a potential offeror can contribute to rumour or undue movement. Keeping the purchases quiet doesn't help once the market is reacting to them."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.1(a)", takeaway:"Pre-approach leak caused by bidder: bidder announces." },

{ id:"tc2x-020", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before any approach, Gannet Ltd's shares rise 22% after an industry newsletter speculates that 'a consolidator' may bid for several companies in the sector. A potential offeror for Gannet has bought no shares, has a tightly controlled team, and nothing suggests the speculation came from it.",
  prompt:"Must the potential offeror announce under Rule 3.1(a)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not necessarily: only where its actions are reasonably concluded to have contributed; if in doubt, consult", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.1(a) has two parts. First, the offeree is the subject of rumour or speculation, or there is undue price movement or a significant increase in turnover. That part is satisfied. Second, 'there are reasonable grounds for concluding that it is the potential offeror's actions (whether through inadequate security, purchase of the offeree company's shares or otherwise) which have directly contributed to the situation'.\n\nOn these facts, the speculation appears to be general sector talk. The potential offeror hasn't bought shares, its team is tight, and nothing links the rumour to it. If there are no reasonable grounds for concluding it directly contributed, Rule 3.1(a) doesn't require it to announce. But the situation is delicate: the potential offeror must keep watching the price and volume, and the line between general speculation and a leak can be hard to draw. Rule 3.1 ends: 'In all cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.' That's the prudent step here."},
    {t:"Yes: any rumour or undue movement before an approach requires the potential offeror to announce", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.1(a) also requires reasonable grounds for concluding that the potential offeror's actions directly contributed. Without that link, the trigger isn't met, although the potential offeror should consult the Council if in doubt."},
    {t:"No, and it can ignore the share price movement entirely until it makes an approach", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.1 says the potential offeror 'should keep a close watch on the offeree company's share price and volume for signs of undue movement'. It must keep monitoring, because the position can change quickly if its own conduct begins to contribute."},
    {t:"Gannet's board must announce instead, since it is the company whose shares moved", correct:false,
     why:"Gannet's board hasn't been approached, so Rule 3.2 isn't engaged. Before an approach, responsibility normally rests with the potential offeror, and only where the conditions in Rule 3.1(a) are met."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.1(a)", takeaway:"3.1(a) needs a causal link to the bidder; if in doubt, consult." },

{ id:"tc2-010", lesson:"TC2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Glenmore's board receives notification of a firm intention to make an offer from a serious source. It thinks the price is too low. What must it do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Tell shareholders without delay, by paid press notice or by announcement", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.2(a) requires the offeree board to announce 'when the offeree board receives notification of a firm intention to make an offer from a serious source. Irrespective of whether the offeree board views the offer favourably or otherwise, it must inform its shareholders without delay. The board of the offeree company must issue a paid press notice or, where the offeror has published a paid press notice, an announcement.'\n\nThe board's opinion of the price doesn't matter. Shareholders are entitled to know that a serious party intends to bid, and to make up their own minds (GP 10). A board that sat on a firm offer it disliked would be denying shareholders the chance to decide on its merits, which offends GP 7. Note 7 defines a 'paid press notice' as a paid advertisement in the most widely circulated English-language national newspaper published daily, which includes papers published every day except Sunday. The board can say in its announcement that it considers the offer too low, and it will later give its views in its circular."},
    {t:"Stay silent, since it intends to reject the offer anyway", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(a) applies 'irrespective of whether the offeree board views the offer favourably or otherwise'. The board can't decide on shareholders' behalf that they don't need to hear about it."},
    {t:"Wait until its independent adviser has reported", correct:false,
     why:"The duty is to inform shareholders 'without delay'. Independent advice comes later, in the board's circular. The initial announcement can simply state the offer and that the board will advise in due course."},
    {t:"Notify the Exchange privately and then wait for the offer document to arrive", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(a) requires shareholders to be informed publicly, by paid press notice or announcement. A private notification to the exchange doesn't inform shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.2(a); r 3, Note 7", takeaway:"Firm offer from a serious source: tell shareholders now." },

{ id:"tc2x-021", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three weeks after an approach to Kingfisher Ltd's board, a financial blog reports that Kingfisher is 'in play'. Kingfisher's price is barely moving. The potential offeror hasn't decided whether to bid.",
  prompt:"Must Kingfisher's board announce?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: after an approach, rumour or speculation alone triggers Rule 3.2(b)", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.2(b) requires the offeree board to announce 'when, following an approach to the offeree company, the offeree company is the subject of rumour or speculation about a possible offer, or there is undue movement in its share price or a significant increase in the volume of share turnover, whether or not there is a firm intention to make an offer'.\n\nThe triggers are alternatives. Rumour or speculation is enough on its own, even if the price hasn't moved. The blog report is speculation about a possible offer, and there has been an approach, so the board must announce. 'Whether or not there is a firm intention' means the potential offeror's indecision doesn't matter. The usual form is a holding announcement under Note 5, saying talks are taking place (without naming the potential offeror) or that a potential offeror is considering an offer. After an approach the board has the primary responsibility, which is why Rule 3.2 tells it to watch the share price and volume closely."},
    {t:"No: the price hasn't moved, so there's nothing to announce", correct:false,
     why:"Price movement is only one of the triggers. Rule 3.2(b) lists 'rumour or speculation about a possible offer' separately. Speculation following an approach is enough even with a stable price."},
    {t:"No: only the potential offeror can announce before a firm intention is notified", correct:false,
     why:"After an approach, primary responsibility for announcements normally rests with the offeree board (Rule 3.2). Rule 3.2(b) expressly applies 'whether or not there is a firm intention to make an offer'."},
    {t:"Only if the blog names the potential offeror", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(b) doesn't require the rumour to name anyone. Speculation that the company is in play is speculation about a possible offer. The holding announcement itself needn't name the potential offeror either."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.2(b); r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"Post-approach rumour alone triggers the board." },

{ id:"tc2-011", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Talks between Harrow Ltd and an offeror have so far involved only the two chairmen and their advisers. Next week they plan to bring in three banks to arrange financing, and Harrow's division heads to help with due diligence.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 3.2 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Harrow's board must announce before talks extend beyond a very restricted group", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.2(c) requires the offeree board to announce 'when negotiations or discussions between the offeror and the offeree company are about to be extended to include more than a very restricted number of people'. Bringing in three banks and Harrow's division heads widens the circle well beyond a very restricted number.\n\nThe wording 'about to be extended' means the announcement should come before the circle widens, not after a leak has happened. Each new person is a new leak risk, and Rule 2's absolute secrecy becomes harder to sustain. The Code prefers an orderly announcement to a disorderly leak. Note 2 on Rules 3.1–3.3 adds that 'agreements and letters of intent relating to an offer or possible offer should be announced publicly', so if the parties sign a letter of intent as part of widening the talks, that too should be announced."},
    {t:"Nothing until a firm intention to make an offer is notified to the board", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(c) doesn't depend on a firm intention. Widening the circle is a trigger in its own right, because it raises the risk of a leak whatever stage the talks have reached."},
    {t:"An announcement only if Harrow's share price starts to move", correct:false,
     why:"Price movement is a separate trigger under Rule 3.2(b). Rule 3.2(c) is triggered by the widening of the talks itself, before any leak or price movement occurs."},
    {t:"The offeror must make the announcement, not Harrow's board", correct:false,
     why:"After an approach, primary responsibility normally rests with the offeree board. Rule 3.2(c) is one of the situations in which 'the offeree board must make an announcement'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.2(c); r 3, Note 2", takeaway:"Talks widening: offeree board announces first." },

{ id:"tc2x-022", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lapwing Ltd's board has decided to sell the company. Its adviser is about to send confidential information memoranda to 15 potential bidders. Lapwing's share price is stable and there's no rumour.",
  prompt:"Must Lapwing's board announce?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: before more than a very restricted number of offerors are approached", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.2(d) applies 'when the board of a company is aware that there are negotiations or discussions between a potential offeror and the holder, or holders, of shares carrying 30% or more of the voting rights of a company or when the board of a company is seeking potential offerors', and either (i) there is rumour, speculation, undue price movement or a significant increase in turnover, or (ii) 'more than a very restricted number of potential purchasers or offerors are about to be approached'.\n\nLapwing's board is seeking potential offerors, and approaching 15 is well beyond a very restricted number. Limb (ii) is met, even though limb (i) isn't. As with Rule 3.2(c), the duty attaches when the approaches are 'about to' happen, so the announcement should come first. A formal sale process involves many parties receiving confidential information, so a leak is likely. The Code requires the market to be told rather than leaving some investors to guess."},
    {t:"No: there's no rumour and the share price has been stable throughout", correct:false,
     why:"Under Rule 3.2(d) the triggers are alternatives. Limb (ii), more than a very restricted number of potential offerors about to be approached, applies on its own, without any rumour or price movement."},
    {t:"No: there's no potential offeror at all until a bid has actually been received", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(d) expressly covers a board that 'is seeking potential offerors'. A sale process run by the board is exactly that, even before any bid comes in."},
    {t:"Only once one of the bidders signs a letter of intent with the board", correct:false,
     why:"A letter of intent would need announcing under Note 2, but Rule 3.2(d)(ii) bites earlier, when more than a very restricted number of potential offerors are about to be approached."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.2(d)(ii)", takeaway:"Auction to many bidders: announce first." },

{ id:"tc2x-023", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Teo holds 35% of Merlin Ltd. He tells Merlin's board that he is negotiating to sell his stake to a buyer, who hasn't approached the board. A week later Merlin's shares jump 21% from their recent low on heavy volume.",
  prompt:"Who must announce?",
  options:[
    {t:"Merlin's board: it knows of talks with a 30%+ holder, and there is undue movement", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.2(d) applies 'when the board of a company is aware that there are negotiations or discussions between a potential offeror and the holder, or holders, of shares carrying 30% or more of the voting rights' and (i) the company is subject to rumour or speculation, undue price movement or a significant increase in turnover.\n\nMr Teo's 35% is above 30%, the board knows of the talks, and a 21% jump on heavy volume is undue movement (Note 3 treats about 20% above the relevant low as untoward). So Merlin's board must announce. The sale of a 35% stake would give the buyer effective control and a mandatory offer obligation under Rule 14, which is why the market needs to know.\n\nCompare Rule 3.3, which applies before an approach where the board doesn't know about the talks and the vendor's inadequate security contributed. There, the vendor announces. Here, the board knows, so the duty sits with the board."},
    {t:"Mr Teo, as the vendor of the block, under Rule 3.3", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.3 deals with a vendor whose inadequate security contributed to rumour or movement, typically where the board doesn't know about the talks. Here the board is aware of the negotiations, so Rule 3.2(d) puts the duty on the board. In cases of doubt, consult the Council."},
    {t:"The buyer, because it is the potential offeror and its talks caused the movement", correct:false,
     why:"Before an approach the potential offeror normally has responsibility (Rule 3.1). But Rule 3.2(d) specifically covers a board that knows of talks with a 30%+ holder, and makes it the board's job to announce when the triggers are met."},
    {t:"No one, until the sale of the block actually completes", correct:false,
     why:"Waiting until completion would leave the market trading on rumour for the whole negotiation. Rule 3.2(d) requires an announcement as soon as the board is aware of the talks and the trigger in (i) or (ii) is met."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.2(d); r 3.3; r 3, Note 3", takeaway:"Board knows of 30% sale talks + movement → board announces." },

{ id:"tc2-012", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Koh holds 34% of Linden Ltd. He has been negotiating the sale of his stake to a buyer, who hasn't approached Linden's board. The board knows nothing of the talks. Mr Koh took the calls in front of his driver, and Linden's shares are now moving on rumours of a deal.",
  prompt:"Who must announce?",
  options:[
    {t:"Mr Koh: as potential vendor, his poor security apparently contributed", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 3.3: 'The holder(s) of shares carrying 30% or more of the voting rights of a company may, on occasions, hold negotiations or discussions with a potential offeror before the offeror makes an approach to the board of the company. If the company then becomes the subject of rumour or speculation about a possible offer, or there is undue movement in its share price ..., and there are reasonable grounds for concluding that the actions of the potential vendor(s) (whether through inadequate security or otherwise) have contributed to the situation, the potential vendor(s) must make an announcement.'\n\nMr Koh holds 34%, there has been no approach to the board, the shares are moving on rumours, and his careless calls are reasonable grounds for concluding he contributed. The duty sits with him. Notice that Rule 3.3 says 'contributed', while Rule 3.1(a) for potential offerors says 'directly contributed'. If Linden's board did know of the talks, Rule 3.2(d) would put the duty on the board instead. In all cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted."},
    {t:"Linden's board, as the board of the company concerned", correct:false,
     why:"The board doesn't know about the talks and hasn't been approached. Rule 3.2(d) applies only when the board 'is aware' of negotiations with a 30%+ holder. Here the leak came from the vendor, so Rule 3.3 applies."},
    {t:"No one: there has been no approach to Linden's board yet", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.3 is written precisely for talks held 'before the offeror makes an approach to the board'. The absence of an approach is the point."},
    {t:"Only the buyer, since it is the potential offeror for the block of shares", correct:false,
     why:"The buyer may also have duties under Rule 3.1(a) if its own actions directly contributed. But the leak here came from Mr Koh's side, and Rule 3.3 puts the duty on the potential vendor whose actions contributed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.3; r 3.2(d)", takeaway:"Vendor's leak: vendor announces." },

{ id:"tc2-013", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Since the approach, Harrow's lowest share price was $0.80. It now trades at $0.97, while the market index is flat. Harrow's board doesn't plan to announce yet.",
  prompt:"How should the board treat the movement?",
  options:[
    {t:"As untoward: it is about 20% above the post-approach low, so consult if not announcing", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rules 3.1–3.3: 'A movement of approximately 20% above the lowest share price since the time of the approach or above an appropriate market index should be regarded as untoward. When there is such a movement or the offeree company is subject to rumour or speculation, the Council should be consulted if no immediate announcement is proposed to be made.'\n\nFrom $0.80 to $0.97 is a rise of about 21%, and the index is flat, so the movement isn't explained by the market. That is untoward. Undue movement after an approach is a Rule 3.2(b) trigger, so the board must announce. If it doesn't propose to announce immediately, it should consult the Council. The Note also says that in judging undue movement the Council may consider 'general market and sector movements, publicly available information about the company, trading activity in the company's securities, the time period over which the price movement has taken place', and other factors it considers appropriate. None of those explains this move."},
    {t:"As normal: a movement is only undue once it reaches 50%", correct:false,
     why:"The Note's benchmark is 'approximately 20%', not 50%. A 21% rise with a flat index is squarely untoward. Waiting for 50% would let a serious false market develop."},
    {t:"As irrelevant until a firm intention to make an offer exists", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 3.2(b) applies 'whether or not there is a firm intention to make an offer'. Undue movement following an approach triggers the board's duty regardless of how far the talks have got."},
    {t:"As untoward only if it is measured from the share price before the approach was first made", correct:false,
     why:"The benchmark in Note 3 is 'the lowest share price since the time of the approach', not the pre-approach price. Using the lowest post-approach price catches leaks that occur after talks begin."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 3; r 3.2(b)", takeaway:"~20% over post-approach low = untoward." },

{ id:"tc2x-024", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Since an approach to Osprey Ltd, its share price has risen 22% from its lowest point. Over the same weeks, the sector index has risen 24% after a government announcement favouring the sector. There's no rumour about Osprey.",
  prompt:"What should Osprey's board do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult the Council if not announcing; the sector rise is a factor it may weigh", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 says a movement of approximately 20% 'above the lowest share price since the time of the approach or above an appropriate market index' should be regarded as untoward. Osprey's 22% rise is above 20% in absolute terms, but it's slightly below the sector index's rise, so relative to an appropriate index it hasn't moved at all.\n\nThe Note gives the Council flexibility: in deciding whether there has been undue movement, it 'may have regard to general market and sector movements, publicly available information about the company, trading activity in the company's securities, the time period over which the price movement has taken place, and any such other factors as it considers appropriate'. A sector-wide rally after a government announcement is exactly that kind of factor. The Council may well conclude that the rise isn't undue. But the Note also says that when there is such a movement, 'the Council should be consulted if no immediate announcement is proposed'. So the board shouldn't decide alone that no announcement is needed."},
    {t:"Announce immediately: any 20% rise is automatically untoward", correct:false,
     why:"The 20% figure is a benchmark, not a mechanical rule. The Note expressly allows the Council to consider market and sector movements. With the sector up 24%, the rise may not be undue. The right course is to consult the Council rather than assume either way."},
    {t:"Do nothing: the sector explains the rise, so no further step is needed by the board at all", correct:false,
     why:"The board may be right that the sector explains it, but the Note says the Council should be consulted if there is such a movement and no immediate announcement is proposed. The decision isn't the board's alone."},
    {t:"Ask the potential offeror to make the announcement instead", correct:false,
     why:"After an approach, primary responsibility for announcements normally rests with the offeree board (Rule 3.2). The question of whether the movement is undue, and whether to announce, is for Osprey's board and, where in doubt, the Council."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 3", takeaway:"20% is a benchmark; market moves count; consult." },

{ id:"tc2-014", lesson:"TC2B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Where a potential vendor runs a closed tender for his offeree shares, an announcement is required only if there is rumour or speculation about a possible tender, undue movement in the share price or a significant increase in turnover.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rules 3.1–3.3: 'When the potential vendor calls a public tender for the sale of his shares in the offeree company, an announcement must be made. In the case of a closed tender, where there is rumour or speculation about a possible tender, or undue movement in the share price of the offeree company, or a significant increase in the volume of share turnover, an announcement must also be made.'\n\nA public tender is, by its nature, public, so the market must be told. A closed tender is private, with a limited set of invited bidders. It is treated like other confidential talks: an announcement is needed once there are signs that information is getting out, through rumour, price movement or volume. A closed tender for a 30%+ stake can still engage Rule 3.2(d) if the board knows of it, or Rule 3.3 if the vendor's inadequate security contributed to rumour."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 distinguishes the two cases. A public tender always requires an announcement. A closed tender requires one only when there is rumour or speculation, undue price movement, or a significant increase in turnover."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 4", takeaway:"Public tender: announce. Closed: on signs." },

{ id:"tc2x-025", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ms Lim, who holds 32% of Pelican Ltd, places advertisements inviting any interested party to bid for her stake by a set date. Pelican's share price hasn't moved.",
  prompt:"Must an announcement be made?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: a vendor's public tender for his offeree shares must be announced", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 says: 'When the potential vendor calls a public tender for the sale of his shares in the offeree company, an announcement must be made.' Advertising for bids from any interested party is a public tender. The obligation doesn't depend on rumour or price movement, so the stable share price is irrelevant.\n\nThe reason is straightforward. A public tender is already known to many people outside the company, so the market can't be left in the dark. Since Ms Lim's stake is over 30%, a buyer would acquire effective control and incur a mandatory offer obligation under Rule 14. The other shareholders have a direct interest in knowing that control of their company is up for sale.\n\nCompare a closed tender, where only selected bidders are invited. That needs an announcement only on rumour, undue price movement or a significant increase in turnover."},
    {t:"No: there has been no movement in the share price", correct:false,
     why:"Price movement matters for a closed tender. For a public tender, Note 4 requires an announcement whatever the share price is doing."},
    {t:"No: a public tender isn't an offer under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 specifically deals with tenders and requires an announcement for a public tender. A tender for a controlling stake is the beginning of a process that will normally lead to a mandatory offer."},
    {t:"Only once a winning bidder has been chosen and the terms of sale are agreed", correct:false,
     why:"The announcement duty arises when the vendor 'calls' the public tender, not when it ends. Shareholders are entitled to know control is for sale while the process runs."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 4", takeaway:"Public tender = announce, whatever the price." },

{ id:"tc2x-026", lesson:"TC2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A potential offeror signs a non-binding letter of intent with the offeree board setting out an indicative structure for a merger. What does Note 2 on Rules 3.1–3.3 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Letters of intent and agreements on a possible offer should be announced", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rules 3.1–3.3: 'Agreements and letters of intent relating to an offer or possible offer should be announced publicly.' The Note doesn't distinguish between binding and non-binding documents. A letter of intent is a concrete step towards an offer, and the market is entitled to know about it.\n\nThe Note works alongside the triggers in Rule 3.2. Signing a letter of intent usually comes with widening the circle (bringing in more advisers or financiers), which itself triggers Rule 3.2(c). It also creates price-sensitive information that, if kept back, would let insiders trade with an advantage. GP 12 requires full and prompt disclosure of relevant information. If a letter of intent mentioned an indicative price, Note 8 (added in 2026) would also be engaged: disclosing an indicative price before a firm intention is normally not permitted except in exceptional circumstances, and once disclosed it fixes a price floor and starts a 28-day clock."},
    {t:"Only binding agreements need be announced, not letters of intent", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 covers 'agreements and letters of intent'. Letters of intent are usually non-binding, so the Note clearly reaches beyond binding contracts. What matters is that the document relates to an offer or possible offer."},
    {t:"Letters of intent stay confidential until a firm offer has been announced", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says the opposite: they should be announced publicly. Keeping them secret would leave the market unaware of a significant step towards an offer."},
    {t:"Only the offeror's own shareholders need be told about it", correct:false,
     why:"The announcement must be public, so the whole market, including offeree shareholders, gets the information. Telling only some shareholders would offend GP 9."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Notes 2, 8; GP 12", takeaway:"Letters of intent: announce publicly." },

{ id:"tc2x-027", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An offeror plans a firm-intention announcement for Puffin Ltd. Its offer will be conditional on undertakings to accept from two founders, who together hold 28%. One founder's undertaking lapses if a competing offer at least 10% higher is announced.",
  prompt:"What must the announcement include about the undertakings?",
  options:[
    {t:"A statement by the founders on whether, and when, the undertakings can be revoked", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rules 3.1–3.3: 'When a proposed offer is conditional on acceptances or undertakings to accept by one or more shareholders, the proposed announcement must include a statement by those shareholders who have accepted or undertaken to accept the offer, whether such acceptances or undertakings are revocable, and if so, the conditions under which such acceptances or undertakings may be revoked.'\n\nThe statement comes from the founders themselves, not just the offeror. One undertaking can fall away on a competing offer 10% higher, and that must be stated. This fits two Notes on Rule 3.5. Note 3 says references to commitments must specify 'in what circumstances, if any, they will cease to be binding; for example, if a higher offer is made'. Note 1 says language mustn't give the impression that people have committed themselves when they haven't. Rule 3.5(c)(iii) also requires the announcement to state the holdings subject to irrevocable commitments. Other shareholders need all this to judge how likely the offer is to succeed, and whether a rival could still win."},
    {t:"Only the number of shares covered by the undertakings", correct:false,
     why:"The number of shares is required under Rule 3.5(c)(iii), but Note 1 requires more: a statement from the founders on revocability and the conditions for revocation. The fall-away on a higher offer is exactly the kind of term that must be disclosed."},
    {t:"Nothing: undertakings are private arrangements between the parties", correct:false,
     why:"Where the offer is conditional on undertakings, Note 1 requires them to be addressed in the announcement. The market needs to know how firm they are."},
    {t:"A statement that the founders 'will accept the offer', without any qualification at all", correct:false,
     why:"That would overstate the position, because one undertaking can lapse. Note 1 on Rule 3.5 says announcements must avoid statements that give the impression people have committed themselves when they haven't."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 1; r 3.5(c)(iii), Notes 1, 3", takeaway:"Shareholders must say if, and when, undertakings can be revoked." },

{ id:"tc2-015", lesson:"TC2B", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Within ____ minutes of incurring an obligation to make or revise an offer, the offeror must announce, or request a temporary trading halt and announce before the halt is lifted.",
  accept:["30","thirty","30 minutes","thirty minutes"], answer:"30",
  why:"The Note on Rule 3, headed 'Timing of announcements', says: 'Within 30 minutes of incurring an obligation to make an offer or to revise an offer already made, the offeror must either make an announcement, or request the Securities Exchange for a temporary halt in trading of the offeree company's shares and make an announcement before the trading suspension is lifted.'\n\nThis puts a concrete time on two provisions. Rule 3.1(b) requires an announcement 'immediately upon an acquisition of shares which gives rise to an obligation to make an offer under Rule 14'. Rule 3.6 requires one 'immediately after' an acquisition that triggers a mandatory offer, a cash offer under Rule 17.1, or a revision under Rule 20. The 30-minute window is short because every minute the market trades without knowing a mandatory offer is coming, sellers may be selling below the price they'll be offered. If the offeror can't get the announcement out within 30 minutes, it must ask for a halt, so the market stops while it finishes.",
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note on timing; r 3.1(b); r 3.6", takeaway:"30 minutes, or halt then announce." },

{ id:"tc2x-028", lesson:"TC2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At 10:05 am, Swift Ltd's broker completes a purchase that takes Swift and its concert parties from 29.4% to 30.2% of Teal Ltd. Swift's announcement won't be ready until early afternoon.",
  prompt:"What must Swift do?",
  options:[
    {t:"By 10:35 am, announce, or request a halt and announce before it is lifted", correct:true,
     why:"The purchase takes Swift's group from below 30% to 30.2%, so it has acquired effective control and incurred an obligation to make a mandatory offer under Rule 14. Rule 3.1(b) requires an announcement 'immediately upon an acquisition of shares which gives rise to an obligation to make an offer under Rule 14'.\n\nThe Note on timing translates 'immediately' into a deadline: 'Within 30 minutes of incurring an obligation to make an offer ..., the offeror must either make an announcement, or request the Securities Exchange for a temporary halt in trading of the offeree company's shares and make an announcement before the trading suspension is lifted.' The obligation arose at 10:05, so Swift has until 10:35. Since its announcement won't be ready, it must request a halt by then and announce before trading resumes. The announcement must include the information Rule 3.6 requires (voting rights acquired, price paid) and Rule 3.5's contents so far as not already announced, including a cash confirmation if the offer involves cash."},
    {t:"Announce by the end of the trading day on which it crossed 30%", correct:false,
     why:"The Note on timing allows 30 minutes, not until the end of the day. A day's trading without knowledge of a mandatory offer could see many shareholders sell below the price they'll be offered."},
    {t:"Wait until a full announcement is ready, however long that takes, then publish", correct:false,
     why:"If Swift can't announce within 30 minutes, it must request a trading halt within that time. It can't let trading continue while it drafts."},
    {t:"Nothing: 30.2% is barely over the line, so no step is needed", correct:false,
     why:"Any acquisition that takes a group from below 30% to 30% or more triggers Rule 14. The size of the excess doesn't matter. The 30-minute clock starts at 10:05."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3.1(b); r 3, Note on timing; r 3.6", takeaway:"Trigger at 10:05 → announce or halt by 10:35." },

{ id:"tc2x-029", lesson:"TC2B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A paid press notice placed in the most widely circulated English-language national newspaper satisfies Note 7, even though that newspaper doesn't publish on Sundays.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 on Rules 3.1–3.3: 'A paid press notice, for the purpose of this Rule or other parts of the Code where this term is used, refers to a paid advertisement in the most widely circulated English-language national newspaper published daily. For the avoidance of doubt, the reference to national newspapers published daily includes those published every day except Sunday.'\n\nSo a newspaper published Monday to Saturday counts as 'published daily'. The definition matters because Rule 3.2(a) requires the offeree board, on notification of a firm intention from a serious source, to 'issue a paid press notice or, where the offeror has published a paid press notice, an announcement'. The definition applies across the Code wherever the term appears. The test is the most widely circulated English-language national daily, which ensures the notice reaches the widest readership."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says expressly that national newspapers 'published daily' include 'those published every day except Sunday'. Not publishing on Sundays doesn't disqualify the paper."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 7; r 3.2(a)", takeaway:"Mon–Sat daily counts." },

{ id:"tc2-016", lesson:"TC2B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each situation to who must announce, and under which rule.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Pre-approach rumour traced to the bidder's stakebuilding", r:"Potential offeror, r 3.1(a)"},
    {l:"Acquisition that triggers a mandatory offer", r:"Offeror, immediately, r 3.1(b)"},
    {l:"Board seeking offerors, about to approach more than a very restricted number", r:"Offeree board, r 3.2(d)"},
    {l:"Pre-approach leak from a 30% holder's sale talks", r:"Potential vendor, r 3.3"}
  ],
  why:"The allocation follows two questions: has the board been approached, and whose actions caused the problem?\n\nBefore an approach, responsibility normally rests with the potential offeror (Rule 3.1). It must announce under 3.1(a) where there is rumour, speculation or undue movement and there are reasonable grounds for concluding its own actions (inadequate security, share purchases or otherwise) directly contributed. Under 3.1(b) it must announce immediately on an acquisition that triggers a Rule 14 offer, which the Note on timing makes 30 minutes.\n\nAfter an approach, primary responsibility normally rests with the offeree board (Rule 3.2): on a firm intention from a serious source (a), on rumour or undue movement (b), when talks are about to widen (c), or when it knows of talks with a 30%+ holder or is seeking offerors and the triggers in (d) are met.\n\nA 30%+ holder who negotiates before any approach must announce under Rule 3.3 if its actions contributed to rumour or undue movement. In all cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.",
  cite:"SG Code r 3.1–3.3", takeaway:"Who caused it, and whether the board's been approached." }

]);

