/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T2 · Lesson TC2C Put Up or Shut Up & Indicative Prices (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code Notes 5, 6, 8 on Rules 3.1–3.3 (Note 5 direction power and Note 8 new in 2026). Replaces TC2C in takeoverCode2.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc2-017", lesson:"TC2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"After a holding announcement, nothing further is announced for a month. What must happen?",
  options:[
    {t:"Monthly progress announcements, until a firm intention or a decision not to proceed", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 on Rules 3.1–3.3 says: 'In cases where an announcement of a firm intention to make an offer is premature or inappropriate, a holding announcement that talks are taking place (without naming the potential offeror) or that a potential offeror is considering making an offer may be made. If following such announcement, no further announcement has been made in respect of the offer or possible offer within 1 month, an announcement must be made setting out the progress on the talks or the consideration of the offer or possible offer. This obligation to provide monthly updates continues until a firm intention to make an offer or a decision not to proceed with an offer is announced.'\n\nIt also says: 'When talks are terminated or a potential offeror decides not to proceed with an offer, an announcement must be made to that effect.'\n\nThe monthly updates stop the market being left with stale information. Since 2026, the Council also has power to cut the process short by directing the potential offeror to decide within 28 days."},
    {t:"The possible offer lapses automatically after a month of silence", correct:false,
     why:"There's no offer to lapse yet. A holding announcement only says talks are taking place or that a potential offeror is considering an offer. Note 5 requires monthly progress updates, not automatic termination."},
    {t:"The potential offeror must now make a firm offer within 28 days", correct:false,
     why:"One month without news triggers a progress update, not an obligation to bid. The potential offeror can still decide not to proceed. Since 2026, the Council can direct a decision within 28 days, but even then the choice includes a no intention to bid statement."},
    {t:"Nothing more needs to be said until the offeree company's next results announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 sets a monthly rhythm, not a results-linked one. If a month passes without a further announcement, an update is required, and the updates continue monthly."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"Holding announcement → monthly updates." },

{ id:"tc2x-040", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Robin Ltd's board issues a holding announcement saying it is in talks that may or may not lead to an offer, without naming the other party. Six weeks later, the talks collapse.",
  prompt:"What must happen?",
  options:[
    {t:"Robin must announce that the talks have been terminated", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 permits a holding announcement 'that talks are taking place (without naming the potential offeror)'. It then requires two kinds of follow-up. If nothing further has been announced within 1 month, there must be a progress update, and the updates continue monthly. And 'When talks are terminated or a potential offeror decides not to proceed with an offer, an announcement must be made to that effect.'\n\nSix weeks after the holding announcement, a monthly update should already have gone out at the one-month mark. Now that the talks have ended, the market must be told. Otherwise investors would keep trading on the belief that an offer might come. That would be a false market, contrary to GP 12."},
    {t:"Nothing: the holding announcement didn't name anyone, so nothing needs correcting", correct:false,
     why:"Not naming the potential offeror doesn't change the market's understanding that an offer might be coming. Note 5 requires an announcement when talks are terminated, whether or not the potential offeror was named."},
    {t:"Only the potential offeror needs to announce", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 says an announcement must be made when talks end. Robin's board made the holding announcement and has primary responsibility after an approach (Rule 3.2), so it would normally make the termination announcement, though the potential offeror may announce too."},
    {t:"It can wait for the next monthly update", correct:false,
     why:"Termination is a separate trigger. The Note says that when talks are terminated, 'an announcement must be made to that effect'. Waiting for the monthly cycle would leave the market misinformed in the meantime."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5; GP 12", takeaway:"Talks end → say so." },

{ id:"tc2-018", lesson:"TC2C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Under the 2026 addition to Note 5, what may the Council direct a potential offeror to do?",
  options:[
    {t:"By day 28 from the direction, announce a firm intention or no intention to bid", correct:true,
     why:"The 2026 addition to Note 5 reads: 'The Council reserves the right to, following consultation with the potential offeror and the offeree company, direct the potential offeror to, by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction, either: (a) announce a firm intention to make an offer; or (b) make a no intention to bid statement. The Council may impose an earlier or later deadline where appropriate.'\n\nThree features stand out. The Council must consult both the potential offeror and the offeree company before it directs. The clock runs from the direction, not from the holding announcement. And the potential offeror keeps the choice: it must either commit to a firm offer or walk away. The Council can't compel it to bid. This is often called a put-up-or-shut-up power. It is separate from the Note 6 deadline for potential competing offerors and from the Note 8 clock that starts automatically when an indicative price is disclosed."},
    {t:"Make an offer to all shareholders within 28 days of the direction", correct:false,
     why:"The Council can force a decision, not an offer. The potential offeror may choose a no intention to bid statement instead. Compelling an offer would be inconsistent with the voluntary nature of a non-mandatory bid."},
    {t:"Post an offer document to shareholders within 28 days of the direction", correct:false,
     why:"The direction is about announcing intentions: a firm intention or a no intention to bid statement. Posting the offer document follows later under the offer timetable."},
    {t:"Suspend trading in the offeree company's shares for 28 days while it makes up its mind", correct:false,
     why:"That isn't what the direction does. Trading halts are a separate tool, requested by the offeree board under Rule 3.4 or as an alternative to an immediate announcement under Note 5."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"New: Council can impose 28-day put up or shut up." },

{ id:"tc2-019", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Talon Capital's holding announcement about Brook Ltd went out in January. Every month since, Talon has issued an update saying it is still considering an offer. By June, Brook's board says the uncertainty is damaging its business.",
  prompt:"What can the Council do under the amended Code?",
  options:[
    {t:"After consulting both, set Talon a deadline, normally day 28, to bid or say it won't", correct:true,
     why:"Before 2026, Note 5 required monthly updates but gave the Council no express power to bring the process to an end. A potential offeror could keep 'considering' indefinitely, leaving the offeree in an offer period with its board restrained by GP 7 and Rule 5, its staff and customers uncertain, and its share price driven by speculation.\n\nThe 2026 amendment closes that gap. The Council 'reserves the right to, following consultation with the potential offeror and the offeree company, direct the potential offeror to, by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction, either: (a) announce a firm intention to make an offer; or (b) make a no intention to bid statement. The Council may impose an earlier or later deadline where appropriate.' Brook's board can raise its concerns with the Council. If the Council directs, Talon keeps the choice of outcome but loses control of the timing."},
    {t:"Nothing: the monthly updates can simply continue until Talon makes up its mind", correct:false,
     why:"That was the practical effect of Note 5 before 2026. The amendment added the Council's power to direct a decision by a deadline. Monthly updates no longer guarantee an open-ended process."},
    {t:"Order Talon to make an offer for Brook at a price the Council considers fair", correct:false,
     why:"The Council can require a decision, not an offer, and it doesn't set prices. Talon may choose to make a no intention to bid statement instead."},
    {t:"Allow Brook's board to set Talon a deadline of its own choosing for the decision to bid", correct:false,
     why:"The power belongs to the Council, which must consult both parties first. Brook's board can't impose a Code deadline, though it can ask the Council to act."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"Open-ended 'considering an offer' can now be cut off." },

{ id:"tc2-020", lesson:"TC2C", type:"tf", amended:true,
  prompt:"True or false: When the Council directs a potential offeror to clarify its intentions under Note 5, the 28 days run from the date of the original holding announcement.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 says the potential offeror must act 'by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction'. The clock starts with the direction, not with the holding announcement. A holding announcement may have been made months earlier; running the deadline from then would often mean it had already passed.\n\nThe Council 'may impose an earlier or later deadline where appropriate', so 28 days is the default, not a fixed period. Compare the other clarification clocks. Under Note 8, the 28 days run from the date the indicative price was disclosed, with no direction needed. Under Note 6, the deadline for a potential competing offeror is the 53rd day from despatch of the first offeror's initial offer document, or for a scheme the 7th day before the shareholders' meeting. If a deadline falls on a non-business day, Note 3 on the Definitions extends it to the next business day."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The 28 days run 'from the date of the Council's direction'. The date of the holding announcement doesn't start the clock under Note 5."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5", takeaway:"Clock runs from the direction." },

{ id:"tc2x-041", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"After consulting both parties, the Council directs a potential offeror on 3 February (not a leap year) to clarify its intentions under Note 5. It sets no different deadline.",
  prompt:"By when must the potential offeror announce a firm intention or make a no intention to bid statement?",
  options:[
    {t:"3 March, the 28th day from the direction", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 requires the potential offeror to act 'by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction'. Counting from 3 February: 4 February is day 1, 28 February is day 25, 1 March is day 26, 2 March is day 27 and 3 March is day 28. So the deadline is 3 March.\n\nBy then the potential offeror must either announce a firm intention to make an offer, which binds it under Rule 4, or make a no intention to bid statement. The Council could have set an earlier or later deadline, but here it didn't. If 3 March were not a business day, Note 3 on the Definitions would extend the deadline to the next business day. In practice, potential offerors work towards the deadline well before it, because a firm-intention announcement needs a cash confirmation and all the Rule 3.5 contents."},
    {t:"3 April, one month after the direction", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 sets 28 days, not one month. One month is the interval for monthly updates after a holding announcement, which is a different obligation."},
    {t:"28 February, the end of the month", correct:false,
     why:"The 28 days run from the date of the direction, 3 February. Counting forward 28 days lands on 3 March, not the end of February."},
    {t:"There's no deadline unless the Council sets a specific date", correct:false,
     why:"The default deadline is in Note 5 itself: the 28th day from the direction. The Council may set an earlier or later date, but if it doesn't, the 28-day default applies."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 5; Notes on Defns, Note 3", takeaway:"Direction 3 Feb → decide by 3 Mar." },

{ id:"tc2-021", lesson:"TC2C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Before an announcement of a firm intention to make an offer, may a potential offeror disclose an indicative offer price?",
  options:[
    {t:"Normally not, except in exceptional circumstances", correct:true,
     why:"Note 8, added in 2026: 'The disclosure of an indicative offer price is normally not permitted prior to an announcement of a firm intention to make an offer being made except in exceptional circumstances.' The reason is the false-market risk. An indicative price, before any commitment, can move the share price on a number the potential offeror may never pay.\n\nIf a price is disclosed, two consequences follow. 'Where the statement concerned relates to the price of a possible offer (or a particular exchange ratio in case of a securities exchange offer), any subsequent offer made by the potential offeror must be made on the same or better terms.' And the potential offeror 'must, by the 28th day from the date of the disclosure of the indicative price, either: (a) announce a firm intention to make an offer; or (b) make a no intention to bid statement'. The Council may impose an earlier or later deadline. So naming a price both sets a floor and starts the clock."},
    {t:"Yes, freely, provided it is clearly marked as indicative only", correct:false,
     why:"Labelling a price 'indicative' doesn't make disclosure permitted. Note 8 says disclosure of an indicative offer price before a firm intention is 'normally not permitted ... except in exceptional circumstances'."},
    {t:"Yes, if the offeree board agrees", correct:false,
     why:"The test in Note 8 is exceptional circumstances, not the offeree board's consent. The concern is the effect on the market, which the board can't waive. In cases of doubt, consult the Council."},
    {t:"Never", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 allows disclosure 'in exceptional circumstances'. It is a strong default, not an absolute ban, and disclosure brings the price floor and 28-day clock with it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 8", takeaway:"Indicative price: normally not before a firm offer." },

{ id:"tc2-022", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"In exceptional circumstances, Kestrel Ltd discloses on 4 May that it is considering a possible offer for Wren Ltd at $2.40 a share. It hasn't announced a firm intention.",
  prompt:"What follows under Note 8?",
  options:[
    {t:"Any offer must be $2.40 or better, and Kestrel must decide by day 28 from 4 May", correct:true,
     why:"Note 8 attaches two consequences to disclosing a possible offer price. First: 'any subsequent offer made by the potential offeror must be made on the same or better terms'. Kestrel can't later offer $2.30 because the market has fallen or due diligence disappointed. Second: 'the potential offeror must, by the 28th day from the date of the disclosure of the indicative price, either: (a) announce a firm intention to make an offer; or (b) make a no intention to bid statement'.\n\nThe 28 days run from the disclosure on 4 May, so the deadline is 1 June (5 May is day 1, 31 May is day 27, 1 June is day 28). No Council direction is needed to start this clock, which is the key difference from Note 5. The Council 'reserves the right to impose an earlier or later deadline where appropriate'. The logic is that once a potential offeror has moved the market with a number, it must either stand behind that number or withdraw promptly."},
    {t:"Kestrel may offer less than $2.40 if Wren's share price falls in the meantime, as it may", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 says any subsequent offer 'must be made on the same or better terms'. A fall in Wren's share price doesn't release Kestrel from the price it disclosed. That is part of the cost of naming a price early."},
    {t:"Kestrel need only give monthly progress updates until it decides", correct:false,
     why:"Monthly updates apply after a holding announcement under Note 5. Disclosing a price brings a specific 28-day deadline under Note 8, by which Kestrel must announce a firm intention or a no intention to bid statement."},
    {t:"The 28 days start to run only if the Council issues a direction to Kestrel", correct:false,
     why:"That's the Note 5 mechanism. Under Note 8, the clock runs automatically 'from the date of the disclosure of the indicative price'. No direction is needed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 8", takeaway:"Price named: same or better; decide in 28 days." },

{ id:"tc2x-042", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"In exceptional circumstances, Gale Ltd discloses on 10 June that it is considering a possible share-for-share offer for Harbour Ltd at 1 new Gale share for every 2 Harbour shares. Gale later wants to offer 1 Gale share for every 2.5 Harbour shares.",
  prompt:"Can it, and by when must it decide?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: any offer must match or beat the disclosed ratio, and it must decide by 8 July", correct:true,
     why:"Note 8 covers an indicative price and, 'in case of a securities exchange offer', 'a particular exchange ratio'. Once disclosed, 'any subsequent offer made by the potential offeror must be made on the same or better terms'.\n\nOne Gale share for every 2 Harbour shares gives each Harbour share half a Gale share. One for every 2.5 gives each Harbour share only 0.4 of a Gale share. That is worse for Harbour shareholders, so Gale can't do it. It could offer 1 for 2, or something better such as 1 for 1.8.\n\nThe 28-day clock runs from the disclosure on 10 June: 11 June is day 1, 30 June is day 20, and 8 July is day 28. By then Gale must announce a firm intention or make a no intention to bid statement, unless the Council sets a different deadline. Exchange ratios get the same treatment as cash prices because they too move the offeree's share price."},
    {t:"Yes: Note 8 applies only to indicative cash prices, not share ratios", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 expressly covers 'a particular exchange ratio in case of a securities exchange offer'. A disclosed share ratio binds Gale in the same way a cash price would."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as it announces its firm intention by 10 July", correct:false,
     why:"Two problems. The new ratio is worse for Harbour holders, which Note 8 forbids. And the deadline is the 28th day from 10 June, which is 8 July, not 10 July."},
    {t:"Yes, if Gale's share price has risen enough to keep the value of the offer the same", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 refers to the 'same or better terms', meaning the ratio itself. Arguments about share-price movements don't change the terms disclosed. In any unusual case the Council should be consulted, but the default rule is clear."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 8", takeaway:"Ratios bind too: same or better; 28 days." },

{ id:"tc2x-043", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"With no exceptional circumstances, a potential offeror's CEO tells a television interviewer that it is 'looking at around $3 a share' for Ibis Ltd. No firm intention has been announced.",
  prompt:"What is the position under Note 8?",
  options:[
    {t:"It was improper, but binds: any offer must be $3 or more, and a 28-day clock runs", correct:true,
     why:"Note 8 says disclosure of an indicative offer price before a firm intention 'is normally not permitted ... except in exceptional circumstances'. A CEO naming a figure in a TV interview, with no exceptional circumstances, is the kind of disclosure the Note is meant to prevent. It moves Ibis's share price on a number the potential offeror hasn't committed to.\n\nThe Note's consequences attach to 'the statement concerned' whenever it relates to the price of a possible offer. Any subsequent offer 'must be made on the same or better terms', and the potential offeror must, by the 28th day from the disclosure, announce a firm intention or make a no intention to bid statement. An improper disclosure doesn't escape those consequences, since otherwise breaking the rule would be cheaper than following it. The statement also engages GP 12 (parties must take care not to make statements which may mislead the market), and the 2026 Rule 9.1 changes on media interviews (TC3F). The potential offeror should consult the Council promptly."},
    {t:"No consequences follow, because 'around $3' isn't a precise price", correct:false,
     why:"An approximate figure still tells the market what to expect. The Council is likely to treat 'around $3' as a statement relating to the price of a possible offer. Hedged wording doesn't avoid Note 8."},
    {t:"The potential offeror must now make a firm offer at $3 within 28 days", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 doesn't force an offer. It requires a decision within 28 days: a firm intention (at $3 or better) or a no intention to bid statement."},
    {t:"Only the CEO is personally bound by what he said, not the potential offeror itself", correct:false,
     why:"The CEO was speaking for the potential offeror. Statements by its chief executive about its intentions are attributed to it. The same-or-better rule and the 28-day clock bind the potential offeror."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 8; GP 12", takeaway:"Loose price talk still binds and starts the clock." },

{ id:"tc2-023", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Orchid Ltd has announced a firm intention to acquire Pebble Ltd by scheme of arrangement. The scheme meeting is on 20 November. In September, rival Saltire Ltd becomes the subject of a possible offer announcement.",
  prompt:"By when must Saltire normally clarify its intentions?",
  options:[
    {t:"13 November, the 7th day before the scheme meeting", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 deals with a 'potential competing offeror' that becomes the subject of a possible offer announcement after another offeror has announced a firm intention. It 'must normally' either announce a firm intention or make a no intention to bid statement by a deadline.\n\nFor a contractual offer, the deadline is 'the 53rd day from the date the first offeror despatches its initial offer document'. 'Where the first offeror's offer is being implemented by way of a scheme of arrangement, a trust scheme or an amalgamation, the above deadline ... would normally be no later than the 7th day prior to the date of the shareholders' meeting to approve the relevant scheme or amalgamation.' The scheme meeting is on 20 November, so the 7th day before is 13 November.\n\nThe purpose is to give Pebble's shareholders certainty before they vote. They should know whether a rival bid is coming before deciding on Orchid's scheme. 'The Council reserves the right to impose an earlier or later deadline where appropriate.'"},
    {t:"The 53rd day from despatch of the scheme document", correct:false,
     why:"The 53rd-day deadline applies where the first offer is a contractual offer. For a scheme, trust scheme or amalgamation, Note 6 uses the 7th day before the shareholders' meeting instead."},
    {t:"The 28th day from Saltire's possible offer announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 has its own deadlines. A 28-day clock arises under Note 5 when the Council directs it, or under Note 8 when an indicative price is disclosed. Neither is the basic rule for a competing offeror."},
    {t:"There is no deadline while the scheme is pending", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 sets one precisely because a scheme is pending. Without it, a rival could hover until after the vote, leaving shareholders to decide without knowing whether a competing bid was coming."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 6", takeaway:"Rival vs scheme: 7 days before the meeting." },

{ id:"tc2x-044", lesson:"TC2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Jade Ltd announced a firm intention to make a contractual offer for Kiwi Ltd and despatched its offer document on 2 March. On 20 March, Lark Ltd becomes the subject of a possible competing offer announcement.",
  prompt:"By when must Lark normally clarify its intentions?",
  options:[
    {t:"24 April, day 53 from despatch of Jade's offer document", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6: where an offeror has announced a firm intention and 'a potential competing offeror becomes the subject of a possible offer announcement, the potential competing offeror must normally, by the 53rd day from the date the first offeror despatches its initial offer document, either: (a) announce a firm intention to make an offer; or (b) make a no intention to bid statement'.\n\nThe clock runs from Jade's despatch on 2 March, not from Lark's own announcement on 20 March. Counting: 3 March is day 1, 31 March is day 29, and 24 April is day 53. So Lark's deadline is 24 April.\n\nWhy day 53? It ties the rival's decision to the first offer's timetable. Kiwi shareholders shouldn't be asked to decide on Jade's offer while a possible rival bid is still unresolved. The Council may impose an earlier or later deadline, and if 24 April isn't a business day, Note 3 on the Definitions extends it to the next business day."},
    {t:"17 April, 28 days from Lark's possible offer announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 doesn't use a 28-day clock from the rival's own announcement. Its deadline is tied to the first offeror's timetable: the 53rd day from despatch of the initial offer document."},
    {t:"12 May, 53 days from Lark's possible offer announcement", correct:false,
     why:"The 53 days run 'from the date the first offeror despatches its initial offer document', which was 2 March. Starting from Lark's announcement would let a late rival push the timetable out."},
    {t:"The 7th day before Kiwi's shareholders' meeting", correct:false,
     why:"That deadline applies where the first offer is a scheme, trust scheme or amalgamation. Jade's offer is contractual, so the 53rd-day rule applies."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Note 6", takeaway:"Contractual first offer: day 53 from its despatch." },

{ id:"tc2x-045", lesson:"TC2C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Which of the Code's clarification deadlines requires a Council direction before it starts to run?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only the Note 5 put-up-or-shut-up deadline", correct:true,
     why:"There are three clarification mechanisms in the Notes on Rules 3.1–3.3.\n\nNote 5 (new in 2026): the Council may, after consulting both parties, 'direct the potential offeror to, by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction', announce a firm intention or make a no intention to bid statement. This deadline needs a direction.\n\nNote 6: a potential competing offeror 'must normally' clarify by the 53rd day from despatch of the first offeror's initial offer document, or for a scheme, trust scheme or amalgamation, no later than the 7th day before the shareholders' meeting. This applies automatically.\n\nNote 8 (new in 2026): after disclosing an indicative price, the potential offeror 'must, by the 28th day from the date of the disclosure', clarify. This applies automatically.\n\nIn all three, the Council may set an earlier or later deadline, and the potential offeror's choice is the same: firm intention or no intention to bid."},
    {t:"All three deadlines need a direction", correct:false,
     why:"Notes 6 and 8 apply automatically. Note 6 says the potential competing offeror 'must normally' clarify by the stated date, and Note 8 runs from the disclosure of the indicative price. Only Note 5 depends on the Council directing."},
    {t:"Only the Note 8 indicative-price deadline, of 28 days", correct:false,
     why:"Note 8 runs automatically from the date of the disclosure. It is the Note 5 deadline that runs from 'the date of the Council's direction'."},
    {t:"None: they all run automatically", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5's deadline exists only if the Council directs, after consulting the potential offeror and the offeree. Without a direction, the potential offeror under a holding announcement simply continues with monthly updates."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Notes 5, 6, 8", takeaway:"Note 5 needs a direction; Notes 6 and 8 don't." },

{ id:"tc2-024", lesson:"TC2C", type:"match", amended:true,
  prompt:"Match each situation to its clarification deadline.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Council direction to a potential offeror", r:"28th day from the direction"},
    {l:"Indicative offer price disclosed", r:"28th day from the disclosure"},
    {l:"Rival to a contractual offer", r:"53rd day from first offeror's initial offer document"},
    {l:"Rival to a scheme or amalgamation", r:"7th day before the shareholders' meeting"}
  ],
  why:"Note 5 (direction, new in 2026): after consulting the potential offeror and the offeree, the Council may direct the potential offeror to clarify 'by the 28th day from the date of the Council's direction'.\n\nNote 8 (indicative price, new in 2026): a potential offeror that discloses an indicative price or exchange ratio 'must, by the 28th day from the date of the disclosure of the indicative price', clarify. It is also bound to offer on the same or better terms.\n\nNote 6 (competing offerors): a potential competing offeror must normally clarify 'by the 53rd day from the date the first offeror despatches its initial offer document'. If the first offer is a scheme, trust scheme or amalgamation, the deadline is normally 'no later than the 7th day prior to the date of the shareholders' meeting'.\n\nIn every case the Council may impose an earlier or later deadline, and the choice is the same: announce a firm intention to make an offer, or make a no intention to bid statement.",
  cite:"SG Code r 3, Notes 5, 6, 8", takeaway:"Four clocks, one choice: bid or walk." }

]);

;
