/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T3 · Lesson TC3C Independent Advice (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code r 7.1–7.3 and Notes. Replaces TC3C in takeoverCode3.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc3-016", lesson:"TC3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does Rule 7.1 require of the offeree board?",
  options:[
    {t:"Get competent independent advice on any offer and make its substance known", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 7.1: 'The board of the offeree company must obtain competent independent advice on any offer and the substance of such advice must be made known to its shareholders.' This turns GP 8 (seek competent independent advice) into a binding rule, and serves GP 10 (sufficient information, advice and time).\n\nThe Notes add detail. Note 2: where there is a significant area of uncertainty in the latest accounts (for example a qualified audit report, a material provision or contingent liability, or doubt over the value of a substantial asset), 'the board and the independent adviser should highlight the factors which they consider important'. Note 3: where directors can't give a firm recommendation, or views diverge within the board or between the board and the adviser, 'this must be drawn to shareholders' attention and an explanation given, including the arguments for acceptance or rejection'. Note 4 exempts partial offers that couldn't take the offeror to 30%."},
    {t:"Obtain advice only if it intends to recommend that shareholders accept", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 7.1 applies to 'any offer', whatever the board's view. Shareholders need independent advice most of all when the board is against the offer, since a board's resistance can reflect its own interests."},
    {t:"Obtain advice, but keep it confidential to the board", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 7.1 requires that 'the substance of such advice must be made known to its shareholders'. Advice that only the board sees doesn't help shareholders decide."},
    {t:"Rely on the offeror's financial adviser for the advice", correct:false,
     why:"The advice must be independent. Rule 7.3 says the Council won't normally accept anyone in the same group as the offeror's financial or professional adviser."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.1, Notes 2–3; GP 8", takeaway:"Independent advice on every offer, shared." },

{ id:"tc3x-040", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Bunting Ltd's latest audited accounts carry a qualified audit opinion over the carrying value of its largest overseas subsidiary. An offer for Bunting is now under way.",
  prompt:"What does Note 2 on Rule 7.1 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board and adviser should highlight the key factors arising from it", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2: 'When there is a significant area of uncertainty in the most recently published accounts or interim figures of the offeree company (e.g. a qualified audit report, a material provision or contingent liability or doubt over the real value of a substantial asset, including a subsidiary company), the board and the independent adviser should highlight the factors which they consider important.'\n\nBunting's case involves two of the Note's examples at once: a qualified audit report, and doubt over the real value of a substantial asset, its largest subsidiary. Shareholders weighing the offer price against the company's apparent value need to know how much uncertainty there is in that value. Both the board and the adviser have a role. Whatever they publish must meet the prospectus-level standard of Rule 8.2 and GP 11."},
    {t:"Nothing: the qualification is already public in the published accounts", correct:false,
     why:"Being in the accounts isn't the same as being drawn to shareholders' attention in the context of the offer. Note 2 requires the board and the adviser to highlight the factors they consider important."},
    {t:"The offer must be suspended until the audit issue has been fully resolved", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in Rule 7 suspends an offer because of uncertainty in the accounts. The response is disclosure and analysis, so shareholders can make an informed decision."},
    {t:"Only the auditors need comment, in their own report", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 places the duty on 'the board and the independent adviser'. The auditors' qualification is the cause of the uncertainty, but explaining its significance for the offer is for the board and adviser."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.1, Note 2", takeaway:"Uncertain accounts: board and IFA highlight the issues." },

{ id:"tc3x-041", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The independent adviser to Crake Ltd says the offer is 'not fair but reasonable'. Four of Crake's seven directors want to recommend acceptance; three want to recommend rejection.",
  prompt:"What must the circular contain?",
  options:[
    {t:"A clear statement of the split, with the case for accepting and rejecting", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 7.1: 'When directors are unable to give a firm recommendation or when there is a divergence of views amongst board members or between the board and the independent adviser as to either the merits of an offer or the recommendation being made, this must be drawn to shareholders' attention and an explanation given, including the arguments for acceptance or rejection, emphasising the important factors.'\n\nBoth kinds of divergence are present: within the board (four against three), and possibly between the majority and the adviser, whose 'not fair' conclusion sits awkwardly with a recommendation to accept. Shareholders need to see the split and the reasons on each side, not a majority view presented as unanimous. Under Rule 8.2 Note 5, the language must reflect the position clearly and concisely. Under GP 13, any director with a personal interest must have regard to shareholders as a whole."},
    {t:"Only the majority's recommendation, as the board's view", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 says the divergence 'must be drawn to shareholders' attention' with an explanation of both sides. Presenting only the majority view would hide relevant information."},
    {t:"Nothing about the offer until the board can reach a unanimous view on it together", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't require unanimity. It requires transparency about the split, so shareholders can weigh the arguments themselves."},
    {t:"Only the adviser's opinion, since the board itself is divided", correct:false,
     why:"The board must still give its views. Note 3 requires an explanation of the divergence, including the arguments for acceptance and rejection, alongside the adviser's advice."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.1, Note 3", takeaway:"Split board or IFA disagreement: show both sides." },

{ id:"tc3-017", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Brightwater Ltd's CEO and finance director tell the chairman they are exploring a management buy-out with a private equity backer. No approach has been made yet.",
  prompt:"When should the board appoint an independent adviser?",
  options:[
    {t:"As soon as possible after learning such an offer may be made", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 7.1: 'The requirement for competent independent advice is particularly important where the offer is a management buy-out or similar transaction or is being made by or with the co-operation of the existing controlling shareholder or group of shareholders. As the responsibility borne by the adviser is considerable, the board of the offeree company or potential offeree company should appoint an independent adviser as soon as possible after it becomes aware that such an offer may be made.'\n\nIn an MBO, the people who know the business best are on the buyer's side. The independent directors and shareholders are at an information disadvantage, and the adviser is their main counterweight. Appointing early lets the adviser understand the business before negotiations set the terms. Note 5 allows the board to share available management projections and forecasts with the adviser. Under Rule 9.2 Note 1, information given to the MBO's financiers must be made available to any competing bona fide offeror."},
    {t:"Once a firm intention to make the offer is announced", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 calls for an appointment 'as soon as possible after it becomes aware that such an offer may be made'. Waiting for an announcement would leave the board without independent advice during the negotiations that shape the deal."},
    {t:"Once the offer document has been posted to the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"That is far too late for an MBO. By then the terms are fixed and the adviser would have little time to analyse a business that management knows intimately."},
    {t:"Only if the CEO and finance director are directors", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 applies to 'a management buy-out or similar transaction' generally. Whether the managers are directors doesn't change the information imbalance the Note addresses."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.1, Notes 1, 5; r 9.2, Note 1", takeaway:"MBO: appoint the IFA early." },

{ id:"tc3-018", lesson:"TC3C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An offeree board must obtain independent advice on a partial offer that could take the offeror and its concert parties from 10% to 25% of the voting rights.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 7.1: 'The requirement for competent independent advice does not apply to partial offers which could not result in the offeror and persons acting in concert with it holding shares carrying 30% or more of the voting rights of the offeree company.' A partial offer from 10% to 25% can't reach 30%, so Rule 7.1 doesn't require advice.\n\nThe reason ties back to effective control. Independent advice matters most when control may change hands. A partial offer that leaves the offeror below 30% doesn't give it effective control, and shareholders who don't accept keep their position in a company whose control hasn't shifted. If the partial offer could take the group to 30% or more, the exemption wouldn't apply. The board is still free to take advice voluntarily, and GP 8 encourages it."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 disapplies Rule 7.1 for partial offers that could not result in the offeror and its concert parties holding 30% or more. 25% is below that line."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.1, Note 4", takeaway:"Partial offer below 30%: no IFA needed." },

{ id:"tc3-019", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Vantor Ltd, incorporated in Singapore, plans an offer for the larger Meridian Ltd, paid for in new Vantor shares on a scale that makes it a reverse take-over.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 7.2 require of Vantor's board?",
  options:[
    {t:"Get independent advice, before announcing, on whether it serves Vantor's shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 7.2: 'The board of a Singapore-incorporated offeror must obtain competent independent advice on an offer where the offer being made is a reverse take-over. The board ... must also obtain competent independent advice when it faces a material conflict of interests. The board must make known the substance of the advice obtained to its shareholders.'\n\nNote 1 adds: the advice 'should do so before announcing an offer or any revised offer'; it 'should be concerned with whether or not the making of the offer is in the interests of the company's shareholders'; 'Shareholders must have sufficient time to consider advice given to them prior to any general meeting held to implement the proposed offer'; and documents must include a directors' responsibility statement.\n\nIn a reverse take-over, Vantor's shareholders end up controlled by Meridian's owners, so they are effectively the ones being taken over. That's why they need independent advice too."},
    {t:"Nothing: Rule 7 applies only to offeree boards, not to offerors", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 7.2 is specifically about offeror boards. A Singapore-incorporated offeror needs independent advice for a reverse take-over or a material conflict of interests."},
    {t:"Obtain advice on whether the offer is fair and reasonable to Meridian's shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The offeror's advice addresses 'whether or not the making of the offer is in the interests of the company's shareholders', meaning Vantor's own shareholders. Meridian's board gets its own advice under Rule 7.1."},
    {t:"Obtain advice, but only after the offer document has been posted", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says the offeror's board should obtain the advice 'before announcing an offer or any revised offer'. Advice after posting would come too late to shape the decision to bid."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.2; r 7.2, Note 1; Defn 20", takeaway:"Reverse take-over: the offeror needs an IFA too." },

{ id:"tc3x-042", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Dunlin Ltd, a Singapore company, plans an ordinary cash offer for Egret Ltd (not a reverse take-over). Three of Dunlin's six directors also sit on Egret's board, and Dunlin and Egret each hold about 15% of the other.",
  prompt:"Does Dunlin's board need independent advice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it has a material conflict, such as cross-holdings and common directors", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 7.2 requires a Singapore-incorporated offeror's board to obtain competent independent advice not only for a reverse take-over but 'when it faces a material conflict of interests'. Note 2 gives examples: 'A conflict of interests will exist, for instance, where there are significant cross-shareholdings between an offeror and the offeree company, where there are a number of directors common to both companies, or where a common substantial shareholder in both companies is a director of or has a nominee director in either company.'\n\nDunlin has two of these: 15% cross-holdings each way, and three common directors. Those directors can't advise Dunlin's shareholders objectively on whether bidding for Egret is in Dunlin's interests. The board must get independent advice before announcing (Note 1), and make its substance known. The common directors also face conflicts on Egret's side, where they would normally be exempted from Egret's recommendation and where condition (c) of the Note on Defn 12 would apply if the deal were done by scheme."},
    {t:"No: Rule 7.2 applies only to reverse take-overs, and this isn't one", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 7.2 has two limbs: reverse take-overs, and material conflicts of interests. This offer isn't a reverse take-over, but the conflict limb applies."},
    {t:"No: only Egret's board, as the offeree board, needs independent advice on the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Egret needs advice under Rule 7.1, but Dunlin's own board needs it too because of the material conflict. The two sets of shareholders have different interests."},
    {t:"Only if Dunlin's own shareholders must vote on the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 7.2 doesn't depend on a shareholder vote. The conflict itself triggers the requirement, though Note 1 says that where a general meeting is held, shareholders need enough time to consider the advice."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.2, Notes 1–2", takeaway:"Cross-holdings or common directors → offeror IFA." },

{ id:"tc3-020", lesson:"TC3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which firm would the Council not normally accept as independent adviser to the offeree board?",
  options:[
    {t:"A firm in the same group as the stockbroker acting for the offeror", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 7.3: 'The Council would not normally regard as an appropriate person to give competent independent advice a person who is in the same group as the financial or professional adviser (including a stockbroker) to the offeror or who has a substantial interest in or financial connection with either the offeror or the offeree company of such a kind as to create a conflict of interests for that person.'\n\nA firm in the same group as the offeror's stockbroker has an obvious group interest in the offer succeeding, which undermines objectivity. Note 1 explains the purpose: the offeree's adviser must have 'a sufficient degree of independence from the offeror to ensure that the advice given is properly objective'.\n\nBy contrast, Note 2 says that 'When an adviser has been actively advising a company which becomes an offeree company, it may be acceptable for it to continue to act for the offeree company.' A fixed fee doesn't create the conflict that an outcome-linked fee can (Note 3)."},
    {t:"A firm that has been actively advising the offeree before the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says it 'may be acceptable' for a firm that has actively advised a company which becomes an offeree to continue acting for it. Its prior knowledge of the offeree is an advantage, not a conflict."},
    {t:"A firm on a fixed fee payable whatever the outcome", correct:false,
     why:"A fixed fee doesn't give the adviser a stake in the result. Note 3 is concerned with fees whose payment depends on the outcome, such as a fee payable only if the offer fails."},
    {t:"A firm with no prior relationship with either party", correct:false,
     why:"That is the clearest case of independence. Rule 7.3 targets group links to the offeror's advisers and conflicting interests or connections, none of which this firm has."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3; r 7.3, Notes 1–3", takeaway:"Offeror's adviser's group can't be the IFA." },

{ id:"tc3x-043", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Finch Capital advised Gull Ltd last year on a debt restructuring and still holds detailed confidential information about Gull's business. Now an offeror wants to hire Finch to advise on a bid for Gull.",
  prompt:"What must Finch do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only if it judges its past work gives no advantage, after consulting the Council", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 7.3: 'When the adviser had previous dealings (other than the provision of credit facilities on an arm's length basis) with the offeree company, the adviser may act for the offeror only if the adviser, in its professional judgment, is satisfied that its previous dealings with the offeree company would not give the offeror an advantage in its take-over offer for the offeree company (i.e. the information the adviser possesses on the offeree company from its previous dealings is no longer material or relevant in the context of the offer). The adviser must consult the Council and be ready to justify the basis of its judgment to the Council before acting for the offeror. Where appropriate, the Council may take into account the views of the offeree company'.\n\nFinch's work was recent and it still holds detailed confidential information. It would struggle to say that information is no longer material. The Note also warns that such a conflict 'may not be possible to resolve ... simply by isolating information within the relevant organisation or by assigning different personnel'. Finch may well have to decline."},
    {t:"Act freely, as long as different staff work on the bid team", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 warns that conflicts may not be resolved 'simply by isolating information ... or by assigning different personnel'. And Finch must consult the Council and justify its judgment before acting."},
    {t:"Act, provided Gull's board consents to it switching sides", correct:false,
     why:"The Council may take the offeree's views into account, but Gull's consent isn't the test. Finch must be satisfied its information gives no advantage, and must justify that to the Council."},
    {t:"Act, because restructuring advice isn't take-over advice under the Code", correct:false,
     why:"The type of earlier work doesn't matter. The question is whether the confidential information Finch holds is still material in the context of the offer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 2", takeaway:"Old target client? Justify to the Council first." },

{ id:"tc3x-044", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Heron Advisory is proposed as independent adviser to Ibis Ltd's board. Eight months ago, Heron advised the offeror on an unrelated acquisition. Ibis's independent directors want Heron, saying it knows their sector best.",
  prompt:"How will the Council approach this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its recent work may make Heron inappropriate; consult the Council, which weighs the board's views", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 7.3: 'The Rule requires the offeree company's adviser to have a sufficient degree of independence from the offeror to ensure that the advice given is properly objective. Accordingly, in certain circumstances it may not be appropriate for a person who has had a recent advisory relationship with an offeror to give advice to the offeree company. In such cases the Council should be consulted. The views of the board of the offeree company will be an important factor in the Council's consideration whether such a person should advise the offeree company.'\n\nHeron's work for the offeror eight months ago is a recent advisory relationship. That doesn't automatically disqualify it, but it raises the question of objectivity. The Council will look at the facts, including how close and significant the relationship was. The independent directors' view that Heron is the best adviser for them is an 'important factor'. They are the ones relying on the advice. The final decision is the Council's."},
    {t:"Heron is automatically disqualified from advising the offeree", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says a recent relationship 'may not be appropriate' in certain circumstances. It isn't an automatic bar, and the Council decides after consultation, giving weight to the offeree board's views."},
    {t:"Heron can act without consulting anyone, since the earlier work was entirely unrelated to the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says that in such cases 'the Council should be consulted'. The independence question isn't for Heron or the board to settle alone."},
    {t:"The offeror's consent alone decides whether Heron may act", correct:false,
     why:"The offeror's view isn't mentioned in Note 1. The relevant views are those of the offeree board, who will rely on the advice."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 1", takeaway:"Recent offeror work: consult; offeree board's view counts." },

{ id:"tc3-021", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ashby's board proposes to pay its independent adviser a modest retainer plus a large bonus payable only if the offer fails.",
  prompt:"What is the Code position?",
  options:[
    {t:"It would normally create a conflict that disqualifies the adviser", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 7.3: 'Certain fee arrangements between an adviser and the offeree company may create a conflict of interests which would disqualify the adviser as an independent adviser to the offeree company. For example, a fee which becomes payable to the adviser only if the offer fails will normally create such a conflict of interests. In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'\n\nAn adviser paid mostly for the offer failing has a financial reason to advise rejection. Shareholders couldn't trust that its view on fairness was objective. The arrangement fails the core test of independence in Note 1. The board should restructure the fee so that it doesn't depend on the outcome. Since 2026, Rule 8.8 also requires the offeree to disclose to all offerors an estimate of its aggregate fees and expenses, including financial advisory fees, and to describe the nature of any variable or outcome-related element."},
    {t:"Fine, as long as the bonus is disclosed in the circular", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure lets shareholders see the conflict, but it doesn't remove it. Note 3 says such a fee 'will normally create' a disqualifying conflict, so the adviser would no longer be independent."},
    {t:"Fine, since the adviser works for the offeree company, not the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"The adviser works for the offeree board, but its job is to give shareholders objective advice. A fee that rewards the offer failing gives it a stake in the outcome."},
    {t:"A problem only if the bonus exceeds the retainer", correct:false,
     why:"There's no ratio test in Note 3. What creates the conflict is a fee 'which becomes payable ... only if the offer fails', whatever its size compared with the retainer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 3; r 8.8", takeaway:"Paid only if the bid fails: not independent." },

{ id:"tc3-022", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Holloway Capital is proposed as independent adviser to the offeree board. Its lending arm provides a small revolving facility to a company acting in concert with the offeror.",
  prompt:"Can Holloway act?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult: normally yes if the link isn't material and the independent directors confirm in writing", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 7.3: 'Where a financial adviser proposing to act for the offeree company has a financial connection with the offeror or persons acting in concert with the offeror or the offeree company or persons acting in concert with the offeree company, the adviser should consult the Council. The Council would normally allow the financial adviser to act for the offeree company if: (a) the financial connection is not material enough to create a conflict of interests; and (b) the independent directors of the offeree company confirm in writing to the Council that, in their opinion, it is appropriate to appoint such financial adviser to render independent advice on the take-over offer.'\n\nHolloway's lending to a concert party of the offeror is a financial connection, so consultation is required. A small revolving facility may well not be material. If so, and the independent directors give written confirmation, the Council would normally allow the appointment. Both conditions are needed."},
    {t:"Never: any financial connection with the offeror at all disqualifies the adviser from acting", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 allows a financial connection that isn't material enough to create a conflict, provided the independent directors confirm in writing. It isn't an absolute bar."},
    {t:"Yes, without consulting anyone, since the connection is a small one", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says the adviser 'should consult the Council' where it has a financial connection with the offeror or its concert parties. It can't decide the question alone."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as the offeror consents to Holloway acting", correct:false,
     why:"The written confirmation must come from the offeree's independent directors, the people relying on the advice. The offeror's consent isn't part of the test."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 2", takeaway:"Small connection: consult; independent directors confirm in writing." },

{ id:"tc3x-045", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Jacana Bank's corporate finance team advises the offeror in a bid for Kite Ltd. During the offer, Jacana's fund management arm buys Kite shares for its discretionary funds. Jacana says the two businesses are run separately.",
  prompt:"What does Note 2 on Rule 7.3 expect of Jacana?",
  options:[
    {t:"Satisfy the Council that its fund operations are totally and effectively segregated", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 says it is 'incumbent upon multi-service financial organisations to familiarise themselves with the implications under the Code of conducting other businesses'. If one part advises on an offer, 'a number of Rules of the Code may be relevant to other parts of that organisation, whose actions may have serious consequences under the Code'.\n\nIt continues: 'It may be a fact that corporate finance, stockbroking, fund management and corporate advisory activities may be conducted on a day-to-day basis quite separately within the same organisation, but it is necessary for such organisation to satisfy the Council that it arranges its affairs to ensure that there is total and effective segregation of those operations, and those operations are conducted without regard for the interests of other parts of the same organisation or of its clients.' Compliance departments 'have an important role' and should liaise with the Council in cases of doubt.\n\nThe stakes are high. Presumption (e) on acting in concert links an adviser's group holdings with its client, and associate category (c) covers the adviser's group. Unless the segregation is real, the fund arm's purchases could count with the offeror's."},
    {t:"Nothing: the fund arm is a separate business, so the Code ignores it", correct:false,
     why:"Being a separate business isn't enough. Jacana must satisfy the Council that there is 'total and effective segregation' and that each part acts without regard for the others' interests."},
    {t:"It must stop all of its fund management activity for the rest of the offer", correct:false,
     why:"The Note doesn't require a shutdown. It requires real segregation, demonstrated to the Council, and attention to the Code's rules on concert parties and associates."},
    {t:"Only the corporate finance team is bound by the Code, not the fund managers in the group", correct:false,
     why:"The Note says the Code's Rules 'may be relevant to other parts of that organisation'. The fund arm's dealings can have Code consequences because of its group link to the offeror's adviser."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 2; Defn 1(e); Defn 2(c)", takeaway:"Multi-service firms: prove total segregation." },

{ id:"tc3-023", lesson:"TC3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a contest for Ashby Ltd, Holloway Capital advised the first offeror until it was replaced. The rival offeror now wants to hire Holloway for the same contest.",
  prompt:"Is that acceptable?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: an adviser can't switch to a rival offeror in the same offer", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 7.3: 'In the case of a competitive offer, it is not acceptable for an adviser to act for more than one of the competing offerors in relation to the offer at any point in time. It is also not acceptable for an adviser who was acting, or had previously acted, for one offeror in a competitive offer to subsequently switch to acting for a competing offeror in relation to the same offer. This restriction does not apply to the provision of credit facilities to an offeror on an arm's length basis.'\n\nHolloway knows the first offeror's strategy, pricing limits and financing in this very contest. Moving to the rival would carry that knowledge across, whatever internal barriers were put up. The ban covers advisers 'who was acting, or had previously acted', so the fact that Holloway was replaced doesn't help. The only carve-out is arm's-length lending, which is ordinary commercial banking rather than advice."},
    {t:"Yes, once effective information barriers are in place", correct:false,
     why:"The rule is a flat prohibition on switching in the same contest. Note 2 also warns more generally that conflicts may not be resolved 'simply by isolating information within the relevant organisation or by assigning different personnel'."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as the first offeror consents to the switch", correct:false,
     why:"The prohibition doesn't have a consent exception. It protects the integrity of the contest and the offeree's shareholders, not just the first offeror."},
    {t:"Yes, because its mandate for the first offeror has now come to an end", correct:false,
     why:"The rule expressly covers an adviser 'who was acting, or had previously acted'. An ended mandate doesn't erase what Holloway learned."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 7.3, Note 2", takeaway:"No switching sides in a contest." }

]);

;
