/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T4 · Lesson TC4C Break Fees (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code Rule 13 and Notes 1–7 (r 13(e) 'and why'; Notes 5–7 new 2026). Replaces TC4C in takeoverCode4.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc4-020", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the normal maximum for a single break fee under Rule 13?",
  options:[
    {t:"1% of the offeree's value, calculated by reference to the offer price", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 13: 'In all cases where a break fee is proposed, certain safeguards must be observed. In particular, a break fee must be minimal (normally no more than 1% of the value of the offeree company calculated by reference to the offer price) and the offeree company board and its financial adviser must provide, in writing, to the Council' five confirmations and explanations (items (a) to (e)).\n\nThe Rule continues: 'Any break fee arrangement must be fully disclosed in the announcement made under Rule 3 and in the offer document. Relevant documents must be made available for inspection. The Council should be consulted at the earliest opportunity in all cases where a break fee or any similar arrangement is proposed.'\n\nA break fee compensates an offeror for its costs if the deal fails, and can encourage a bidder to come forward. But a large fee is paid out of the offeree's money and can deter rival bids, which hurts shareholders. The 1% cap keeps the fee small enough that it doesn't decide the contest."},
    {t:"1% of the offeree's market capitalisation before the offer was announced", correct:false,
     why:"The reference is 'the offer price', so the bid value, not the pre-bid market value. The pre-announcement value is used only for whitewash inducement fees (Note 3)."},
    {t:"1% of the offeror's own value at the announcement date", correct:false,
     why:"The fee is paid by the offeree, so the cap is measured against 'the value of the offeree company'. The offeror's size is irrelevant."},
    {t:"2% of the offeree's value, to cover the offeror's costs", correct:false,
     why:"The normal maximum is 1%. The Code says the fee must be 'minimal'. It isn't designed to cover every cost the offeror might incur."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13", takeaway:"Minimal means 1% at the offer price." },

{ id:"tc4x-040", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"An offeree agrees to pay an offeror $3m if the offeree board withdraws its recommendation of the offer. Which feature makes this a 'break fee' under Note 1 on Rule 13?",
  options:[
    {t:"A cash sum payable by the offeree if set events stop or defeat the offer", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 13: 'A break fee is an arrangement which may be entered into between an offeror or a potential offeror and the offeree company pursuant to which a cash sum will be payable by the offeree company if certain specified events occur which have the effect of preventing the offer from proceeding or causing it to fail (e.g. the recommendation by the offeree company board of a higher competing offer).'\n\nA board withdrawing its recommendation is the kind of event that can cause an offer to fail, so a fee triggered by it is a break fee. Three features define the arrangement: it is between the offeror (or potential offeror) and the offeree; it is a cash sum payable by the offeree; and it is triggered by events that stop the offer or cause it to fail.\n\nNote 1 extends the Rule to 'any other favourable arrangements with an offeror or potential offeror which have a similar or comparable financial or economic effect, even if such arrangements do not actually involve any cash payment', including 'penalties, put or call options or other provisions having similar effects'. The cap, written confirmations, disclosure and Council consultation all apply."},
    {t:"That it is agreed only after the offer has been publicly announced by the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"Timing doesn't define a break fee. Most break fees are agreed before or at the firm announcement, which is why Rule 13 requires disclosure in the Rule 3 announcement."},
    {t:"That it is payable by the offeror to the offeree company", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 describes a sum 'payable by the offeree company'. Here the offeree pays the offeror."},
    {t:"That it is more than 1% of the offeree's value", correct:false,
     why:"The 1% figure is the normal cap, not part of the definition. A fee is a break fee whatever its size, and then must normally be no more than 1%."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 1", takeaway:"Offeree pays if the deal is blocked or fails." },

{ id:"tc4-021", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Instead of a cash break fee, Ashby Ltd agrees to give Corran a call option over its best-performing subsidiary at book value, exercisable if Ashby's board recommends a rival offer. Ashby says the option is an ordinary-course commercial arrangement.",
  prompt:"Does Rule 13 apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it has comparable economic effect, even though it is in the ordinary course", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 13: 'This Rule will also apply to any other favourable arrangements with an offeror or potential offeror which have a similar or comparable financial or economic effect, even if such arrangements do not actually involve any cash payment. Such arrangements also include, but are not limited to, penalties, put or call options or other provisions having similar effects, regardless of whether such arrangements are considered to be in the ordinary course of business. In cases of doubt, the Council should be consulted.'\n\nA call option over the best subsidiary at book value, exercisable if the board recommends a rival, is a classic 'crown jewel lock-up'. If the rival wins, Corran takes a valuable asset cheaply, which compensates it far more than a 1% cash fee and could deter any rival from bidding at all. That's a comparable economic effect, and arguably a much bigger one.\n\nThe words 'regardless of whether such arrangements are considered to be in the ordinary course of business' rule out Ashby's argument. The option would be measured against the 1% cap at its economic value, and would also raise frustrating-action issues under Rule 5."},
    {t:"No: no cash is payable under the arrangement, so it isn't a fee", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says the Rule applies 'even if such arrangements do not actually involve any cash payment'. Options are named expressly."},
    {t:"No: arrangements made in the ordinary course of business are excluded from Rule 13", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says the Rule applies 'regardless of whether such arrangements are considered to be in the ordinary course of business'."},
    {t:"Only if the option is actually exercised by the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 applies when the arrangement is proposed. The Council should be consulted 'at the earliest opportunity in all cases where a break fee or any similar arrangement is proposed'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 1; r 5", takeaway:"Options and penalties are break fees too." },

{ id:"tc4-022", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ibis Ltd has 600m shares in issue, 30m options exercisable at $0.90 and 20m options exercisable at $1.60. It has no other convertible securities. Corran offers $1.20 a share.",
  prompt:"Using the fully diluted basis Note 4 allows, what is the highest break fee within the normal 1% limit?",
  options:[
    {t:"$7.29m", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 13: 'The 1% limit can be calculated on the basis of the fully diluted equity share capital of the offeree company. In this regard, only options and warrants which are \"in the money\" may be included in the calculation. When determining the value of the fully diluted share capital, the value to be attributed to such warrants and options is their \"see-through\" value, taking into account the offer price for the relevant shares and any exercise price.'\n\nStep by step:\n• Shares in issue: 600m × $1.20 = $720m.\n• $0.90 options: in the money (exercise price below the $1.20 offer price). See-through value = 30m × ($1.20 − $0.90) = 30m × $0.30 = $9m.\n• $1.60 options: out of the money (exercise price above $1.20). Excluded.\n• Fully diluted value: $720m + $9m = $729m. 1% = $7.29m.\n\nThe see-through method reflects what the options are actually worth at the offer price: their holders would pay $0.90 to receive $1.20. Note 4 also says taxes payable as a result of the fee count towards the limit, unless the offeree can recover them."},
    {t:"$7.20m", correct:false,
     why:"That uses issued shares only (600m × $1.20 × 1%). Note 4 allows in-the-money options to be included on a fully diluted basis, which adds $9m to the value."},
    {t:"$7.56m", correct:false,
     why:"That values the 30m $0.90 options at the full offer price ($36m). Note 4 uses 'see-through' value: offer price less exercise price, so $0.30 each, or $9m."},
    {t:"$7.80m", correct:false,
     why:"That includes the $1.60 options. They are out of the money at a $1.20 offer, and Note 4 says 'only options and warrants which are \"in the money\" may be included'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 4", takeaway:"Fully diluted: in-the-money options at see-through value." },

{ id:"tc4x-041", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Jay Ltd has 200m shares and convertible bonds convertible into 10m shares. Kite Ltd offers $2.00 a share. The parties propose a $4.0m break fee, on which Jay would pay a 9% tax that it can't recover.",
  prompt:"Is the fee within the normal 1% limit?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the cap is 1% of $420m fully diluted, $4.2m; fee plus tax is $4.36m", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 13 gives the method.\n\nValue of the offeree: shares 200m × $2.00 = $400m. Convertibles: 'The value attributable to convertible securities is the offer price multiplied by the conversion ratio', so 10m shares × $2.00 = $20m. Fully diluted value: $420m. 1% cap: $4.2m.\n\nTaxes: 'Any taxes payable as a result should be taken into account in determining whether the 1% limit would be exceeded (except to the extent that such taxes is recoverable by the offeree company).' A 9% tax on $4.0m is $0.36m, and Jay can't recover it. The total cost to Jay is $4.36m, which exceeds $4.2m.\n\nSo the fee as proposed is over the normal limit. To fit, the fee would need to be reduced so that fee plus non-recoverable tax is no more than $4.2m: roughly $3.85m. The logic of counting tax is that the cap protects the offeree's shareholders, and non-recoverable tax is money that leaves the company just as the fee does."},
    {t:"Yes: $4.0m is below the $4.2m cap", correct:false,
     why:"That ignores the tax. Note 4 says non-recoverable taxes payable as a result must be taken into account. With them, Jay's cost is $4.36m."},
    {t:"Yes: the cap is $4.36m including the bonds at face value", correct:false,
     why:"Convertibles are valued at 'the offer price multiplied by the conversion ratio', so 10m × $2.00 = $20m, not face value. The cap is $4.2m."},
    {t:"No: convertibles can't be counted, so the cap is only $4.0m and is exceeded", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 allows the fully diluted basis, including convertible securities at the offer price times the conversion ratio. The cap is $4.2m. The fee fails only because of the tax."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 4", takeaway:"Convertibles at offer price × ratio; count non-recoverable tax." },

{ id:"tc4-023", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Vantor's share-for-share offer for Meridian was worth $500m when announced. Vantor's share price then rises 20%. For Rule 13, what is Meridian's value?",
  options:[
    {t:"$500m, so the normal cap is $5m", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 13: 'In the case of a securities exchange offer, the value of the offeree company for these purposes will be fixed by reference to the value of the offer at the time of announcement of the transaction (and will not fluctuate as a result of subsequent movements in the price of the consideration securities after announcement).'\n\nThe offer was worth $500m at announcement, so Meridian's value for Rule 13 is $500m and the normal cap is $5m. The 20% rise in Vantor's share price doesn't change it.\n\nFixing the value gives certainty. The parties need to know at signing whether the fee is within the cap. If the value floated with the offeror's share price, a fee agreed within 1% could later breach the cap, or a fee that was too large could later become acceptable. Note 4 also says convertibles are valued at the offer price multiplied by the conversion ratio, and in-the-money options and warrants at see-through value."},
    {t:"$600m, so the normal cap is $6m", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 says the value 'will not fluctuate as a result of subsequent movements in the price of the consideration securities after announcement'. The 20% rise is ignored."},
    {t:"Meridian's market capitalisation before the offer", correct:false,
     why:"The reference is 'the value of the offer at the time of announcement'. Pre-offer market value is used only for whitewash inducement fees under Note 3."},
    {t:"Whichever is higher when the fee becomes payable", correct:false,
     why:"The value is fixed at announcement. Measuring it when the fee becomes payable would make the cap uncertain when the fee is agreed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 4", takeaway:"Paper offer: value fixed at announcement." },

{ id:"tc4-024", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"An inducement fee is proposed in a whitewash transaction. What is the 1% measured against?",
  options:[
    {t:"The offeree's value just before the whitewash transaction is announced", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 13, headed 'Whitewashes': 'This Rule also applies to the payment of an inducement fee in the context of a \"whitewash\" transaction. In this context, the 1% test will normally be calculated by reference to the value of the offeree company immediately prior to the announcement of the proposed \"whitewash\" transaction.'\n\nIn a whitewash, independent shareholders vote to waive a general offer that would otherwise arise, for example when a new investor subscribes for shares that take it over 30% (TC5C, TC8D). There's no offer price to measure against, because no offer is being made. So the Code uses the company's value just before the transaction is announced.\n\nThe same concerns apply. An inducement fee to a subscriber is paid out of company funds, and if large, could pressure shareholders to approve the whitewash or deter alternatives. The safeguards in Rule 13 (minimal fee, written confirmations, disclosure, early consultation with the Council) apply in full."},
    {t:"The total subscription price of the new shares being issued", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 uses 'the value of the offeree company immediately prior to the announcement'. The subscription price for the new shares isn't the reference."},
    {t:"The company's value after the new shares have been issued to the subscriber", correct:false,
     why:"The reference point is before the announcement. Post-issue value would be affected by the transaction itself."},
    {t:"Nothing: fees in whitewash transactions aren't capped at all", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 says 'This Rule also applies to the payment of an inducement fee in the context of a \"whitewash\" transaction'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 3", takeaway:"Whitewash: 1% of pre-announcement value." },

{ id:"tc4x-042", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The Council confirms that a proposed 0.9% break fee is acceptable under Rule 13. The offeree's directors then say they need not consider whether the fee is consistent with their duties under the Companies Act.",
  prompt:"What does Note 2 on Rule 13 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Council views relate only to the Code, not to any other law or regulation", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 13, headed 'Statutory provisions': 'Any view expressed by the Council in relation to such fees or arrangements can only relate to the Code and must not be taken to extend to any other relevant law or regulation.'\n\nA break fee is a payment of company money, so it raises questions under the general law, such as directors' fiduciary duties, any restrictions on a company giving financial assistance for the acquisition of its own shares, and the company's constitution. The Council administers the Code, which 'does not have the force of law', and its duty is 'the enforcement of good business standards and not the enforcement of law' (Introduction §§1–2). It can't clear an arrangement under statute.\n\nThe directors must still take their own legal advice. Rule 13(e) also requires them and their financial adviser to confirm they believe the fee is in shareholders' best interests, and since 2026, to explain why. That matches the analysis their legal duties demand."},
    {t:"Council clearance of the fee covers all the legal issues it raises", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says the opposite: the Council's view 'can only relate to the Code and must not be taken to extend to any other relevant law or regulation'."},
    {t:"Council clearance of the fee overrides the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"The Code is non-statutory and can't override legislation. The Council's view on a break fee is about Code compliance only."},
    {t:"The Council will refer the fee to ACRA for legal approval", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 doesn't involve any referral for legal approval. The parties must take their own advice on other laws."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 2; Intro §§1–2", takeaway:"Council clearance is Code-only." },

{ id:"tc4-025", lesson:"TC4C", type:"match", amended:true,
  prompt:"Match each item the offeree board and its financial adviser must provide to the Council in writing to what it covers.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Rule 13(a) confirmation", r:"Agreed through normal commercial negotiations"},
    {l:"Rule 13(b) explanation", r:"Basis, appropriateness and when the fee becomes payable"},
    {l:"Rule 13(c) information", r:"Possible competing offerors: status, terms, pre-conditions, timing"},
    {l:"Rule 13(d) confirmation", r:"All other related agreements or understandings fully disclosed"},
    {l:"Rule 13(e) confirmation", r:"Each believes it is in shareholders' best interests, and why"}
  ],
  why:"Rule 13 requires the offeree board and its financial adviser to provide, in writing, to the Council:\n'(a) a confirmation that the break fee arrangements were agreed as a result of normal commercial negotiations;\n(b) an explanation of the basis (including appropriateness) and the circumstances in which the break fee becomes payable;\n(c) any relevant information concerning possible competing offerors, e.g. the status of any discussions, the possible terms, any pre-conditions to the making of an offer, the timing of any such offer etc.;\n(d) a confirmation that all other agreements or understandings in relation to the break fees arrangements have been fully disclosed; and\n(e) a confirmation they each believe the fee to be in the best interests of offeree company shareholders, and an explanation as to why that is so.'\n\nThe words 'and an explanation as to why that is so' were added in 2026. Item (c) is especially important. If a rival is in the wings, a break fee can tip the balance against it, and the Council needs to know that before it accepts the fee as minimal and appropriate.",
  cite:"SG Code r 13(a)–(e)", takeaway:"Five items, now with reasons." },

{ id:"tc4-026", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Wren Ltd's board and financial adviser write to the Council about a 0.8% break fee agreed with Kestrel. Item (e) of the letter reads: 'The board and its financial adviser each believe the break fee to be in the best interests of Wren shareholders.'",
  prompt:"Does item (e) meet the amended Rule 13?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: it must also explain why the fee is in shareholders' interests", correct:true,
     why:"Before 2026, Rule 13(e) required only 'a confirmation they each believe the fee to be in the best interests of offeree company shareholders'. The 2026 amendment added ', and an explanation as to why that is so'.\n\nWren's letter states the belief but gives no reason. Under the amended Rule, that isn't enough. A good explanation might cover how the fee helped secure the offer (for example, the bidder wouldn't proceed without it), how the fee compares with the offeror's likely costs, why it won't deter competing offers (perhaps because the fee is small and the offeree has a fiduciary out and short matching rights), and the price and certainty the offer brings.\n\nThe change makes the board and its adviser think the question through and put their reasoning on record. A bare statement of belief invites box-ticking. An explanation can be tested by the Council."},
    {t:"Yes: the fee is under 1%, so nothing more is needed", correct:false,
     why:"The size of the fee is a separate requirement. The written confirmations in items (a) to (e) are required for every break fee, however small."},
    {t:"Yes, if the financial adviser signs a separate letter", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 is satisfied by the board and adviser together providing the items. A separate letter with the same bare wording would still lack the explanation."},
    {t:"No: it must also attach a fairness opinion on the offer itself to the letter", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 doesn't require a fairness opinion on the offer. Rule 7.1 deals with independent advice on the offer. What item (e) lacks is the explanation of why the fee is in shareholders' best interests."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13(e)", takeaway:"Believing it isn't enough: say why." },

{ id:"tc4x-043", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A break fee has been agreed. Where must it be disclosed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Fully in the Rule 3 announcement and offer document, open for inspection", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 13: 'Any break fee arrangement must be fully disclosed in the announcement made under Rule 3 and in the offer document. Relevant documents must be made available for inspection. The Council should be consulted at the earliest opportunity in all cases where a break fee or any similar arrangement is proposed.'\n\nDisclosure matters to two audiences. Shareholders need to know that their company has agreed to pay money if the deal fails, because it affects their assessment of the offer and of any rival. Potential competing offerors need to know too, since a rival effectively has to beat the offer by more than the fee. Rule 3.5(e) separately requires the firm-intention announcement to disclose material arrangements in relation to offeror or offeree shares.\n\n'Relevant documents' (typically the implementation agreement containing the fee) must be available for inspection, so the market can see the actual terms. Since 2026, Note 6 regulates other terms of implementation agreements too: exclusivity, notification and matching rights."},
    {t:"Only privately to the Council, which keeps it confidential", correct:false,
     why:"The Council receives the written confirmations under items (a) to (e), but Rule 13 also requires full public disclosure in the Rule 3 announcement and the offer document."},
    {t:"Only in the offeree board circular, once it is despatched", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 13 names the Rule 3 announcement and the offer document. The market needs to know from the start, not only once the board circular comes out."},
    {t:"Only if and when the fee actually becomes payable to the offeror, by announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is required when the arrangement is agreed. Its deterrent effect on rivals exists from the moment it's signed, whether or not it is ever paid."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13; r 3.5(e)", takeaway:"Disclose in announcement and offer doc; documents on display." },

{ id:"tc4-027", lesson:"TC4C", type:"tf", amended:true,
  prompt:"True or false: Under the amended Code, an offeree facing two offerors may agree a break fee of up to 1% with each.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"New Note 5 on Rule 13, headed 'Competing offerors': 'If the offeree company agrees to more than one break fee, the aggregate value of all break fees that may be payable by the offeree company must be minimal, i.e. normally no more than 1% of the value of the offeree company calculated by reference to the price of the first competing offer at the time of the announcement made under Rule 3.5. Any break fee is capable of becoming payable only if an offer becomes or is declared unconditional.'\n\nBefore 2026, each offeror could argue for its own 1%. Two or three fees together could add up to a significant drain on the offeree. The amendment applies a single 1% cap to all break fees combined.\n\nThe cap is measured at the price of the first competing offer at its Rule 3.5 announcement. That will usually be higher than the original offer, so the cap rises a little when a rival appears, but it then stays fixed, however many further bidders arrive."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5, added in 2026, caps 'the aggregate value of all break fees' at normally no more than 1%. Each offeror doesn't get its own 1%."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 5", takeaway:"One 1% for everyone." },

{ id:"tc4-028", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Heron Ltd has 500m shares. In March it agreed a $9m break fee with Kestrel Ltd, whose offer is $2.00 a share. In May, Osprey Ltd announces a firm intention under Rule 3.5 to make a competing offer at $2.40 a share, and asks Heron for a break fee.",
  prompt:"What is the most Heron can normally agree to pay Osprey?",
  options:[
    {t:"$3m", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 caps the aggregate of all break fees at normally no more than 1% of the offeree's value 'calculated by reference to the price of the first competing offer at the time of the announcement made under Rule 3.5'.\n\nStep by step:\n• Osprey's is the first competing offer, at $2.40.\n• Heron's value at that price: 500m × $2.40 = $1.2bn.\n• Aggregate cap: 1% of $1.2bn = $12m.\n• Already committed: Kestrel's $9m.\n• Headroom for Osprey: $12m − $9m = $3m.\n\nKestrel's fee was within its own limit when agreed: 1% of 500m × $2.00 is $10m, and $9m is less than that. But once there are two fees, the aggregate cap governs. Heron's board should consult the Council and provide the Rule 13 written confirmations for Osprey's fee, including the explanation under item (e)."},
    {t:"$12m", correct:false,
     why:"That gives Osprey its own full 1%. Note 5 caps the aggregate of all break fees, so Kestrel's $9m must be deducted."},
    {t:"$1m", correct:false,
     why:"That measures the cap at Kestrel's $2.00 price ($10m − $9m). Note 5 uses the price of the first competing offer, Osprey's $2.40."},
    {t:"Nothing: an offeree may agree only one break fee", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 contemplates more than one break fee: 'If the offeree company agrees to more than one break fee'. They must fit within the aggregate cap."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 5", takeaway:"Cap at the competing price, minus what's committed." },

{ id:"tc4-029", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Rowan Ltd has 300m shares. It agreed a $5.4m break fee with Bidder A, whose offer is $1.80. Bidder B then announced a competing firm offer under Rule 3.5 at $2.10, and Rowan agreed a $0.9m break fee with B. Bidder C now announces a firm offer at $2.40 and asks for a $0.9m break fee.",
  prompt:"Can Rowan normally agree it?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: B's $2.10 first competing offer sets a $6.3m aggregate cap, already used up", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 fixes the cap 'by reference to the price of the first competing offer at the time of the announcement made under Rule 3.5'. B was the first competing offer, at $2.10.\n\n• Cap: 300m × $2.10 × 1% = $6.3m.\n• Committed: A's $5.4m + B's $0.9m = $6.3m.\n• Headroom: nil.\n\nC's higher price of $2.40 doesn't raise the cap, because C isn't the first competing offer. If it did, each new bidder at a higher price would open up room for another fee, and a series of fees could pile up, which is what Note 5 is designed to stop. Rowan can't normally agree C's fee unless the existing fees are reduced, which would need the agreement of A or B. As always, the Council should be consulted at the earliest opportunity."},
    {t:"Yes: at C's $2.40 the cap is $7.2m, leaving exactly $0.9m", correct:false,
     why:"The cap is set by the first competing offer's price at its Rule 3.5 announcement, B's $2.10, not by the latest or highest offer."},
    {t:"Yes: each bidder may have a fee of up to 1% of the value at its own price", correct:false,
     why:"That was arguable before 2026. Note 5 now caps 'the aggregate value of all break fees'."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as C's fee is payable only if C's own offer becomes or is declared unconditional", correct:false,
     why:"Under Note 5, every break fee 'is capable of becoming payable only if an offer becomes or is declared unconditional'. That condition is already required, so it doesn't create headroom under the cap."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 5", takeaway:"Later, higher bids don't raise the cap." },

{ id:"tc4-030", lesson:"TC4C", type:"fill", amended:true,
  prompt:"Finch Ltd has 400m shares and has agreed break fees with two offerors. The original offer is $1.25 a share. The first competing offer was announced under Rule 3.5 at $1.50 a share. The two fees together can normally total no more than $____m.",
  accept:["6","6m","$6m","$6","6.0","$6.0m","6 million","$6 million","s$6m","6,000,000","$6,000,000","6000000"], answer:"6",
  why:"Note 5 caps the aggregate of all break fees at normally no more than 1% of the offeree's value 'calculated by reference to the price of the first competing offer at the time of the announcement made under Rule 3.5'.\n\n• First competing offer price: $1.50.\n• Value: 400m × $1.50 = $600m.\n• Aggregate cap: 1% × $600m = $6m.\n\nThe original offer's $1.25 price would give a lower figure: 400m × $1.25 × 1% = $5m. That was the cap for the first fee on its own under the main Rule (by reference to its offer price). Once there are competing offers and more than one fee, Note 5 recalculates the overall cap at the first competing price. If Finch had in-the-money options or convertibles, Note 4's fully diluted method could also be used.",
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Notes 4–5", takeaway:"Shares × first competing price × 1%." },

{ id:"tc4-031", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Heron has agreed break fees with both Kestrel and Osprey. Its board withdraws its recommendation of Kestrel's offer and recommends Osprey's higher offer, which is the trigger event in Kestrel's agreement. Kestrel's offer then lapses. Osprey's offer later lapses too, after failing to meet its acceptance condition.",
  prompt:"Is Kestrel's break fee payable?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: under Note 5 a fee can become payable only if an offer goes unconditional", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 ends: 'Any break fee is capable of becoming payable only if an offer becomes or is declared unconditional.'\n\nThe trigger event in Kestrel's agreement, the board switching its recommendation to Osprey, did happen. But Note 5 adds a further condition that applies to every break fee: some offer must become or be declared unconditional. Here neither Kestrel's offer nor Osprey's did. Both lapsed. So Kestrel's fee can't become payable.\n\nThe logic: a break fee is meant to compensate a bidder that loses to someone else. If no offer succeeds, the offeree's shareholders have gained nothing from the contest, and they shouldn't also pay a fee out of company funds. If Osprey's offer had become unconditional, the condition would have been met, and Kestrel's fee could have become payable on its contractual trigger."},
    {t:"Yes: the trigger event in the agreement has occurred", correct:false,
     why:"The contractual trigger alone isn't enough under the amended Code. Note 5 requires an offer to become or be declared unconditional before any break fee can become payable."},
    {t:"Yes, but reduced to half because neither offer succeeded", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 doesn't provide for partial payment. Either an offer becomes unconditional, or the fee can't become payable."},
    {t:"Only if Osprey's offer had been recommended by the board for longer than 7 days", correct:false,
     why:"There's no such test. The 7-day figure belongs to matching rights under Note 6(c)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 5", takeaway:"No unconditional offer, no break fee." },

{ id:"tc4-032", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Kestrel's implementation agreement with Heron bars Heron's board from engaging with any other potential offeror for four months, with no exceptions.",
  prompt:"What does new Note 6 on Rule 13 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"A fiduciary proviso releasing the directors where needed, including for Rules 5 and 9.2", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 13, new in 2026: 'The Council recognises that the parties to an offer usually enter into an implementation agreement ... Offeree directors should carefully consider whether the commitments given to an offeror would deter other potential competing offerors from making an approach or offer: (a) Exclusivity arrangements which limit the ability of the offeree company board to engage with competing offerors or potential competing offerors must be subject to the proviso that the offeree directors are relieved of such obligations if it is necessary to discharge their fiduciary duties (including but not limited to their obligations under Rule 5 and Rule 9.2).'\n\nA four-month exclusivity with no exceptions would stop Heron's board engaging with a better offer, and might conflict with Rule 9.2, which requires information to be given to any bona fide competing offeror on request. The fiduciary out lets the board engage where its duties require.\n\nNote 6 also says: 'If, after an implementation agreement is executed, the Council identifies any provision of the implementation agreement which it considers to be in breach of this Note, the Council will require remedial action and may initiate disciplinary action.'"},
    {t:"Nothing more, as long as the exclusivity clause is disclosed in the Rule 3 announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is required, but it doesn't cure the substance. Note 6(a) requires a fiduciary proviso in any exclusivity arrangement."},
    {t:"Exclusivity is prohibited outright in any implementation agreement", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 accepts exclusivity arrangements, provided they include the fiduciary out. The concern is unqualified exclusivity, not exclusivity as such."},
    {t:"Exclusivity is limited to 7 calendar days, like a matching right", correct:false,
     why:"The 7-day benchmark in Note 6(c) applies to matching rights. For exclusivity, the requirement is the fiduciary proviso."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 6(a); r 9.2", takeaway:"Exclusivity needs a fiduciary out." },

{ id:"tc4-033", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"The draft implementation agreement gives Kestrel the right to match any competing offer within 14 calendar days of being told of it.",
  prompt:"How will the Council normally view the matching right?",
  options:[
    {t:"As anti-competitive: over 7 calendar days would normally have that effect", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6(c) on Rule 13, new in 2026: 'A matching right, which allows the offeror to match or better a competing offer, cannot be for a duration that removes any practical likelihood of a potential competing offeror putting forward a proposal to the offeree company. In this regard, a duration of a matching right exceeding 7 calendar days would normally be regarded as having an anti-competitive effect.'\n\nA matching right means a rival's proposal may simply be matched, so it gains nothing. A long matching period makes things worse: the rival's proposal is held up for weeks while the first offeror decides, and its financing and board support may fall away in the meantime. Fourteen days is twice the benchmark, so it would normally be anti-competitive. The parties should shorten it to 7 calendar days or less.\n\nIf such a clause slipped through, the Council would require remedial action. Note 6 recommends a clause saying any provision the Council finds impermissible under Rule 13 'shall have no effect and shall be disregarded'."},
    {t:"As acceptable: the Code doesn't limit the length of matching rights", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(c), added in 2026, does limit them. A duration exceeding 7 calendar days would normally be regarded as anti-competitive."},
    {t:"As prohibited: matching rights aren't allowed in any agreement", correct:false,
     why:"Matching rights are permitted, within the duration benchmark. Note 6(c) targets only those long enough to deter rival proposals."},
    {t:"As acceptable, as long as Kestrel pays the offeree for it", correct:false,
     why:"Paying for a matching right doesn't change its effect on competition. The test is whether the duration removes any practical likelihood of a competing proposal."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 6(c)", takeaway:"Matching rights: 7 days, normally." },

{ id:"tc4-034", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Which notification obligation in an implementation agreement is least likely to deter a potential competing offeror?",
  options:[
    {t:"Telling the original offeror only that an approach has been received", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6(b) on Rule 13: 'A notification obligation which requires the offeree to disclose details of any potential competing offer to the original offeror may reduce the likelihood of a potential competing offeror making an approach. In this regard, the notification of the fact of an approach would have limited anti-competitive effect as compared to the provision of detailed information relating to the competing offer.'\n\nA rival considering an approach knows that anything it tells the offeree may be passed to the first offeror. If that includes its price, conditions or financing, the first offeror can tailor a counter-bid, and the rival may decide it isn't worth trying. A bare notice that 'an approach has been received' gives the first offeror much less to work with.\n\nThe Note doesn't ban detailed notification outright, but the offeree board should weigh its deterrent effect carefully, consistently with its duties. Combined with a matching right, detailed notification makes it especially easy for the first offeror to see off a rival."},
    {t:"Passing on the competing proposal's price and conditions", correct:false,
     why:"That is 'detailed information relating to the competing offer', which Note 6(b) says may reduce the likelihood of a rival approaching."},
    {t:"Providing a copy of the competing offeror's proposal letter", correct:false,
     why:"A copy of the proposal is the most detailed information possible. It would give the first offeror everything it needs to counter."},
    {t:"Passing on details of how the competing offeror plans to finance its offer", correct:false,
     why:"Financing details are detailed information about the competing offer, and could let the first offeror target the rival's weaknesses."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 6(b)", takeaway:"Notify the fact, not the details." },

{ id:"tc4-035", lesson:"TC4C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Which provision may be included in an implementation agreement as customary under Note 6?",
  options:[
    {t:"An obligation to take the procedural steps needed to progress a scheme", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 13: 'For the avoidance of doubt, customary provisions pertaining to (i) representations and warranties in relation to information provided by the offeree company to the offeror; (ii) an obligation that requires the offeree company to carry out certain procedural actions to progress an offer via a scheme of arrangement; or (iii) the non-occurrence of specific events or actions which do not deter competition may be included in the implementation agreement.'\n\nA scheme can't proceed without the offeree's cooperation: it must convene the court meeting, send the scheme document, and apply for sanction. An obligation to carry out those steps is mechanical, not anti-competitive. Representations about information the offeree has given are normal commercial protections. Conditions about specific events not happening (for example, no material adverse change) are fine, so long as they don't deter competition.\n\nThe contrasts are the provisions Note 6 restricts: exclusivity without a fiduciary out, detailed notification of rival proposals, and matching rights over 7 calendar days."},
    {t:"A 21-day matching right in favour of the original offeror", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(c) says a matching right exceeding 7 calendar days would normally be regarded as anti-competitive. 21 days is three times the benchmark."},
    {t:"Exclusivity with no fiduciary proviso for the directors", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(a) requires exclusivity to be subject to a proviso relieving directors where necessary to discharge their fiduciary duties."},
    {t:"A duty to pass on full details of any competing proposal the offeree receives", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(b) warns that providing detailed information about a competing offer may reduce the likelihood of a rival approaching."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 6", takeaway:"Customary terms that don't deter rivals are fine." },

{ id:"tc4-036", lesson:"TC4C", type:"tf", amended:true,
  prompt:"True or false: The Council considers that an implementation agreement should include a clause providing that any provision the Council determines is not permitted by Rule 13 shall have no effect and be disregarded.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 13: 'The Council considers that, in order to ensure that the parties to an implementation agreement are able to comply with a direction made by the Council, the following clause should be included in the agreement: \"The parties agree that, if the Council determines that any provision of this agreement that requires the offeree company to take or not to take action, whether as a direct obligation or as a condition to any other person's obligation (however expressed), is not permitted by Rule 13 of the Code, that provision shall have no effect and shall be disregarded.\"'\n\nThe Code has no force of law, so the Council can't strike down a contract term itself. If an implementation agreement contained an impermissible term, such as a 14-day matching right, the parties might be contractually bound to it even after the Council objected. The clause solves that by having the parties agree in advance that any term the Council finds impermissible falls away. The clause covers obligations 'whether as a direct obligation or as a condition to any other person's obligation', so it can't be avoided by drafting. The same Note says that if the Council finds a breach after signing, it 'will require remedial action and may initiate disciplinary action'."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 sets out the clause the Council expects, so that the parties can comply with any Council direction that a provision isn't permitted under Rule 13."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Note 6", takeaway:"Build in the Council override." },

{ id:"tc4-037", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"During Corran's offer for Ashby, Ashby's board announces agreed terms to sell its two main divisions, which contribute 55% of its earnings, to Northgate Ltd. Northgate asks for a break fee.",
  prompt:"What does new Note 7 on Rule 13 provide?",
  options:[
    {t:"A fee with Northgate is normally allowed at announcement, on Note 5's conditions", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 on Rule 13, new in 2026: 'Where, in competition with an offer or a possible offer, the board of the offeree company announces that it has agreed terms on which it intends to sell all or materially all of the company's assets and/or businesses, the Council will normally grant consent, such that the offeree company would be permitted, subject to the same conditions as set out in Note 5 above, to enter into a break fee arrangement with the asset purchaser at the time of the announcement.'\n\nThe Note adds: 'the Council would normally regard a person to be seeking to acquire materially all of the assets and/or businesses of a company if such assets and/or businesses account for or contribute more than 30% of the offeree company's sales, earnings, assets or market capitalisation'. Northgate's purchase covers 55% of earnings, so it qualifies.\n\nThe Note 5 conditions mean any fee to Northgate and any fee to Corran must together stay within the aggregate 1% cap, and a fee can become payable only if an offer becomes unconditional. The asset sale itself is frustrating action under Rule 5, so it needs shareholder approval with the Note 9 safeguards, unless an exception applies."},
    {t:"No break fee is possible: Rule 13 deals only with offerors for shares", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 extends break fee consent to an asset purchaser competing with an offer, where it is buying all or materially all of the business."},
    {t:"A fee is possible, outside any cap, because the transaction is an asset sale, not an offer", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says the fee is 'subject to the same conditions as set out in Note 5', including the aggregate cap."},
    {t:"Only if the divisions being sold exceed 50% of total assets", correct:false,
     why:"The normal benchmark is 'more than 30%' of any of sales, earnings, assets or market capitalisation. Northgate's 55% of earnings is enough."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Notes 5, 7; r 5, Note 9", takeaway:"Competing asset buyer: break fee allowed, inside the cap." },

{ id:"tc4x-044", lesson:"TC4C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"During an offer for Linnet Ltd, its board agrees to sell a business to a buyer and announces it. The business contributes 25% of Linnet's sales and 22% of its assets, but 35% of its market capitalisation on analysts' valuations. The buyer asks for a break fee.",
  prompt:"Can Note 7 apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: one measure over 30% is normally enough, and market cap is 35%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 says the Council 'would normally regard a person to be seeking to acquire materially all of the assets and/or businesses of a company if such assets and/or businesses account for or contribute more than 30% of the offeree company's sales, earnings, assets or market capitalisation'. The measures are alternatives, joined by 'or'. One is enough.\n\nThe business is below 30% on sales (25%) and assets (22%), but above it on market capitalisation (35%). On the normal test, the buyer is seeking materially all of Linnet's business, so Note 7 can apply, and the Council will normally consent to a break fee at the time of the announcement, subject to the Note 5 conditions.\n\nBecause the market-capitalisation figure rests on analysts' valuations, which are less objective than sales or assets, this is a case for the Note's last sentence: 'The Council should be consulted in cases of doubt.' The same 30% test applies under Note 2 on Rule 9.2 for equal information to asset buyers, and under Rule 9.1 since 2026."},
    {t:"No: both sales and assets are under 30%, so the test fails", correct:false,
     why:"The four measures are alternatives. A business can qualify on market capitalisation alone if it exceeds 30% on that measure."},
    {t:"No: all four of the measures must exceed 30% for Note 7 to apply", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says 'sales, earnings, assets or market capitalisation'. The word 'or' means any one is enough."},
    {t:"Yes, and the fee may be outside the usual 1% cap as well", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 applies 'subject to the same conditions as set out in Note 5', including the aggregate 1% cap on all break fees."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 13, Notes 5, 7; r 9.2, Note 2", takeaway:"Any one measure over 30% = materially all." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t3p3/TC5A.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T5 · Lesson TC5A The Triggers (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code r 14.1 and Notes 9–17 on Rule 14.1 (Note 10 heading new 2026). Replaces TC5A in takeoverCode5.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc5-001", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sable Ltd holds 22% of Tern Ltd. Sable's parent, which acts in concert with it, holds 6%. On 4 March Sable buys a further 2.5% from a fund.",
  prompt:"What does Rule 14.1 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"An immediate offer: with its parent, Sable now holds 30.5%", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a): 'Except with the Council's consent, where any person acquires whether by a series of transactions over a period of time or not, shares which (taken together with shares held or acquired by persons acting in concert with him) carry 30% or more of the voting rights of a company ... such person must extend offers immediately, on the basis set out in this Rule'.\n\nSable (22%) and its parent (6%) are presumed to act in concert under presumption (a) of Defn 1, so they start at 28%. The 2.5% purchase takes the group to 30.5%. The trigger is the moment the group crosses 30%, which is 4 March. 'Immediately' is given practical meaning by the Note on Rule 3: Sable must announce within 30 minutes, or request a trading halt and announce before it is lifted. The offer must be on Rule 14's terms: only the more-than-50% acceptance condition (Rule 14.2), cash or a cash alternative at not less than the highest price paid in the 6 months before (Rule 14.3)."},
    {t:"No offer: Sable on its own holds only 24.5% of the votes", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) counts shares 'taken together with shares held or acquired by persons acting in concert with him'. Sable's parent's 6% counts, so the group holds 30.5%."},
    {t:"An offer only if the group reaches 30% in a single purchase", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) applies 'whether by a series of transactions over a period of time or not'. It doesn't matter how many purchases it took to get there."},
    {t:"An offer within 6 months of the purchase, not immediately", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1 says 'must extend offers immediately'. The 6-month period is part of the creeper test in Rule 14.1(b) and the pricing look-back in Rule 14.3, not a deadline for the offer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(a); r 3, Note on timing", takeaway:"Group crosses 30%: offer now." },

{ id:"tc5x-001", lesson:"TC5A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A person who builds a stake from 20% to 31% through 40 small market purchases over three years must make a mandatory offer when the purchase that takes him to 31% is made.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) applies where 'any person acquires whether by a series of transactions over a period of time or not, shares which ... carry 30% or more of the voting rights'. The words 'by a series of transactions over a period of time or not' mean the route to 30% doesn't matter. Forty small purchases over three years are treated the same as one large purchase.\n\nThe obligation arises on the purchase that crosses 30%. Below 30%, there's no creeper limit: a holder can buy as much as he likes, as long as he doesn't reach 30%. The 1% in 6 months rule in Rule 14.1(b) applies only to holders already between 30% and 50%. On the crossing, the offer price will be at least the highest price he or his concert parties paid in the offer period and the 6 months before (Rule 14.3), so purchases made years ago don't set the floor, but recent ones do."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) expressly covers acquisitions 'by a series of transactions over a period of time'. The crossing purchase triggers the offer, however gradual the build-up."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(a); r 14.3", takeaway:"Gradual or sudden: crossing 30% triggers." },

{ id:"tc5-002", lesson:"TC5A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Once the Rule 14.1 obligation arises, to whom must the offer be extended, and who may have to make it?",
  options:[
    {t:"Every voting class the group holds; any principal group member may be liable", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1: 'such person must extend offers immediately ... to the holders of any class of share capital of the company which carries votes and in which such person, or persons acting in concert with him, hold shares. In addition to such person, each of the principal members of the group of persons acting in concert with him may, according to the circumstances of the case, have the obligation to extend an offer.'\n\nTwo points. The offer goes to each voting class in which the group holds shares. If the company has ordinary shares and voting preference shares, and the group holds both, both classes must receive offers. Classes that don't carry votes, or in which the group holds nothing, aren't covered by this part of Rule 14 (though Rule 19 on convertibles and comparable offers may matter).\n\nAnd liability can extend beyond the person who made the triggering purchase. If a minor concert party makes the purchase, the Council can look to the group's leader to make the offer, depending on the circumstances."},
    {t:"Ordinary shareholders only, and only the person who made the acquisition is liable", correct:false,
     why:"The offer goes to 'any class of share capital ... which carries votes and in which such person, or persons acting in concert with him, hold shares'. And 'each of the principal members of the group' may have the obligation."},
    {t:"Holders of every class of share capital, whether or not it carries votes", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1 is limited to classes 'which carries votes'. Non-voting classes aren't part of the mandatory offer under this Rule."},
    {t:"Only those who were shareholders at the moment the 30% threshold was crossed", correct:false,
     why:"The offer is made to 'the holders' of the relevant classes during the offer. People who buy in the market after the trigger can accept too."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1", takeaway:"Every voting class the group holds; principal members can be liable." },

{ id:"tc5-003", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Corvid Holdings has held 42% of Linnet Ltd for two years. In 2026 it buys 0.6% on 15 January, 0.3% on 20 May, 0.5% on 10 August and 0.4% on 1 September. It buys nothing else.",
  prompt:"Which purchase, if any, triggers a mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"The 1 September purchase", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(b) applies where a person 'together with persons acting in concert with him, holds not less than 30% but not more than 50% of the voting rights and such person, or any person acting in concert with him, acquires in any period of 6 months additional shares carrying more than 1% of the voting rights'.\n\nAfter each purchase, look back 6 months:\n• 20 May: window from 20 November. It contains 15 January (0.6%) and 20 May (0.3%) = 0.9%. Not more than 1%.\n• 10 August: window from 10 February. January has dropped out. It contains 20 May (0.3%) and 10 August (0.5%) = 0.8%.\n• 1 September: window from 1 March. It contains 20 May (0.3%), 10 August (0.5%) and 1 September (0.4%) = 1.2%. More than 1%, so this purchase triggers the offer.\n\nThe 'any period of 6 months' wording means a rolling window, checked at each purchase. Corvid needed to track its rolling total and stop at 0.2% on 1 September."},
    {t:"The 10 August purchase", correct:false,
     why:"By 10 August the January purchase is more than 6 months old. The window from 10 February holds only 0.3% + 0.5% = 0.8%."},
    {t:"The 20 May purchase", correct:false,
     why:"0.6% + 0.3% = 0.9%, which isn't more than 1%."},
    {t:"None: no single purchase exceeds 1%", correct:false,
     why:"The test is the total 'additional shares' acquired 'in any period of 6 months'. No single purchase needs to exceed 1%."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(b)", takeaway:"Rolling 6 months, more than 1% in total." },

{ id:"tc5-004", lesson:"TC5A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A shareholder holding 35% who acquires exactly 1% of the voting rights within 6 months must make a mandatory offer.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(b) applies where the holder 'acquires in any period of 6 months additional shares carrying more than 1% of the voting rights'. Exactly 1% isn't more than 1%. The shareholder stays within the limit.\n\nThe practical consequence is that a 30–50% holder can buy up to 1% in each rolling 6-month window without an offer. That's often called the 'creeper' allowance. But the margin is tight: any further purchase in the same window, however small, would push the total over 1% and trigger an offer. Compare the boundary at 30%: effective control is '30% or more' (Defn 11), so reaching exactly 30% triggers Rule 14.1(a). Watch the wording at each threshold."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The threshold is 'more than 1%', not '1% or more'. An acquisition of exactly 1% within six months doesn't trigger Rule 14.1(b)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(b); Defn 11", takeaway:"1% is allowed; more than 1% isn't." },

{ id:"tc5-005", lesson:"TC5A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each holding and purchase to its Rule 14.1 result. Percentages are of voting rights; each purchase is made within one 6-month period.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"29% holder buys 1.5%", r:"Offer: crosses 30% under r 14.1(a)"},
    {l:"40% holder buys 0.9%", r:"No offer: within the creeper limit"},
    {l:"50% holder buys 1.2%", r:"Offer: creeper under r 14.1(b)"},
    {l:"51% holder buys 5%", r:"No offer: already over 50%"}
  ],
  why:"29% + 1.5% = 30.5%, crossing 30%: Rule 14.1(a) applies.\n\n40% + 0.9%: Rule 14.1(b) applies only to acquisitions of 'more than 1%' in 6 months. 0.9% is within the limit.\n\n50% + 1.2%: Rule 14.1(b) covers a person who 'holds not less than 30% but not more than 50%'. Exactly 50% is inside the band ('not more than 50%'), so 1.2% in 6 months triggers an offer. This is a classic trap: a 50% holder doesn't have statutory control, which requires 'more than 50%' (Defn 23).\n\n51% + 5%: above 50%, the holder has statutory control and is outside Rule 14.1(b). It can buy freely without a mandatory offer obligation.\n\nSo: under 30%, don't cross 30%; from 30% to 50% inclusive, don't acquire more than 1% in any 6 months; over 50%, free.",
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(a)–(b); Defn 23", takeaway:"Under 30%: don't cross. 30–50%: creep 1%. Over 50%: free." },

{ id:"tc5-006", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Corvid holds 40% of Linnet. On 1 March it buys 0.8%. On 1 April it sells 1.5%. On 1 June it buys 0.6%, leaving it at 39.9%.",
  prompt:"Has Corvid triggered a mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it bought 1.4% in 6 months, and the sale can't be netted", correct:true,
     why:"Note 11 on Rule 14.1: 'If a shareholder or group of shareholders acting in concert holding 30% or more sells shares, the provisions of this Rule will apply to the reduced holding. As a result, an offer obligation will arise if: ... (b) the reduced holding is 30% or more, and is increased by more than 1% in any 6-month period. In this context, sales of shares may not be netted off against purchases.'\n\nRule 14.1(b) counts 'additional shares' acquired. Corvid acquired 0.8% on 1 March and 0.6% on 1 June, a total of 1.4% within six months. The 1.5% sale in between doesn't reduce that figure. Corvid ends below where it started, but it has still acquired more than 1% in 6 months and must make an offer.\n\nWhy no netting? Otherwise a holder could sell to one group of shareholders and buy from another, churning its stake while concentrating it among friendly hands, without ever triggering an offer."},
    {t:"No: its holding is now lower than it was on 1 March, so no creep", correct:false,
     why:"The net position is irrelevant. Note 11 says 'sales of shares may not be netted off against purchases'. Purchases are counted gross."},
    {t:"No: the 1 June purchase on its own was only 0.6%", correct:false,
     why:"The 0.8% bought on 1 March is in the same 6-month window. Together they make 1.4%."},
    {t:"Only if its holding goes back above 40% again", correct:false,
     why:"There's no test based on the starting holding. Rule 14.1(b) asks how much was acquired in any 6-month period."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(b); r 14.1, Note 11", takeaway:"Count purchases gross; sales don't offset." },

{ id:"tc5-007", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Finch Partners held 36% of Avocet Ltd. In February it sold 8%, cutting its holding to 28%. In May Avocet's price falls and Finch buys 2.5% in the market.",
  prompt:"What is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"An offer is due: its reduced 28% holding has risen to 30.5%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 11 on Rule 14.1 applies Rule 14 to 'the reduced holding': 'an offer obligation will arise if: (a) following a reduction of the holding below 30%, it is increased to 30% or more'.\n\nFinch sold down to 28%, below 30%. Its May purchase takes it back to 30.5%, crossing 30% again. That triggers an offer, just as it would for someone who had never been above 30%.\n\nThe earlier 36% gives Finch no credit. Once it falls below 30%, its effective control is gone, and other shareholders are entitled to the Code's protection if control is acquired again. Notice that the creeper allowance doesn't help here: Rule 14.1(b) only applies to holders already at 30% or more, and Finch was at 28% before the purchase."},
    {t:"No offer: Finch has held more than 30% at some time in the past", correct:false,
     why:"Note 11 says the Rule 'will apply to the reduced holding'. Past control doesn't carry over once the holding drops below 30%."},
    {t:"No offer: 2.5% is less than the 8% it sold earlier", correct:false,
     why:"Note 11 says 'sales of shares may not be netted off against purchases'. The question is whether the reduced holding has been increased to 30% or more."},
    {t:"An offer only if Finch's holding goes back above 36% again", correct:false,
     why:"Below 30%, Finch is treated like anyone else. Its trigger is 30%, not its previous high."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 11(a)", takeaway:"Sell below 30%, and 30% is your line again." },

{ id:"tc5x-002", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Grebe Ltd held 45% of Heron Ltd. It sold 10% in January, leaving it at 35%. In March it buys 0.7% and in May another 0.6% in the market.",
  prompt:"Has Grebe triggered a mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: its reduced 35% is still 30%+, and it rose 1.3% in 6 months", correct:true,
     why:"Note 11 on Rule 14.1 applies Rule 14 to 'the reduced holding'. Limb (b): 'the reduced holding is 30% or more, and is increased by more than 1% in any 6-month period'.\n\nGrebe's reduced holding is 35%, which is 30% or more, so the creeper limit applies to it. March (0.7%) plus May (0.6%) is 1.3% within six months, more than 1%. The offer is triggered on the May purchase.\n\nGrebe can't argue that it's still below its former 45%. Note 11 measures increases from the reduced holding, not the peak. And 'sales of shares may not be netted off against purchases', so the January sale gives no allowance. The policy is that a holder who sells down and then buys back is changing the balance of control, and the minority should be protected each time."},
    {t:"No: it is still well below its former 45% holding", correct:false,
     why:"Note 11 applies Rule 14 to 'the reduced holding'. Being below a previous peak doesn't create headroom."},
    {t:"No: the January sale gives it 10% of headroom to rebuild", correct:false,
     why:"Note 11 says sales may not be netted off against purchases. The sale doesn't create an allowance for later purchases."},
    {t:"Only if its holding goes back above 45% again", correct:false,
     why:"The test is more than 1% in 6 months from the reduced 35% holding. Recovering the old 45% isn't the trigger."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 11(b)", takeaway:"Reduced but still 30%+: creeper runs from the new level." },

{ id:"tc5-008", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Pemberton Ltd made a mandatory offer for Shrike Ltd that closed on 30 April with Pemberton at 46%. It bought 4% in the market during the offer. On 10 June it buys a further 0.8%.",
  prompt:"Does the June purchase trigger a new offer under Rule 14.1(b)?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: shares bought before or during the previous offer are ignored, leaving 0.8%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 12 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Acquisitions of up to 1% in any 6-month period': 'For the purpose of the 1% limit on acquisitions in any 6-month period by a person or group of persons acting in concert, shares acquired before or during a previous mandatory offer are not taken into account. This also applies to shares acquired with the prior approval of independent shareholders within the terms of Note 1 of the Notes on Dispensation from Rule 14.'\n\nPemberton's 4% bought during its mandatory offer doesn't count towards the 1% limit. Only the 0.8% bought on 10 June does, and that's under 1%.\n\nThe logic: when Pemberton made the mandatory offer, every shareholder had the chance to sell at the required price. Those shares have already been 'paid for' in Code terms. Counting them again would penalise Pemberton twice. Similarly, shares acquired with independent shareholders' approval under a whitewash were approved by the very people the creeper protects."},
    {t:"Yes: 4.8% has been acquired within 6 months", correct:false,
     why:"Note 12 excludes 'shares acquired before or during a previous mandatory offer' from the 1% count. Only the June purchase counts."},
    {t:"Yes: any purchase within 6 months of a mandatory offer triggers another", correct:false,
     why:"There's no such rule. The normal creeper limit applies, with the Note 12 exclusion for shares acquired before or during the previous mandatory offer."},
    {t:"No: a holder who has made a mandatory offer is outside Rule 14.1(b) for good", correct:false,
     why:"Pemberton is still between 30% and 50%, so Rule 14.1(b) applies to future acquisitions. Buying more than 1% in any 6 months from now on would trigger an offer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 12", takeaway:"Mandatory-offer purchases don't count towards the next 1%." },

{ id:"tc5-009", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Merlin Capital holds 26% of Godwit Ltd. Godwit issues Merlin warrants to subscribe for new Godwit shares. Exercising them all would take Merlin to 32%. The warrant issue was not put to an independent shareholder vote.",
  prompt:"When does Rule 14 bite?",
  options:[
    {t:"On exercise: buying warrants doesn't trigger an offer, but exercising them does", correct:true,
     why:"Note 10 on Rule 14.1, whose heading 'Instruments convertible into, rights to subscribe for and options in respect of new shares which carry voting rights' was inserted in 2026: 'In general, the acquisition of instruments convertible into, rights to subscribe for and options in respect of new shares which carry voting rights does not give rise to an obligation under this Rule to make an offer. However, the exercise of any conversion or subscription rights or options will be considered to be an acquisition of voting rights for the purpose of this Rule.'\n\nWarrants over new shares carry no votes until exercised, so holding them doesn't change control. Exercise creates new voting shares in Merlin's hands. If exercise takes Merlin from 26% to 30% or more, that's an acquisition crossing 30% under Rule 14.1(a).\n\nThe Note offers a route to avoid this: the Council won't normally require an offer following exercise if the issue of the warrants to Merlin was approved by independent shareholders in the manner of Dispensation Note 1. That wasn't done here, so exercise would trigger an offer."},
    {t:"On acquiring the warrants, as Merlin's fully diluted interest then exceeds 30%", correct:false,
     why:"Note 10 says acquiring such instruments 'does not give rise to an obligation'. Fully diluted interest isn't the test. Voting rights are counted when they exist."},
    {t:"Never: warrants over new shares are outside Rule 14", correct:false,
     why:"Note 10 says 'the exercise of any conversion or subscription rights or options will be considered to be an acquisition of voting rights'. Exercise is caught."},
    {t:"Only if Merlin also buys shares in the market", correct:false,
     why:"Exercise alone can trigger the offer if it takes Merlin to 30% or more. Market purchases matter to the separate dispensation for an approved warrant issue, which wasn't sought here."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 10", takeaway:"Warrants: buying is free, exercising counts." },

{ id:"tc5x-003", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Pipit Ltd has 100m shares in issue and 20m currently exercisable options held by employees. Mr Ong buys shares taking him to 30.2m shares, which is 30.2% of the existing shares but only 25.2% on a fully diluted basis.",
  prompt:"Has Mr Ong triggered a mandatory offer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: where rights are exercisable, the test is 30% of existing votes", correct:true,
     why:"Note 10 on Rule 14.1 ends: 'Where there are conversion or subscription rights or options currently capable of being exercised, this Rule is invoked at a level of 30% of the existing voting rights.'\n\nMr Ong can't argue that the employees' options will dilute him to 25.2% when exercised. The test uses the voting rights that exist now. He holds 30.2m of 100m existing shares, which is 30.2%, so he has crossed 30% and must make an offer.\n\nThis fits Defn 24, which defines voting rights as those 'currently exercisable at a general meeting'. Options aren't counted until they are exercised, either for the holder of the options (Note 10's first paragraph) or as a dilution of other holders. Using fully diluted figures would let someone sit above 30% of the votes that actually count, without making an offer, on the speculation that others might later exercise."},
    {t:"No: on a fully diluted basis he holds only 25.2% of the votes", correct:false,
     why:"Note 10 says that where options are currently exercisable, the Rule 'is invoked at a level of 30% of the existing voting rights'. Fully diluted numbers aren't used."},
    {t:"No, unless and until the employees exercise their options", correct:false,
     why:"The employees' exercise would reduce Mr Ong's percentage, not trigger anything. He has already crossed 30% of the existing voting rights."},
    {t:"Only if he holds 30% or more of the fully diluted share capital of the company", correct:false,
     why:"That reverses Note 10. The trigger is 30% of existing voting rights."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 10; Defn 24", takeaway:"Measure against existing votes, not fully diluted." },

{ id:"tc5-010", lesson:"TC5A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Merlin holds 27% of Godwit and plans a cash-settled total return swap giving it long economic exposure to a further 4% of Godwit's existing shares. What does Note 16 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Merlin is normally treated as acquiring the 4%, and must consult the Council first", correct:true,
     why:"Note 16 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Options and derivatives': 'For the purposes of Rule 14, a person who has acquired or written any option or derivative which causes him to have a long economic exposure, whether absolute or conditional, to changes in the price of securities will normally be treated as having acquired those securities. Such options and derivatives would exclude instruments convertible into, rights to subscribe for and options in respect of new shares (see Note 10 on Rule 14.1). Any person who would breach the thresholds stipulated in Rule 14.1 as a result of acquiring such options or derivatives ... must consult the Council beforehand to determine if an offer is required, and, if so, the terms of the offer to be made.'\n\nA total return swap gives Merlin the economic gains and losses on 4% of Godwit's existing shares. Merlin's 27% plus 4% is 31%, so it would breach the 30% threshold. It must consult first. 'In determining if an offer is required, the Council will consider, amongst others, the time when the option or derivative is entered into, the consideration paid for the option or derivative, and the relationship and arrangements between the parties'. A swap counterparty that hedges by buying shares and might vote them as Merlin wishes would be a significant factor."},
    {t:"Nothing: a cash-settled swap carries no votes, so it doesn't count", correct:false,
     why:"Note 16 looks at 'long economic exposure', not voting rights or settlement method. A cash-settled swap can still give Merlin the economics of control."},
    {t:"Merlin need consult the Council only if the swap can be physically settled in shares", correct:false,
     why:"Note 16 covers 'any option or derivative which causes him to have a long economic exposure'. Cash-settled derivatives are within it."},
    {t:"Note 10 applies, so only settlement of the swap in shares counts", correct:false,
     why:"Note 10 covers instruments over new shares. Note 16 deals with options and derivatives over existing securities and expressly excludes Note 10 instruments."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Notes 10, 16", takeaway:"Long exposure near 30%: consult first." },

{ id:"tc5x-004", lesson:"TC5A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Under Note 16, a conditional put and call option agreement over a 31% block, which works in substance like a conditional share purchase agreement, is treated as an option or derivative giving the buyer long economic exposure.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 16 on Rule 14.1 ends: 'For the avoidance of doubt, a conditional put and call option agreement, the nature of which is no different from a conditional share purchase agreement, will not be regarded as an option or derivative.'\n\nThis keeps the two regimes apart. A conditional put and call over a block is really a way of agreeing to buy, subject to conditions. It's dealt with under Note 1 on Rule 14.2, which allows an offeror to attach conditions to 'a share acquisition agreement or a put and call option agreement' that would trigger a mandatory bid on fulfilment, as long as the pre-conditions are clearly stated, objective and reasonable, have a reasonable time limit, can't be invoked without reasonable efforts and materiality, and are announced immediately with the offer terms.\n\nIf the arrangement were treated as a derivative under Note 16, the buyer might be treated as acquiring the shares straight away, triggering an immediate offer before the conditions were met, which isn't how the Code means such agreements to work."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 16 says expressly that such an agreement 'will not be regarded as an option or derivative'. It's handled as a conditional agreement under Note 1 on Rule 14.2."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 16; r 14.2, Note 1", takeaway:"Conditional put/call = conditional SPA, not a derivative." },

{ id:"tc5-011", lesson:"TC5A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Heron Fund holds 31% of Pipit Ltd and wants to lend 3% to a borrower. Which loan term keeps Heron from being treated as disposing of the voting rights and then reacquiring them?",
  options:[
    {t:"A right to recall the shares on 7 days' notice or less at any time", correct:true,
     why:"Note 15 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Securities borrowing and lending': 'the lender is deemed to have disposed of the voting rights attached to the shares that he lent out. On the return of those shares, the lender is deemed to have acquired the voting rights attached to the shares. However, if the lender has the right to recall the borrowed shares by giving advance notice of 7 days or less to the borrower any time during the period of the loan, the lender, for the purpose of Rule 14, will not be deemed to have disposed of the voting rights attached to those shares when he lends them out, nor will he be deemed to have acquired the voting rights attached to those shares when they are returned to him.'\n\nWithout the recall right, Heron would be deemed to drop from 31% to 28% on lending. When the shares came back, it would be deemed to acquire 3%, going from below 30% to 30% or more, which Note 11(a) says triggers an offer. A recall right of 7 days or less means Heron keeps effective control of the votes throughout, so no deemed disposal or reacquisition happens. The borrower, though, is still deemed to acquire the voting rights for the loan period, unless it can show it can't exercise or influence them."},
    {t:"A right to recall the shares on 14 days' notice at any time", correct:false,
     why:"Note 15 requires a recall right on 'advance notice of 7 days or less'. 14 days doesn't qualify."},
    {t:"A loan term of under 6 months in total", correct:false,
     why:"Note 15's test is the recall right, not the length of the loan."},
    {t:"A promise by the borrower not to vote the shares while it holds them", correct:false,
     why:"That affects whether the borrower is deemed to acquire the votes (it can show it can't exercise them). The lender's position under Note 15 depends on its recall right."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Notes 11, 15", takeaway:"Lender: 7-day recall, or it's a disposal and a reacquisition." },

{ id:"tc5x-005", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Swan Capital holds 28% of Tern Ltd. It borrows 3% of Tern's shares from a pension fund under a stock-lending agreement. Nothing in the agreement stops Swan from voting the borrowed shares.",
  prompt:"What is Swan's Rule 14 position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Swan is deemed to hold the borrowed votes, so 31%, unless it can't influence them", correct:true,
     why:"Note 15 on Rule 14.1: 'In a securities borrowing and lending transaction, the borrower of the shares is deemed to have acquired the voting rights attached to the shares for the purpose of Rule 14, unless he can show that he cannot exercise, direct, or influence the exercise of such voting rights. When the borrower returns the shares, he is deemed to have disposed of the voting rights attached to those shares for the purpose of Rule 14.'\n\nSwan's 28% plus the borrowed 3% is 31%. Nothing stops Swan from voting the borrowed shares, so it can't show it lacks control over the votes. Swan is deemed to have acquired them, which takes it across 30% and triggers Rule 14.1(a).\n\nBorrowing is a common way to build voting power cheaply, which is why the Note deems it an acquisition. The borrower can escape only by showing it genuinely can't exercise, direct or influence the votes, for example where the borrowed shares are immediately on-lent or sold and it has no voting control. The lender's position is dealt with separately: a recall right of 7 days or less avoids deemed disposal and reacquisition on its side."},
    {t:"No effect: borrowed shares never count towards Swan's holding", correct:false,
     why:"Note 15 deems the borrower 'to have acquired the voting rights attached to the shares', unless it can show it can't exercise or influence them."},
    {t:"Only the pension fund's position changes, as the lender of the shares to Swan", correct:false,
     why:"Note 15 deals with both sides. The borrower is deemed to acquire the votes, and the lender is deemed to dispose of them unless it has a recall right of 7 days or less."},
    {t:"No effect, as long as the loan is for less than 6 months", correct:false,
     why:"The length of the loan doesn't matter to the borrower's deemed acquisition. The question is whether it can exercise or influence the votes."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 15", takeaway:"Borrowed votes count as acquired." },

{ id:"tc5-012", lesson:"TC5A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A person who takes general control of the voting rights on another shareholder's 5% stake under a voting agreement, without buying the shares, has acquired relevant shares for Rule 14.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 13 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Acquisition or control of voting rights': 'If voting rights or general control of them, as distinct from the shares themselves, are acquired, the Council will deem this to be the acquisition of relevant shares for the purpose of this Rule. This will not normally apply in the case of a bank taking security over shares in the normal course of its business (see also Note 2 of the Notes on Dispensation from Rule 14).'\n\nRule 14 is about control, and control comes from votes, not legal title. A voting agreement that gives one person general control over another's votes gives the first person the same power as owning the shares. If that took him to 30% or more, he would have acquired effective control and would have to make an offer.\n\nThe bank exception reflects ordinary lending. A bank that takes shares as security doesn't usually control how they are voted, and if it does enforce, Dispensation Note 2 normally waives its offer obligation provided the security was taken on arm's length terms in the ordinary course."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 13 deems acquiring 'voting rights or general control of them, as distinct from the shares themselves' to be an acquisition of relevant shares. Rule 14 looks at control of votes."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 13; Dispensation Note 2", takeaway:"Control of the votes counts as the shares." },

{ id:"tc5x-006", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During a rights issue by Ibis Ltd, Mr Tan takes up his entitlement and buys rights from others. He now holds renounceable letters of allotment for new shares that would take him from 25% to 33% once the new shares are issued.",
  prompt:"What does Note 14 on Rule 14.1 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult the Council: shares allotted but not yet issued may count", correct:true,
     why:"Note 14 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Allotted but unissued shares': 'When shares of a company carrying voting rights have been allotted (even if only provisionally) but have not yet been issued, for example, under a rights issue when the shares are represented by renounceable letters of allotment, the Council should be consulted.'\n\nMr Tan's position sits between two clear cases. Holding warrants over new shares doesn't trigger an offer (Note 10), and holding issued shares obviously counts. Provisionally allotted shares on renounceable letters are close to being shares, and he'll own them on issue. The Code doesn't lay down a fixed answer. Instead, the Council should be consulted, so it can decide when the acquisition is treated as happening and whether Mr Tan's 33% will trigger an offer. Buying rights from others to build a stake above 30% is exactly the kind of thing the Council will want to examine. Mr Tan could also consider whether a whitewash under Dispensation Note 1 (which covers underwriting of an issue of new shares) is relevant."},
    {t:"Nothing happens until the new shares are actually issued", correct:false,
     why:"Note 14 doesn't let him wait. It says the Council should be consulted while the shares are allotted but unissued."},
    {t:"He has already acquired 33%, so an offer is due now", correct:false,
     why:"The Code doesn't say that automatically. Note 14 asks for consultation, so the Council can decide how the allotted shares are treated."},
    {t:"Rights issues are always exempt from Rule 14, whatever the result", correct:false,
     why:"There's no general exemption. Buying rights to increase a stake above 30% can trigger an offer, which is why Note 14 asks for consultation."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Notes 10, 14", takeaway:"Allotted-not-issued shares: consult." },

{ id:"tc5x-007", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Wee and his concert parties held 27% of Lark Ltd, a public company, before 1 January 2002 and still do. He wants to buy another 3% to reach exactly 30%.",
  prompt:"What does Note 17 on Rule 14.1 provide?",
  options:[
    {t:"Up to just under 30% freely; 30% needs consent, normally given on proof of his pre-2002 holding", correct:true,
     why:"Note 17 on Rule 14.1 deals with a transitional group: 'A person who, together with his concert parties, holds 25% or more but less than 30% of the voting rights of a public company before 1 Jan 2002 can acquire additional voting rights in the company on or after 1 Jan 2002 without making a general offer for the company, as long as the total voting rights in the company held by him and his concert parties are less than 30%.'\n\nIt continues: 'Except with the Council's prior consent, any acquisitions that would result in such a person and his concert parties acquiring 30% or more ... will be subject to Rule 14.1(a). The Council will normally consent to waiving Rule 14.1(a) where a person and his concert parties acquire not more than 30% of the voting rights of the company if that person applies to the Council with satisfactory evidence to show that he and his concert parties held 25% or more but less than 30% ... before 1 Jan 2002. After increasing their aggregate voting rights to 30% pursuant to the Council's consent, such person and his concert parties will be subject to Rule 14.1(b).'\n\nThe trigger used to be 25%. When it rose to 30%, those already between 25% and 30% were allowed to move up to 30%. Mr Wee can go to exactly 30% with consent, and after that the 1% creeper applies."},
    {t:"He must make an offer for any purchase at all, because he was above 25% under the old rules", correct:false,
     why:"Note 17 allows him to acquire freely while below 30%, and normally to reach 30% with the Council's consent on evidence of his pre-2002 holding."},
    {t:"He can go above 30% freely, because his pre-2002 holding is grandfathered", correct:false,
     why:"The concession stops at 'not more than 30%'. Above 30% Rule 14.1(a) applies, and once at 30% he is subject to Rule 14.1(b)."},
    {t:"Note 17 no longer applies after the 2026 amendments, so Rule 14 applies", correct:false,
     why:"Note 17 remains in the amended Code. It applies to anyone who held 25% or more but less than 30% before 1 January 2002."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 17", takeaway:"Pre-2002 25–30% holders: up to 30% with consent." },

{ id:"tc5-013", lesson:"TC5A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"During Kestrel's voluntary offer for Wren, Kestrel buys shares in the market that take it to 31%. What follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"Consult and announce a Rule 14 offer; if the price holds, a written notice can suffice", correct:true,
     why:"Note 9 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Triggering a mandatory offer during a voluntary offer': 'If the offeror in a voluntary offer or any person acting in concert with it incurs an obligation under this Rule during the offer by acquiring voting rights otherwise than through acceptances of the voluntary offer, the Council must be consulted. Once such an obligation is incurred, an offer in compliance with this Rule must be announced immediately. If there is no change in the offer price, it will be sufficient, following the announcement, simply to notify offeree company shareholders in writing of the new total holding of the offeror, of the fact that the acceptance condition in the form required by Rule 14.2 is the only condition remaining, and of the period for which the offer will remain open following posting of the document.'\n\nThe key change is to the conditions. A voluntary offer may have a higher acceptance condition and other conditions, such as regulatory approvals. A mandatory offer can be conditional 'upon, and only upon' acceptances giving more than 50% (Rule 14.2(a)), so everything else falls away. The price must also meet Rule 14.3, which may require a revision if Kestrel paid more in the market."},
    {t:"Nothing more: the voluntary offer already goes to all of Wren's shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The offer must be brought into line with Rule 14. In particular, its conditions must be reduced to the more-than-50% acceptance condition."},
    {t:"Kestrel must withdraw the voluntary offer and post a completely new offer document instead", correct:false,
     why:"Note 9 says that if the price doesn't change, written notice to shareholders is enough. A whole new offer isn't required."},
    {t:"Kestrel must reduce its holding below 30% before the offer can continue", correct:false,
     why:"Once the obligation is incurred, 'an offer in compliance with this Rule must be announced immediately'. Selling down isn't the remedy."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 9; r 14.2(a)", takeaway:"Mandatory mid-bid: announce, strip conditions, tell holders." },

{ id:"tc5x-008", lesson:"TC5A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lantern Bank lends Mr Kwok $40m on ordinary arm's-length terms, secured by a charge over his 32% stake in Robin Ltd. The charge gives Lantern no voting rights unless Mr Kwok defaults.",
  prompt:"Has Lantern acquired relevant shares under Note 13 by taking the charge?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: taking security in the normal course of banking isn't normally caught", correct:true,
     why:"Note 13 on Rule 14.1 deems acquiring 'voting rights or general control of them, as distinct from the shares themselves' to be an acquisition of relevant shares, but adds: 'This will not normally apply in the case of a bank taking security over shares in the normal course of its business (see also Note 2 of the Notes on Dispensation from Rule 14).'\n\nLantern's charge is ordinary lending security, and it doesn't give Lantern voting control unless Mr Kwok defaults. Taking the charge isn't an acquisition for Rule 14.\n\nIf Mr Kwok defaults and Lantern enforces, taking the shares or control of their votes, Dispensation Note 2 is relevant: the Council normally waives the offer obligation for a lender enforcing arm's-length, ordinary-course security, provided the security wasn't given when the lender had reason to believe enforcement was likely. If Lantern then sold the 32% block, the buyer would be subject to Rule 14 in the normal way."},
    {t:"Yes: a charge over 32% of the shares gives the bank effective control", correct:false,
     why:"Note 13 says the deemed acquisition 'will not normally apply' to a bank taking security in the normal course of business. A charge with no voting rights before default isn't control."},
    {t:"Yes, unless Mr Kwok's own stake is below 30%", correct:false,
     why:"The size of the stake doesn't change the analysis. Ordinary-course bank security is normally outside Note 13."},
    {t:"No, and enforcing the security can never trigger an offer for the bank", correct:false,
     why:"Enforcement is dealt with separately. Dispensation Note 2 normally waives the bank's obligation on enforcement, but only on conditions, including that the security wasn't given when enforcement was likely."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 13; Dispensation Note 2", takeaway:"Ordinary bank security: not an acquisition." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t3p3/TC5B.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T5 · Lesson TC5B Concert Parties & Creeping Control (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code r 14.1 and Notes 1–7 on Rule 14.1. Replaces TC5B in takeoverCode5.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc5-014", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Over several years Orchid Holdings built an 18% stake in Plover Ltd, and Mr Lim, independently, built 16%. On 1 March 2026 they agree to co-operate to consolidate control of Plover. In April Orchid buys 0.6% and in June Mr Lim buys 0.5%.",
  prompt:"What is the Rule 14 position?",
  options:[
    {t:"None on joining, but the June purchase adds 1.1% in 6 months at 34%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 14.1: 'Where a party has acquired shares independently of other shareholders ... but subsequently joins them to co-operate to obtain or consolidate effective control of a company, the Council would not normally require a general offer to be made under Rule 14.1 even if their existing aggregate shareholdings amount to 30% or more of the voting rights of that company. However, once such parties have joined together, the provisions of Rule 14.1 would apply so that: ... (b) if the combined shareholdings amounted to between 30% and 50% ..., no member of that group could acquire shares which would result in aggregate acquisitions by the group amounting to more than 1% of the voting rights of the company in any 6-month period without incurring a similar obligation.'\n\nOrchid (18%) and Mr Lim (16%) built their stakes independently, so coming together at 34% on 1 March doesn't trigger an offer. From then on they're one group in the 30–50% band. April (0.6%) plus June (0.5%) is 1.1% within six months, more than 1%, so the June purchase triggers a Rule 14.1(b) offer. The Code doesn't punish the act of joining, but it doesn't let a newly formed group creep up without limit either."},
    {t:"An offer from 1 March, when the members first came together at 34%", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 says the Council 'would not normally require a general offer' merely because independent holders join together, even at 30% or more. The obligation comes from later acquisitions."},
    {t:"No offer: each member of the group bought less than 1%", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1(b) refers to 'aggregate acquisitions by the group'. Purchases by different members are added together within the 6-month window."},
    {t:"No offer until the group's holding goes above 50%", correct:false,
     why:"The group is in the 30–50% band, where the 1% creeper applies. Crossing 50% isn't the trigger."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(b); r 14.1, Note 1(b)", takeaway:"Join freely; then creep as one." },

{ id:"tc5x-020", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ms Ho, who built her 18% of Quail Ltd independently, agrees with Mr Yeo, who independently holds 9%, to work together to gain control of Quail. A month later Mr Yeo buys 3.5% in the market.",
  prompt:"What is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"An offer is due: the group was at 27% and is now at 30.5%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1(a) on Rule 14.1: once independent holders join together, 'if the combined shareholdings amounted to less than 30% of the voting rights of that company, an obligation to make an offer would arise if any member of that group acquired further shares such that the group's aggregate holdings of voting rights reached 30% or more'.\n\nAt 18% + 9% = 27%, the group was below 30% when it formed, so no offer was due then. Mr Yeo's 3.5% purchase takes the group's aggregate to 30.5%, crossing the threshold. Under Rule 14.1, the obligation falls on Mr Yeo as the acquirer, and 'each of the principal members of the group ... may, according to the circumstances of the case, have the obligation'. Ms Ho, as the larger holder and likely leader, could be required to make the offer too. The announcement must be made within 30 minutes, or a trading halt requested (Note on Rule 3)."},
    {t:"No offer: neither member holds 30% alone", correct:false,
     why:"Once they act in concert, their holdings are aggregated. Note 1(a) asks whether 'the group's aggregate holdings' reach 30%."},
    {t:"No offer, because they formed the group while below 30%", correct:false,
     why:"Forming the group below 30% avoids an obligation at the moment of joining. Later acquisitions that take the group to 30% or more still trigger Rule 14.1."},
    {t:"Only Mr Yeo can be required to make the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1 says 'each of the principal members of the group' may have the obligation, depending on the circumstances. Ms Ho could be required to make it."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1; r 14.1, Note 1(a)", takeaway:"Group formed under 30%: crossing 30% triggers." },

{ id:"tc5-015", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Koh holds 27% of Ibis Ltd. At his request, and with money he lends her, his business partner Ms Ng buys 4% of Ibis.",
  prompt:"What is the likely Rule 14 consequence?",
  options:[
    {t:"Ms Ng acts in concert with him, so at 31% an offer is due, which he can be made to make", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) applies where 'any person acquires ... shares which (taken together with shares held or acquired by persons acting in concert with him) carry 30% or more of the voting rights'. Rule 14.1 adds that 'each of the principal members of the group of persons acting in concert with him may, according to the circumstances of the case, have the obligation to extend an offer'.\n\nMs Ng bought at Mr Koh's request and with his money. That is co-operation through the acquisition of shares to obtain or consolidate control, which is the definition of acting in concert (Defn 1). Several presumptions point the same way: a partner (presumption (g)), a person accustomed to act on the individual's instructions ((h)(iv)), and a person providing financial assistance ((h)(vi), which here runs in reverse but shows the link). The group's 27% + 4% = 31% crosses 30%. Mr Koh, as the principal member, can be required to make the offer even though he bought nothing himself."},
    {t:"None: Mr Koh hasn't bought any shares himself", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1(a) counts 'shares held or acquired by persons acting in concert with him'. Ms Ng's 4% counts towards the group's total."},
    {t:"Only Ms Ng must make an offer, since she made the acquisition", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 14.1 allows the Council to require 'each of the principal members of the group' to make the offer. Mr Koh, who directed and funded the purchase, is the obvious principal."},
    {t:"An offer only if Ms Ng votes with Mr Koh at more than one general meeting of the company", correct:false,
     why:"Voting patterns are one indication of concert (Note 2). Here the request and the funding already show co-operation, so no voting evidence is needed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1(a); Defn 1", takeaway:"Your concert party's shares are your shares." },

{ id:"tc5-016", lesson:"TC5B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Two shareholders who vote the same way on a resolution at one general meeting are, for that reason alone, acting in concert for Rule 14.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Shareholders voting together': 'The Council will not normally regard the action of shareholders voting together on particular resolutions at one general meeting as action which of itself should lead to an offer obligation. Such voting pattern at more than one general meeting may, however, be taken into account as one indication that the shareholders are acting in concert.'\n\nShareholders often vote the same way because they reach the same view independently. Treating that as concert would chill normal shareholder engagement. But a consistent pattern over several meetings is evidence the Council can weigh, alongside other factors, in deciding whether there is an agreement or understanding. Note 3 is an important exception: shareholders who requisition a board control-seeking proposal are presumed to act in concert 'notwithstanding Note 2'."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says voting together at one meeting won't normally, of itself, lead to an offer obligation. A pattern across several meetings is only 'one indication' of concert."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Notes 2–3", takeaway:"One meeting: no. A pattern: maybe." },

{ id:"tc5x-021", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two funds, holding 17% and 15% of Rook Ltd, have voted identically on every resolution at Rook's last four AGMs and EGMs, including against the board's nominees. They say they reach their views separately. Neither has requisitioned anything.",
  prompt:"How will the Council approach this?",
  options:[
    {t:"One meeting isn't enough, but a pattern over several may be one sign of concert", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2 on Rule 14.1 says that voting together 'at one general meeting' won't normally of itself lead to an offer obligation, but 'Such voting pattern at more than one general meeting may, however, be taken into account as one indication that the shareholders are acting in concert.'\n\nFour meetings of identical votes, including against the board's own nominees, is a pattern the Council may weigh. It isn't conclusive. The test remains whether there's an agreement or understanding to co-operate to obtain or consolidate control (Defn 1). The Council will look for other evidence: communications between the funds, shared advisers, coordinated purchases, or common plans for the board. Under Note 2 on the definition of acting in concert, both funds must disclose all relevant information if the Council investigates, and failure to do so may lead to an inference of concert. If they are found to act in concert, their combined 32% puts them in the 30–50% band, so the 1% creeper would apply to further purchases."},
    {t:"They are automatically acting in concert after voting together at four meetings", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 treats a pattern as 'one indication', not as proof. The Council needs to find an agreement or understanding."},
    {t:"Voting is never relevant to whether parties act in concert", correct:false,
     why:"Note 2 says a pattern over more than one meeting 'may ... be taken into account'. It's relevant evidence."},
    {t:"Only requisitioning a meeting together can make them a concert party", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3's presumption for requisitions is one route. A finding of concert can also be based on other evidence, including voting patterns."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 2; Defn 1, Note 2", takeaway:"Repeated identical votes: one piece of evidence." },

{ id:"tc5-017", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three funds holding 11%, 9% and 8% of Larch Ltd agree to requisition an EGM to replace four of Larch's seven directors. Two of the candidates are former partners of the largest fund, which will pay them a fee if they are appointed. A week after the agreement, the 9% fund buys a further 2.5%.",
  prompt:"What is the Rule 14 position?",
  options:[
    {t:"In concert from the agreement, so the purchase takes them past 30%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 14.1: 'Notwithstanding Note 2 above, the Council will normally presume shareholders who requisition or threaten to requisition the consideration of a board control-seeking proposal at a general meeting, together with their supporters as at the date of requisition or threat, to be acting in concert with each other and with the proposed directors. Such parties will be presumed to be acting in concert once an agreement or understanding is reached ... with the result that subsequent acquisitions of interests in shares by any member of the group could give rise to an obligation to make a general offer'.\n\nIs the proposal board control-seeking? Factor (a): two candidates are former partners of a proposing fund and will be paid by it, which is a prior relationship, an arrangement, and remuneration. That relationship isn't insignificant. Factor (b): four of seven would be a majority, which is 'normally ... board control-seeking'.\n\nSo the presumption applies from the agreement. The group holds 11% + 9% + 8% = 28%. The 2.5% purchase takes it to 30.5%, which triggers Rule 14.1(a)."},
    {t:"Nothing: agreeing how to vote at one meeting isn't concert action", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 applies 'Notwithstanding Note 2'. Requisitioning a board control-seeking proposal is treated differently from ordinary voting."},
    {t:"The presumption runs only from the date of the EGM itself", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 says the parties are presumed to act in concert 'once an agreement or understanding is reached between them', which was a week before the purchase."},
    {t:"No offer: no individual fund holds anywhere near 30%", correct:false,
     why:"Once presumed to act in concert, their holdings are aggregated. The group crosses 30% with the purchase."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 3", takeaway:"Board-control requisition: concert party from the deal." },

{ id:"tc5-018", lesson:"TC5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In each proposal below, a proposing shareholder has a relationship with a candidate that is not insignificant. Which proposal is normally NOT board control-seeking?",
  options:[
    {t:"Replacing one of nine directors", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3(b) on Rule 14.1: 'If it is proposed to appoint or replace only one director, the proposal will not normally be considered to be board control-seeking. If it is proposed to replace the entire board, or if the implementation of the proposal would result in the proposed directors representing a majority of the directors on the board, the proposal will normally be considered to be board control-seeking. If, however, the implementation of the proposal would not result in the proposed directors representing a majority ..., the proposal will not normally be considered to be board control-seeking unless an analysis of the factors set out at paragraphs (c) to (f) below would indicate otherwise'.\n\nSo there are three bands. One director: normally not control-seeking. A majority, or the whole board: normally control-seeking. In between: normally not, unless factors (c) to (f) point the other way. Those factors are the board positions involved, the candidates' mandate, any benefit to the proposers beyond their shareholding, and relationships with the existing directors."},
    {t:"Replacing the entire board", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3(b) says that replacing the entire board 'will normally be considered to be board control-seeking'."},
    {t:"Replacing three directors of a five-member board", correct:false,
     why:"Three of five would be a majority. Note 3(b) treats proposals that would give the proposed directors a majority as normally board control-seeking."},
    {t:"Any of them, since a relationship exists", correct:false,
     why:"A relationship that isn't insignificant only opens the analysis. The proposal 'may be considered' board control-seeking depending on factor (b) and, if appropriate, factors (c) to (f)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 3(a)–(b)", takeaway:"One director: not normally control-seeking." },

{ id:"tc5x-022", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholders holding 20% propose to replace two of Martin Ltd's seven directors: the chairman and the CEO. One candidate is a long-time business associate of the lead shareholder. The candidates' published mandate is to sell Martin's main division to a company the lead shareholder controls.",
  prompt:"Is the proposal likely to be board control-seeking?",
  options:[
    {t:"Possibly: two of seven isn't a majority, but factors (c) to (f) may point the other way", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 starts with factor (a), the relationship. A long-time business associate is a prior relationship that isn't insignificant, so the analysis continues. Factor (b): two of seven isn't a majority, so the proposal 'will not normally be considered to be board control-seeking unless an analysis of the factors set out at paragraphs (c) to (f) below would indicate otherwise'.\n\nThose factors weigh heavily here:\n(c) 'the board positions held by the directors being replaced and to be held by the proposed directors': the chairman and CEO are the two most powerful seats.\n(d) 'the nature of the mandate, if any': the candidates are committed to selling the main division.\n(e) 'whether any of the proposing shareholders ... will benefit, either directly or indirectly, as a result of the implementation of the proposal other than through its interest in shares': the buyer is a company the lead shareholder controls.\n\nTaken together, the proposal may well be board control-seeking despite being a minority of seats. If so, the proposers and candidates are presumed to act in concert from their agreement, and further acquisitions taking the group to 30% would trigger an offer."},
    {t:"No: two seats out of seven is a minority of the board, so it can never be control-seeking", correct:false,
     why:"A minority proposal 'will not normally' be control-seeking 'unless an analysis of the factors set out at paragraphs (c) to (f) ... would indicate otherwise'. Here those factors point strongly the other way."},
    {t:"Yes, automatically, because there's a relationship", correct:false,
     why:"A relationship that isn't insignificant opens the analysis but doesn't decide it. The factors in (b) to (f) must be applied."},
    {t:"No, because the group holds only 20% of the voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3's test is about the proposal, not the group's percentage. The holding matters later, when acquisitions could take the group to 30%."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 3(a)–(f)", takeaway:"Minority of seats can still be control-seeking." },

{ id:"tc5-019", lesson:"TC5B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: If there is no relationship, or only an insignificant one, between the proposed directors and the proposing shareholders or their supporters, the proposal is not board control-seeking and the Council need not consider the other factors.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 14.1: 'If, on this analysis, there is no relationship between any of the proposed directors and any of the proposing shareholders or their supporters, or if any such relationship is insignificant, the proposal will not be considered to be board control-seeking such that the parties will not be presumed to be acting in concert and it will not be necessary for the factors set out at paragraphs (b) to (f) below to be considered.'\n\nThe relationship factor in (a) is the gateway. It covers '(i) whether there is or has been any prior relationship ...; (ii) whether there are any agreements, arrangements or understandings ... with regard to their proposed appointment; and (iii) whether any of the proposed directors will be remunerated in any way by any of the proposing shareholders'. If shareholders propose genuinely independent candidates, with no links and no payments, the proposal isn't treated as a control grab, however many seats are involved. This protects ordinary governance activism, such as investors proposing independent directors to improve a board."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 says expressly that without a relationship, or with only an insignificant one, the proposal isn't board control-seeking and factors (b) to (f) needn't be considered."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 3(a)", takeaway:"No relationship, no presumption." },

{ id:"tc5x-023", lesson:"TC5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A group of requisitioning shareholders was presumed to act in concert under Note 3. Their nominees have now been appointed. Which factor will the Council consider in deciding whether they are no longer acting in concert?",
  options:[
    {t:"All of them, including its objective, any board fight and links with new directors", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 14.1: 'In determining whether it is appropriate for such parties to be held no longer to be acting in concert, the Council will take account of a number of factors, including the following: (a) whether the parties have been successful in achieving their stated objective; (b) whether there is any evidence to indicate that the parties should continue to be held to be acting in concert; (c) whether there is any evidence of an ongoing struggle between the proposing shareholders, or their supporters, and the board of the company; (d) the types of proposing shareholders involved and the relationship between them; and (e) the relationship between the proposing shareholders, or their supporters, and the proposed/new directors.'\n\nOnce a campaign succeeds, the group may genuinely disband: institutional investors who came together on a single governance issue often go their own ways. But if the struggle with the board continues, or the new directors remain tied to the proposers, the concert relationship may continue too. Compare Note 3 on the definition of acting in concert: once a group has been ruled or admitted to be acting in concert, the Council requires 'clear evidence to the contrary' to rule that it no longer is."},
    {t:"Only whether the group's resolutions were won at the EGM", correct:false,
     why:"Success in achieving the objective is factor (a), but the Council also looks at continuing evidence of concert, any ongoing struggle, the types of shareholders, and their links to the new directors."},
    {t:"Only whether they have publicly announced that the group has now disbanded", correct:false,
     why:"An announcement alone isn't a listed factor. The Council looks at evidence, consistent with the general rule that breaking up a concert party needs clear evidence."},
    {t:"Nothing: requisitioning shareholders remain in concert permanently", correct:false,
     why:"Note 3 sets out factors for deciding when such parties should 'be held no longer to be acting in concert'. The status isn't permanent."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 3; Defn 1, Note 3", takeaway:"Ending a requisition concert: objective met, links cut." },

{ id:"tc5-020", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Teal Ltd's five directors hold 27% between them. Two weeks into Kestrel's hostile offer for Teal, the chairman buys 3.5% of Teal in the market without consulting the Council.",
  prompt:"What is the problem?",
  options:[
    {t:"Directors are presumed in concert in the offer period, so reaching 30.5% can trigger an offer", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Directors of a company': 'Directors of a company will be presumed to be acting in concert during an offer period or when they have a reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent. In such circumstances, the provisions of this Rule will apply. ... The directors of companies defending against an offer, their advisers and others acting in concert with them should consult the Council before acquiring any voting rights which might lead them to incur an obligation under this Rule.'\n\nThe directors' combined 27% is aggregated during the offer period. The chairman's 3.5% takes the group to 30.5%, crossing 30%. The directors could find themselves obliged to make a mandatory offer for their own company, competing with Kestrel. Buying shares to defend against a bid is also a common way of trying to block it. The chairman should have consulted the Council first. Presumption (f) on acting in concert reaches the same result, and extends to the directors' close relatives, related trusts and controlled companies."},
    {t:"None: directors are free to deal in their own company's shares, subject to disclosure", correct:false,
     why:"That is the position outside an offer period, and even then Rule 14 applies to each director's own holding. During an offer period, Note 4 presumes the directors act in concert."},
    {t:"Only the chairman's own holding is tested", correct:false,
     why:"During the offer period, the directors are presumed to be one group, so their holdings are added together."},
    {t:"The only issue is disclosure under Rule 12", correct:false,
     why:"The dealing must also be disclosed, but the bigger issue is that it can trigger a mandatory offer obligation."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 4; Defn 1(f)", takeaway:"Offer period: the directors are one group." },

{ id:"tc5x-024", lesson:"TC5B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Outside an offer period, and with no reason to believe an offer is imminent, the directors of a company are presumed to be acting in concert in relation to control of their company.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 14.1: 'At other times, directors of a company are not presumed to be acting in concert in relation to control of their company. Subject to the constraints imposed by the Code, particularly the normal application of Rule 14 to the shareholdings which each director controls, directors are free to deal in the shares of their company. The Council reserves the right, however, to examine situations closely should the actions of the directors suggest that they are acting in concert.'\n\nOutside the offer context, directors aren't treated as a bloc. Each director's own holding (with his close relatives, related trusts and controlled companies under presumption (h)) is subject to Rule 14 in the normal way. But if directors are actually coordinating purchases to build up control, the general definition of acting in concert applies, and the Council may find concert on the evidence. Remember also presumption (b): a company is presumed to act in concert with its own directors, which matters when the company itself is buying shares in another company."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 says the presumption applies only 'during an offer period or when they have a reason to believe that a bona fide offer is imminent'. At other times, directors aren't presumed to act in concert, though the Council can examine their actions."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 4", takeaway:"No offer in sight: directors not presumed a group." },

{ id:"tc5-021", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ang Holdings holds 22% of Sora Ltd. Mr Ang Wei, who acts in concert with it, holds 14%. Ang Holdings buys all of Mr Ang Wei's 14% at the market price.",
  prompt:"Does an offer obligation arise?",
  options:[
    {t:"Normally yes, as Ang Holdings alone holds 36%, but it may be waived", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 on Rule 14.1: 'the membership of such groups may change at any time. This being the case, there will be circumstances when the acquisition of voting rights by one member of a group acting in concert from another member will result in the acquirer of the voting rights having an obligation to make an offer. Whenever the holdings of a group acting in concert total 30% or more of the voting rights of a company, an obligation to make an offer will normally arise when, as a result of an acquisition of voting rights from another member of the group, a single member or a sub-group comes to hold 30% or more'.\n\nThe group holds 36%. Ang Holdings, a single member, goes from 22% to 36% by buying from another member. That normally triggers an offer. The Council may waive it, considering '(a) whether the leader of the concert-party group or the largest individual shareholding has changed and whether the balance between the shareholdings in the group has changed significantly; (b) the price paid for the shares acquired; and (c) the relationship between the persons acting in concert and how long they have been acting in concert'. Ang Holdings was already the largest holder and paid market price, which may help. The Note adds that close relatives are treated no differently from unrelated concert parties, except for bequests."},
    {t:"No: the group's total holding is unchanged at 36%, so nothing has changed", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 looks at the individual member, because 'membership of such groups may change at any time'. A single member coming to hold 30% or more normally triggers an offer."},
    {t:"No: transfers between close relatives are exempt", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 says 'concert parties who are close relatives will be treated no differently from unrelated concert parties'. Only a bequest without consideration is normally waived."},
    {t:"Only if Ang Holdings paid more than the market price", correct:false,
     why:"The price paid is a factor in considering a waiver, not a condition of the obligation. The obligation normally arises regardless."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 5", takeaway:"A member crosses 30% inside the group: offer, unless waived." },

{ id:"tc5x-025", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A concert group holds 62% of Wren Ltd. Within the group, Mr Lee holds 22% and buys another 10% from his brother, a fellow member, taking Mr Lee to 32%. The group's total stays at 62%.",
  prompt:"How does Note 5 treat this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Normally none in a group over 50%, but his crossing 30% may count", correct:true,
     why:"Note 5 on Rule 14.1: 'When the group holds over 50%, no obligation normally arises from acquisitions by any member of the group. However, subject to considerations similar to those set out above, the Council may regard as giving rise to an obligation to make an offer any acquisition by a single member or sub-group of the group of voting rights sufficient to increase his/its holding to 30% or more or, if he/it already holds between 30% and 50%, by more than 1% in any 6-month period.'\n\nThe group already has statutory control, so the starting point is no obligation. But Mr Lee individually has moved from 22% to 32%, crossing 30%. If the group later broke up, he would hold effective control on his own. The Council 'may' treat that as triggering an offer, weighing the same factors as for groups between 30% and 50%: whether the leadership or balance of the group has changed, the price paid, and the relationship and how long the parties have acted together. A transfer between brothers is treated no differently from one between unrelated members. Only a bequest without consideration is normally waived."},
    {t:"No obligation: the group already holds over 50% of the votes between them", correct:false,
     why:"That's the normal position, but Note 5 lets the Council treat an individual member's crossing 30% as triggering an offer even in a group above 50%."},
    {t:"An offer is always required whenever a member crosses 30%", correct:false,
     why:"For groups above 50%, Note 5 says the Council 'may' regard it as triggering an offer. It isn't automatic."},
    {t:"No obligation, because transfers between brothers are exempt", correct:false,
     why:"Close relatives 'will be treated no differently from unrelated concert parties'. The exemption is for bequests without consideration."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 5", takeaway:"Over 50% group: watch individuals crossing 30%." },

{ id:"tc5-022", lesson:"TC5B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each concert-group situation to its normal Rule 14 result.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Group at 40%: members buy 1.2% from outsiders within 6 months", r:"Offer obligation"},
    {l:"Group at 40%: a 25% member buys 7% from another member", r:"Offer normally, unless the Council waives"},
    {l:"Group at 60%: members buy 5% from outsiders", r:"No obligation"},
    {l:"Group at 60%: a 20% member buys 11% from another member", r:"Council may treat it as giving rise to an offer"},
    {l:"A member bequeaths his 35% to his children for no consideration", r:"Normally waived"}
  ],
  why:"Note 5 on Rule 14.1 deals with each case.\n\nGroup at 30–50%, buying from outsiders: 'an offer obligation will arise if there are acquisitions from non-members of more than 1% in aggregate in any 6-month period'.\n\nGroup at 30% or more, internal transfer: an obligation 'will normally arise' when 'a single member or a sub-group comes to hold 30% or more or, if already holding between 30% and 50%, to have acquired more than 1%'. The Council may waive, considering changes in leadership and balance, price, and the relationship.\n\nGroup over 50%: 'no obligation normally arises from acquisitions by any member of the group', but the Council 'may regard as giving rise to an obligation' a single member or sub-group crossing 30%, or creeping by more than 1% in 6 months within the 30–50% band.\n\nBequests: 'the Council will normally waive this Rule in the case where a person bequeaths his shareholding in a company to his close relatives without consideration.' Otherwise close relatives are treated like any other concert parties.",
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 5", takeaway:"Watch the individual inside the group." },

{ id:"tc5-023", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ms Tay holds 55% of Wigeon Ltd. Quail Ltd agrees to buy 29% of Wigeon from her, staying under 30%.",
  prompt:"Which term would make it hardest to satisfy the Council that Quail has no significant control over Ms Tay's retained 26%?",
  options:[
    {t:"A very high price, plus a call option over the retained 26%", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 14.1, headed 'Vendor of part only of a shareholding', addresses buyers who stay just under 30%. 'The Council will be concerned to see whether in such circumstances the arrangements between the purchaser and vendor effectively allow the purchaser to exercise a significant degree of control over the retained voting rights, in which case a general offer would normally be required.'\n\nGuidance points:\n'(a) a significant degree of control over the retained voting rights would be less likely if the vendor was not an \"insider\"';\n'(b) the payment of a very high price for the voting rights would tend to suggest that control over the entire holding was being secured';\n'(c) if the parties negotiate options over the retained voting rights it may be more difficult for them to satisfy the Council that a significant degree of control is absent';\n'(d) where the retained voting rights are in themselves a significant part of the company's capital (or ... a significant sum of money in absolute terms) a correspondingly greater element of independence may be presumed'; and\n'(e) it would be natural ... that a purchaser of a substantial holding ... should press for board representation', which alone wouldn't lead to an offer requirement.\n\nA very high price plus a call option combines (b) and (c), the two strongest indicators of control over the whole 55%."},
    {t:"Quail asks Ms Tay to support its nominees for two board seats", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(e) says pressing for board representation is natural for a buyer of a substantial holding and, without other evidence, wouldn't lead the Council to require an offer."},
    {t:"Ms Tay is not an insider", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(a) says control over the retained shares 'would be less likely if the vendor was not an \"insider\"'. That points away from control."},
    {t:"The retained 26% is worth $80m", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6(d) says that where the retained voting rights are significant, including 'a significant sum of money in absolute terms', 'a correspondingly greater element of independence may be presumed'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 6", takeaway:"High price plus options: you've bought the lot." },

{ id:"tc5-024", lesson:"TC5B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In assessing a sale of part only of a holding, the Council looks only at the share purchase agreement between buyer and vendor.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 14.1: 'The Council will also take into account any other transactions between the purchaser and the vendor, and between the purchaser and other members of the group acting in concert with the vendor. This could include, for example, the aggregation of transfers of voting rights to the purchaser over a period of time, or arrangements which have an effect similar to transfer, such as the underwriting by a purchaser of a rights issue which the vendor has agreed not to take up, or a placing of shares with the purchaser.'\n\nA buyer could stay just under 30% in the main purchase and then increase its position quietly: buying a little more from the vendor later, underwriting a rights issue the vendor doesn't take up, or taking a placing of new shares. Each step alone may look small, but together they could hand the buyer control. The Council looks at the whole relationship. The same concerns apply 'when the purchaser is already a member of a group acting in concert with the vendor, or when the purchaser joins such a group'."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 expressly takes into account other transactions between the buyer and the vendor or its concert parties, including staged transfers, underwriting a rights issue the vendor won't take up, and placings."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 6", takeaway:"The whole relationship, not one contract." },

{ id:"tc5x-026", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Chan's family group holds 48% of Swan Ltd. Petrel Ltd buys 25% of Swan from Mr Chan at a 60% premium to market, and agrees to underwrite a rights issue in which the Chan family will not take up its entitlement. After the rights issue Petrel would hold 29.5%.",
  prompt:"How is the Council likely to view this?",
  options:[
    {t:"With concern: the premium and underwriting suggest control of the whole holding", correct:true,
     why:"Note 6 on Rule 14.1 is concerned with buyers who stay under 30% but in fact obtain 'a significant degree of control over the retained voting rights'. The Council looks at the deal as a whole.\n\nTwo features stand out. First, 'the payment of a very high price for the voting rights would tend to suggest that control over the entire holding was being secured'. A 60% premium is very high. Second, the Note lists 'arrangements which have an effect similar to transfer, such as the underwriting by a purchaser of a rights issue which the vendor has agreed not to take up'. Petrel's underwriting would move more of Swan's voting power from the Chans to Petrel.\n\nPetrel would end at 29.5%, just below the 30% trigger. If the Council concludes that Petrel effectively controls the Chans' retained shares, it would normally require a general offer. The parties should consult the Council before proceeding. Note 6 also says these concerns apply where the buyer is or becomes a member of a concert group with the vendor."},
    {t:"No concern: Petrel stays under 30% throughout the arrangement", correct:false,
     why:"Staying under 30% is exactly the situation Note 6 addresses. The question is whether Petrel in substance controls the vendor's retained shares."},
    {t:"No concern: underwriting a rights issue is a normal commercial service for a buyer", correct:false,
     why:"Note 6 expressly names underwriting a rights issue 'which the vendor has agreed not to take up' as an arrangement with an effect similar to a transfer."},
    {t:"Concern only about the premium price, not about the underwriting", correct:false,
     why:"Both features matter. Note 6 names very high prices and underwriting arrangements as relevant to whether control has passed."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 6", takeaway:"Premium + rights underwriting = control by the back door." },

{ id:"tc5-025", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Gannet Pte Ltd, an unlisted company with book NTA of $200m, holds 35% of SGX-listed Dunlin Ltd. Gannet's Dunlin stake has a market value of $160m. Merganser Ltd acquires 60% of Gannet.",
  prompt:"Does Merganser have to make an offer for Dunlin?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably: it controls Dunlin via Gannet, and Dunlin is 80% of Gannet's NTA; consult", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 on Rule 14.1, headed 'The chain principle': 'Occasionally, a person or group of persons acting in concert to acquire statutory control of a company (which need not be a company to which the Code applies) will thereby acquire or consolidate effective control, as defined in the Code, of a second company because the first company itself holds ... a controlling interest in the second company ... The Council will not normally require an offer to be made ... unless the second company constitutes or contributes significantly to the first company in the following aspects: (a) assets; (b) market capitalisation (where the first and second companies are listed); (c) sales; or (d) earnings.'\n\nFor unlisted first companies: 'the pro-rated book NTA or market value of shares in the second company held by the unlisted first company is compared against the first company's book NTA'. Gannet's Dunlin stake is worth $160m against Gannet's $200m book NTA, which is 80%. That's highly significant.\n\nMerganser acquired statutory control of Gannet (60%), and through Gannet effective control of Dunlin (35%). 'The Council should be consulted in all cases which may come within the scope of this Note.'"},
    {t:"No: the chain principle doesn't apply where the first company is an unlisted company", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says the first company 'need not be a company to which the Code applies', and gives a test specifically for unlisted first companies."},
    {t:"No: Merganser didn't buy any Dunlin shares itself", correct:false,
     why:"The chain principle exists precisely to catch indirect acquisitions of control. Merganser controls Dunlin through Gannet."},
    {t:"Yes, whatever Dunlin's significance is to Gannet's business", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says an offer isn't normally required 'unless the second company constitutes or contributes significantly to the first company'. Significance matters. Here it's very high."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 7", takeaway:"Buy the parent, maybe owe the child." },

{ id:"tc5x-027", lesson:"TC5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Heron Ltd, a large listed conglomerate, holds 32% of listed Ibis Ltd. Ibis accounts for about 3% of Heron's assets, sales, earnings and market capitalisation. Kite Holdings acquires 55% of Heron.",
  prompt:"Will Kite normally have to make an offer for Ibis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not normally, as Ibis isn't significant to Heron; but it should consult", correct:true,
     why:"Note 7 on Rule 14.1 says the Council 'will not normally require an offer to be made under this Rule in these circumstances unless the second company constitutes or contributes significantly to the first company' in assets, market capitalisation (where both are listed), sales or earnings.\n\nKite has acquired statutory control of Heron (55%), and through Heron effective control of Ibis (32%). But Ibis is only about 3% of Heron on every measure. Kite was buying Heron's wider business, not Ibis, and Ibis was incidental to the deal. The rationale for the chain principle (stopping someone gaining control of a target indirectly, by buying its parent) doesn't really apply. So an offer for Ibis isn't normally required.\n\nThe Note still says 'The Council should be consulted in all cases which may come within the scope of this Note'. If Ibis were a large part of Heron, for example 40% of earnings, the answer could be different."},
    {t:"Yes: Kite has acquired effective control of Ibis indirectly, through Heron", correct:false,
     why:"Acquiring effective control indirectly is necessary, but Note 7 adds a significance test. Where the second company isn't significant to the first, an offer isn't normally required."},
    {t:"No, and there's no need to consult the Council about it", correct:false,
     why:"Note 7 says the Council 'should be consulted in all cases which may come within the scope of this Note'. That includes cases that are likely to be clear."},
    {t:"Yes, because Heron and Ibis are both listed on the Exchange", correct:false,
     why:"Listing determines which measures apply (market capitalisation is used only where both are listed), not whether an offer is required."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 14.1, Note 7", takeaway:"Small child, big parent: chain offer not normally needed." }

]);

