window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p4v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p4v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc7-013", lesson:"TC7B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Rule 23.2 lists the offeror's intentions that the offer document should cover. Which item is not on the list?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its intended dividend policy for its own shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.2: 'An offeror should cover the following points in the offer document: (a) its intentions regarding the business of the offeree company; (b) its intentions regarding any major changes to be introduced in the business, including any redeployment of the fixed assets of the offeree company; (c) the long-term commercial justification for the proposed offer; and (d) its intentions with regard to the continued employment of the employees of the offeree company and of its subsidiaries.'\n\nThe list is about the offeree: its business, changes, why the offeror wants it, and what happens to its employees. Shareholders deciding whether to sell, and particularly those who might stay on as a minority, need to know what the offeror plans. The offeree board must then comment on these statements under Rule 24.2. The offeror's own dividend policy isn't one of the Rule 23.2 items, though in a securities exchange offer the offeror's financial information under Rules 23.4 and 23.12 will be relevant."},
    {t:"Its intentions for the offeree's employees", correct:false,
     why:"That's Rule 23.2(d): 'its intentions with regard to the continued employment of the employees of the offeree company and of its subsidiaries'."},
    {t:"The long-term commercial justification for the offer", correct:false,
     why:"That's Rule 23.2(c)."},
    {t:"Any planned redeployment of the offeree's fixed assets", correct:false,
     why:"That's within Rule 23.2(b): 'any major changes ... including any redeployment of the fixed assets'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.2; r 24.2", takeaway:"Business, changes, justification, employees." },

{ id:"tc7-014", lesson:"TC7B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mink Ltd makes a voluntary offer for Sable Ltd. The offer period began on 1 July. A Mink concert party bought Sable shares on 15 February and again on 20 May. Mink itself bought on 10 August, before posting its offer document.",
  prompt:"Which dealings must the offer document detail under Rule 23.3?",
  options:[
    {t:"The 20 May and 10 August dealings; 15 February is outside the 3-month window", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.3: 'If any party whose shareholdings are required by this Rule to be disclosed has dealt for value in the shares in question during the period commencing 6 months (3 months in the case of a voluntary offer) prior to the beginning of the offer period and ending with the latest practicable date prior to the posting of the offer document, the details, including dates and prices, must be stated. If no such deals have been made, this fact should be stated.'\n\nThis is a voluntary offer, so the window starts 3 months before 1 July, on 1 April, and runs to the latest practicable date before posting.\n• 15 February: before 1 April. Outside.\n• 20 May: inside. Concert parties' holdings must be disclosed under Rule 23.3(c), so their dealings are covered.\n• 10 August: inside, in the offer period before posting.\n\nFor a mandatory offer, the window would start 6 months before, and the February purchase would be in. The window matches the price look-back in Rule 15.2 (3 months) and Rule 14.3 (6 months)."},
    {t:"All three dealings, with their dates and prices", correct:false,
     why:"For a voluntary offer, the look-back is 3 months before the offer period. 15 February is outside it."},
    {t:"Only Mink's own dealing, not its concert parties'", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.3(c) requires disclosure of concert parties' shareholdings, and their dealings in the window must be detailed too."},
    {t:"None, because all of the dealings were already disclosed at the time under Rule 12", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 12 disclosures don't replace Rule 23.3. The offer document must set out the dealings, with dates and prices, or say there were none."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.3; r 15.2; r 14.3", takeaway:"Dealings: 6 months back, or 3 for voluntary offers." },

{ id:"tc7-015", lesson:"TC7B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under Rule 23.3(f), what must the offer document state about the offeree shares held by the offeror and its concert parties?",
  options:[
    {t:"The number and % charged, borrowed or lent by it and its concert parties", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.3(f) requires 'the number and percentage of any shareholdings in the offeree company which the offeror or any person acting in concert with it has: (i) granted a security interest to another person, whether through a charge, pledge or otherwise; (ii) borrowed from another person (excluding borrowed shares which have been on-lent or sold); or (iii) lent to another person.' And: 'If in any of the above categories there are no shareholdings, this fact should be stated.'\n\nThis repeats in the offer document what Rule 3.5(f) required in the firm-intention announcement. Charged shares may be enforced; lent shares may not be available to vote or tender; borrowed shares can inflate apparent voting power. Shareholders need the real picture of the offeror group's position. The nil-statement requirement means silence can't be read as 'none'."},
    {t:"Only the shares the offeror itself has charged or pledged to its own bank", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.3(f) also covers borrowed and lent shares, and those of concert parties."},
    {t:"Only the shares it has lent to other parties", correct:false,
     why:"Charged and borrowed shares are covered too."},
    {t:"Nothing, unless the offeree board specifically asks for it", correct:false,
     why:"Disclosure is mandatory, and nil statements are required where there are none."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.3(f); r 3.5(f)", takeaway:"Charged, borrowed, lent: count and percentage." },

{ id:"tc7x-020", lesson:"TC7B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: If no person who must be disclosed under Rule 23.3 holds any offeree shares, or none has dealt in the window, the offer document can simply say nothing about it.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.3 says: 'If in any of the above categories there are no shareholdings, this fact should be stated.' And for dealings: 'If no such deals have been made, this fact should be stated.'\n\nNil statements matter because shareholders can't tell the difference between 'none' and 'forgot to mention' if the document is silent. A positive statement that, for example, the offeror's directors hold no offeree shares and no concert party has dealt in the window, is subject to the directors' responsibility statement under Rule 8.3, so it has to be checked. The same approach appears in Note 1 on Rule 8.1, which requires later documents to state that there have been no material changes if that is the case. By contrast, Rule 24.3(b) relaxes the nil-statement requirement for some offeree board disclosures."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.3 requires a statement where there are no shareholdings in a category, and a statement where no deals have been made."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.3; r 8.1, Note 1; r 8.3", takeaway:"State the nil." },

{ id:"tc7-016", lesson:"TC7B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Where the offer document refers to irrevocable commitments to accept, it must make clear any circumstances in which they cease to be binding, such as a higher offer being made.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 23.3: 'References to irrevocable commitments to accept an offer must make it clear if there are circumstances in which they cease to be binding, for example, if a higher offer is made.' Rule 23.3(d) requires disclosure of the shareholdings of 'any persons who, prior to the posting of the offer document, have irrevocably committed themselves to accepting the offer together with the names of such persons'. And Rule 23.6 says 'Any document evidencing an irrevocable undertaking to accept the offer should be made available for inspection.'\n\nThe same disclosure appeared at the firm-intention stage under Note 3 on Rule 3.5. Shareholders, and potential rival bidders, need to know whether the undertakings are hard or soft. A soft irrevocable that falls away on a 10% higher offer invites competition; a hard one makes a rival's task much harder."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 on Rule 23.3 requires references to irrevocables to make clear when they cease to be binding."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.3(d), Note 4; r 23.6; r 3.5, Note 3", takeaway:"Irrevocables: say when they stop binding." },

{ id:"tc7-017", lesson:"TC7B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wren Capital's all-cash offer document includes its bank's unconditional confirmation that Wren has resources to satisfy full acceptance. The bank reviewed Wren's committed facilities and cash balances carefully. At completion, a facility lender unexpectedly fails to fund.",
  prompt:"Is the bank expected to produce the cash?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not if it acted responsibly and took all reasonable steps to check the cash", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.8: 'Where the offer is for cash or includes an element of cash, the offer document must include an unconditional confirmation by an appropriate third party (e.g. the offeror's banker or financial adviser) that resources are available to the offeror sufficient to satisfy full acceptance of the offer. The party confirming that resources are available will not be expected to produce the cash itself if, in giving the confirmation, it acted responsibly and took all reasonable steps to assure itself that the cash would be available to satisfy full acceptance of the offer. The Council reserves the right to require evidence to support such confirmation.'\n\nThe confirmer isn't a guarantor. Its duty is diligence: checking the facilities, cash and any conditions. If it did that, an unexpected funding failure isn't its liability. But if it had confirmed carelessly, for instance without checking that facilities were committed, it could be expected to produce the cash. This makes confirmers take the job seriously, which protects shareholders. Rule 3.5 requires the same confirmation in the firm-intention announcement."},
    {t:"Yes, always: the cash confirmation is effectively a guarantee given by the bank", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.8 says the confirmer 'will not be expected to produce the cash itself' if it acted responsibly and took all reasonable steps."},
    {t:"No, never: giving the confirmation carries no risk for the bank", correct:false,
     why:"The protection depends on the confirmer having 'acted responsibly and took all reasonable steps'. A careless confirmer may be expected to produce the cash."},
    {t:"Only if the Council orders it to pay compensation to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.8 sets its own standard for when the confirmer is expected to produce the cash, based on whether it acted responsibly."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.8; r 3.5", takeaway:"Cash confirmer: careful, not a guarantor." },

{ id:"tc7-018", lesson:"TC7B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Finch Bidco, a newly formed subsidiary of Finch Group, makes an all-cash offer. What financial information does Rule 23.4 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"The prescribed offeror information, normally for the ultimate parent", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.4: 'Except with the Council's consent, the offer document must contain the following information about the offeror, whether the offer consideration is securities or cash', including directors, principal activities, three years of turnover, profit, EPS and dividends, the last audited balance sheet, material changes since, interim figures, accounting policies and comparability changes. It must also state 'whether or not there has been, within the knowledge of the offeror, any material change in the financial position or prospects of the offeree company'.\n\nThe Note on Rule 23.4: 'If the offeror is a subsidiary, its ultimate holding company will normally have to disclose the prescribed information on a consolidated basis in the offer document. The Council may dispense with this requirement if the subsidiary in question is of sufficient substance in relation to the group and the offer.'\n\nA newly formed bid vehicle has no history, so its own figures would tell shareholders nothing. The group's figures show who really stands behind the offer. Compare Note 4 on Rule 8.3, which requires the parent's directors to take responsibility for the vehicle's documents."},
    {t:"None, because the offer is all cash and needs no offeror information", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.4 applies 'whether the offer consideration is securities or cash'. Only Rule 23.7 (directors' emoluments) has a cash exception."},
    {t:"Only Finch Bidco's own accounts, as the offeror", correct:false,
     why:"A new vehicle's accounts say little. The Note normally requires the ultimate holding company's consolidated information."},
    {t:"Only a confirmation that cash resources are available", correct:false,
     why:"The cash confirmation under Rule 23.8 is required too, but it doesn't replace Rule 23.4's financial information."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.4; r 23.4, Note; r 8.3, Note 4", takeaway:"Cash or paper: offeror financials, often the parent's." },

{ id:"tc7-019", lesson:"TC7B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each offer document requirement to what it involves.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Rule 23.1", r:"Prominent advice to consult a professional adviser if in doubt"},
    {l:"Rule 23.5", r:"Price, all conditions, last unconditional day, compulsory acquisition intention"},
    {l:"Rule 23.11", r:"Securities offered for, cum or ex dividend"},
    {l:"Rule 23.13", r:"Shareholders' rights under Rules 18, 22 and 28 written into the document"}
  ],
  why:"Rule 23.1: every offer document must display prominently: 'If you are in doubt about this offer you should consult your stockbroker, bank manager, solicitor or other professional adviser.'\n\nRule 23.5: the document 'must set out clearly: (a) the price or other considerations to be paid for the securities; (b) all conditions attached to acceptances, in particular whether the offer is conditional upon acceptances being received in respect of a minimum number and the last day on which the offer can become unconditional as to acceptances; and (c) a statement whether or not the offeror intends to avail itself of powers of compulsory acquisition'.\n\nRule 23.11: 'details of the securities for which the offer is made and a statement whether they are to be acquired cum or ex any dividend or other distribution which has been or may be declared'.\n\nRule 23.13: 'The obligations of the offeror and the rights of the offeree company shareholders under Rules 18, 22 and 28 must be specifically incorporated in the offer document.' That covers comparable offers, the timetable and acceptances, which then become contractual terms of the offer.",
  cite:"SG Code r 23.1, 23.5, 23.11, 23.13", takeaway:"The document states its own ground rules." },

{ id:"tc7x-021", lesson:"TC7B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An offeror has agreed to pay Kestrel Ltd's chairman a $500,000 'transition fee' when its offer succeeds, and to give the CEO a new 3-year contract with the offeror's group.",
  prompt:"What must the offer document disclose about these arrangements?",
  options:[
    {t:"Full details of any deal with offeree directors linked to the offer", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.6(a) requires every offer document to include 'a statement as to whether or not any agreement, arrangement or understanding exists between the offeror or any person acting in concert with it and any of the directors, or recent directors, shareholders or recent shareholders of the offeree company having any connection with or dependence upon the offer, and full particulars of any such agreement, arrangement or understanding'.\n\nNote 1 on Rule 23.6 adds that information must be given on '(a) details of any payment or other benefit which will be made or given to any director of the offeree company ... as compensation for loss of office or otherwise in connection with the offer; and (b) details of any agreement or arrangement made between the offeror and any of the directors of the offeree company or any other person in connection with or conditional upon the outcome of the offer'.\n\nShareholders need this to judge whether the board's recommendation is affected by personal interests (GP 13). The chairman and CEO would normally be exempted from responsibility for the board's recommendation (Note 1 on Rule 8.3), and the arrangements may need scrutiny under Rule 10 if they give a shareholder-director a favourable deal."},
    {t:"Nothing: directors' employment terms are a private matter", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.6 and its Note 1 require full particulars of arrangements with offeree directors connected with the offer."},
    {t:"Only the chairman's fee, because it's a cash payment to him", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1(b) covers 'any agreement or arrangement made between the offeror and any of the directors ... in connection with or conditional upon the outcome of the offer'. The CEO's contract is caught."},
    {t:"Only if the directors themselves hold shares in the offeree", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.6 applies to directors whether or not they hold shares. Shareholding may bring in Rule 10 as well."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.6, Note 1; r 8.3, Note 1; GP 13", takeaway:"Side deals with directors: disclose in full." },

{ id:"tc7-020", lesson:"TC7B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An offeror offering solely cash need not state in the offer document how its directors' emoluments will be affected by acquiring the offeree.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.7: 'The offer documents should, except in the case of an offeror offering solely cash, contain a statement (if appropriate, a negative statement) indicating whether and in what manner the emoluments of the directors of the offeror will be affected by the acquisition of the offeree company, or any other associated relevant transaction.' It adds that alterations to fixed amounts and the effect on commissions or variable amounts should be included, and grouping or aggregating directors' figures is normally acceptable.\n\nWhy the cash exception? In a securities exchange offer, offeree shareholders become offeror shareholders. They have an interest in whether the offeror's directors will be paid more as a result of the deal. In a cash offer, they sell and leave, so the offeror's directors' pay doesn't affect them. It's one of the few cash-offer exceptions in Rule 23; most of the Rule, including financial information under Rule 23.4, applies whatever the consideration."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.7 expressly excepts 'the case of an offeror offering solely cash'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.7; r 23.4", takeaway:"All-cash offer: no emoluments statement." },

{ id:"tc7-021", lesson:"TC7B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which closing price of the offeree's shares is not required by Rule 23.10?",
  options:[
    {t:"The closing price on the first closing date of the offer", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.10 requires: '(a) the closing price ... of the securities of the offeree company which are the subject of the offer: (i) on the latest practicable date prior to publication of the document; (ii) on the latest business day immediately preceding the date of the initial announcement of the offer; and (iii) at the end of each of the 6 calendar months preceding the date of the initial announcement; and (b) the highest and lowest closing prices during the period between the start of the 6 months preceding the date of the initial announcement and the latest practicable date prior to the posting of the offer document, and the respective dates of the relevant sales.'\n\nThe first closing date lies in the future when the document is written, so its price can't be stated. The required prices let shareholders see the offer premium against the undisturbed price (the day before announcement), the trend over 6 months, the range, and the latest price. That context helps them judge whether the offer is attractive."},
    {t:"The closing price on the business day before the initial announcement", correct:false,
     why:"That's Rule 23.10(a)(ii), the undisturbed price before the offer became known."},
    {t:"The closing price at the end of each of the 6 months before the announcement", correct:false,
     why:"That's Rule 23.10(a)(iii)."},
    {t:"The closing price on the latest practicable date before publication", correct:false,
     why:"That's Rule 23.10(a)(i)."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.10", takeaway:"Undisturbed price, 6 monthly closes, high/low, latest." },

{ id:"tc7x-022", lesson:"TC7B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Vantor Ltd is offering its own shares for Meridian Ltd. Vantor is defending a large lawsuit that could cost it 20% of its net assets, and has a $300m bank loan secured on its main factory.",
  prompt:"Under Rule 23.12, must the offer document mention these?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: a paper offer must cover material litigation and borrowings", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.12 requires 'additional information ... when it is offering its securities in exchange for the securities of the offeree company', including '(k) details of any bank overdrafts or loans, or other similar indebtedness, mortgages, charges, or guarantees or other material contingent liabilities of the offeror and any of its subsidiaries, or, if there are no such liabilities, a statement to that effect; (l) details of any material litigation to which the offeror is, or may become, a party; (m) details of every material contract entered into with an interested person not more than three years before the date of the offer ...'.\n\nThe list also covers Vantor's business, incorporation, share capital and rights, whether the new shares rank pari passu, prices of its listed shares, outstanding convertibles, capital reorganisations and how documents of title will be issued.\n\nMeridian's shareholders are being asked to become Vantor shareholders, so they need prospectus-type information about Vantor. A lawsuit that could cost 20% of net assets and a secured $300m loan go directly to what Vantor shares are worth. GP 11's prospectus standard applies."},
    {t:"No: litigation and loans are Vantor's own private business, not Meridian's", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.12(k) and (l) require disclosure of indebtedness and material litigation in a securities exchange offer."},
    {t:"Only if Meridian's board specifically asks for them", correct:false,
     why:"The information is mandatory in a securities exchange offer. It doesn't depend on a request."},
    {t:"Only in a cash offer, where funding matters most", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.12 applies where the offeror 'is offering its securities in exchange'. In a cash offer, it doesn't apply."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.12(k)–(m); GP 11", takeaway:"Paper offer: prospectus-type disclosure of the offeror." },

{ id:"tc7x-023", lesson:"TC7B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Heron Ltd's offer for Ibis Ltd is backed by Jacana Ltd. Heron has agreed that, once the offer closes, it will transfer half of the Ibis shares it acquires under the offer to Jacana. Jacana already holds 4% of Ibis.",
  prompt:"Unless the Council agrees otherwise, what must Heron's offer document say about this?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the shares will be transferred, naming Jacana and stating its holding in Ibis", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 23.9: 'Unless otherwise agreed with the Council, the offer document must contain a statement as to whether or not any securities acquired pursuant to the offer will be transferred to any other persons, together with the names of the parties to any such agreement, arrangement or understanding and particulars of all securities in the offeree company held by such persons, or a statement that no such securities are held.'\n\nShareholders deciding whether to accept are entitled to know who will end up owning their shares. Here that is partly Jacana. So the document must say that acquired shares will be transferred, name Jacana as a party to the arrangement, and give particulars of Jacana's 4% holding. If Jacana held no Ibis shares, the document would have to say so.\n\nThe statement is needed even where nothing will be transferred: the Rule calls for a statement 'whether or not' any acquired securities will pass to others."},
    {t:"Nothing: a transfer agreed for after the offer closes is outside the document", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.9 is aimed at what happens to securities 'acquired pursuant to the offer'. A transfer after closing is exactly what must be disclosed."},
    {t:"Only that a transfer is planned; the other party's identity can stay confidential", correct:false,
     why:"The Rule requires 'the names of the parties to any such agreement, arrangement or understanding'. Jacana can't stay anonymous."},
    {t:"Only Jacana's name, as its 4% holding is already disclosed under the dealing rules", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 23.9 requires 'particulars of all securities in the offeree company held by such persons' in the offer document itself, as well as the fact of the transfer."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 23.9", takeaway:"Shares passing to someone else: say so, name them, give their holdings." }

]);

;
