/* DealCraft — Take-over Code Ch T7 · Lesson TC7C Offeree Board Circulars (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: SG Code Rule 24 and Notes (2026 amendments to r 24.1, Notes 2 and 5, r 24.3(a)(iv)). Replaces TC7C in takeoverCode7.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t3p4v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t3p4v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"tc7-022", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Under the amended Rule 24.1(a), what must the offeree board circular contain?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its opinion on the offer, alternatives included, with reasons, and its recommendation", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.1(a) now reads: 'The offeree board circular should: (i) set out the opinion of the board of directors of the offeree company on the offer (including any alternative offer), as well as the board's reasons for forming its opinion and should include views pursuant to Rule 24.2; and (ii) indicate whether or not the board of directors of the offeree company recommends to shareholders the acceptance or rejection of the offer (including any alternative offer) made, or to be made, by the offeror.'\n\nParagraph (i) is new in 2026. Before, the Rule asked only for the recommendation. Now shareholders get the board's reasoning, so they can judge the recommendation rather than simply follow it. The Rule 24.2 views are the board's comments on the offeror's plans for the company and its employees."},
    {t:"Whether the board recommends acceptance or rejection; reasons are optional", correct:false,
     why:"That was close to the pre-2026 wording. The amended Rule 24.1(a)(i) requires the board's opinion and 'the board's reasons for forming its opinion'."},
    {t:"The independent financial adviser's recommendation, which stands in for the board's", correct:false,
     why:"The board gives its own opinion and recommendation. The substance of the adviser's advice is disclosed separately under Rule 24.1(b). Only where the Council exempts every director does primary responsibility move to the adviser (Note 3)."},
    {t:"The board's opinion on the main offer only; alternatives are for the offeror to explain", correct:false,
     why:"Both limbs of the amended Rule expressly say '(including any alternative offer)'. A shareholder choosing between cash and a share alternative needs the board's view on each."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1(a); r 24.2", takeaway:"Opinion + reasons + recommendation, covering every alternative." },

{ id:"tc7-023", lesson:"TC7C", type:"tf", amended:true,
  prompt:"True or false: Since the 2026 amendments, an offeree board circular that says only \"the board recommends that shareholders reject the offer\" satisfies Rule 24.1(a).",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"That would have met the old wording, which asked only whether the board recommended acceptance or rejection. A bare recommendation now satisfies only paragraph (ii). Paragraph (i) separately requires the board's opinion, its reasons and its Rule 24.2 views."},
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.1(a)(i), added in 2026, requires the circular to 'set out the opinion of the board ... on the offer (including any alternative offer), as well as the board's reasons for forming its opinion and should include views pursuant to Rule 24.2'.\n\nA one-line recommendation tells shareholders what to do but not why. The reasons let them test the recommendation against the independent adviser's advice, which must also be summarised in the circular under Rule 24.1(b), and against their own circumstances."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1(a)", takeaway:"A bare recommendation is no longer enough." },

{ id:"tc7-024", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Tern Ltd offers $3.00 cash per Plover Ltd share, with an alternative of 1 Tern share plus $0.50 cash. Plover's independent financial adviser writes an opinion on the $3.00 cash offer and says nothing about the share alternative.",
  prompt:"Does the advice meet Rule 24.1(b)?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: independent advice must cover any alternative too, with its substance disclosed", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.1(b): 'The board of the offeree company must obtain competent independent advice on the offer (including any alternative offer) and the substance of such advice must be made known to its shareholders in the offeree board circular.'\n\nThe 2026 amendment replaced 'any offer' with 'the offer (including any alternative offer)'. The share alternative raises different questions from the cash offer: what Tern shares are worth, how liquid they are, and what risk the shareholder takes on. Advice that skips it leaves shareholders unadvised on half the choice. The adviser needs to cover both, and the circular must summarise that advice."},
    {t:"Yes: independent advice is needed only on the cash element of the offer", correct:false,
     why:"The amended Rule expressly covers 'any alternative offer'. The share alternative is arguably where advice matters most, because its value is harder to judge."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as Tern's offer document explains the share alternative", correct:false,
     why:"The offeror's description of its own shares isn't independent advice to the offeree board. Rule 24.1(b) needs advice from a competent independent adviser."},
    {t:"Yes, as long as Plover's board gives its own view on the share alternative", correct:false,
     why:"The board's opinion is a separate requirement under Rule 24.1(a). Rule 24.1(b) still requires independent advice on the alternative."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1(b)", takeaway:"Independent advice must cover every alternative." },

{ id:"tc7-025", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Plover's board considers that both Tern's cash offer and its share alternative can be recommended. Which suits a shareholder depends on tax position, investment horizon and appetite for Tern's shares.",
  prompt:"How can the board satisfy the recommendation requirement in Rule 24.1(a)(ii)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Say more than one can be recommended, depending on circumstances, and set out key factors", correct:true,
     why:"New Note 5 on Rule 24.1: 'Where there are alternative offers and: (a) more than one of the offers can, in the board's opinion, be recommended to shareholders; but (b) the appropriate action for individual shareholders to take will depend on various factors and their particular circumstances, the board may satisfy the requirement in Rule 24.1(a)(ii) by including statements to that effect and explaining the key factors which it considers that shareholders should take into account in making their decision as to what action to take.'\n\nA board can't know each shareholder's tax position or appetite for risk, so forcing it to pick one alternative could mislead. The Note lets it say both are acceptable while still guiding the choice. Here the board would explain, for example, that cash suits those wanting certainty and an exit, and the share alternative suits those willing to hold Tern."},
    {t:"It must pick one of the alternatives and recommend that one alone", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 exists for exactly this case: it lets the board say more than one alternative can be recommended."},
    {t:"It may make no recommendation at all and simply refer shareholders to their own advisers", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5 requires statements that more than one offer can be recommended and an explanation of the key factors. Silence doesn't meet Rule 24.1(a)(ii)."},
    {t:"It must ask the Council to exempt it from making any recommendation", correct:false,
     why:"No exemption is needed. Note 5 explains how the requirement can be met. Council exemptions (Note 3) are for directors with conflicts."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1, Note 5", takeaway:"Two good alternatives: say so, and explain the factors." },

{ id:"tc7-026", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario", amended:true,
  scenario:"Five of Curlew Ltd's seven directors recommend acceptance of an offer. The other two think it undervalues Curlew and recommend rejection.",
  prompt:"What does the amended Note 2 on Rule 24.1 expect?",
  options:[
    {t:"The dissenters publish their views, which Curlew will normally have to circulate too", correct:true,
     why:"Note 2: 'If the board of the offeree company is split in its views on the offer (or any alternative offer), the directors who hold dissenting views and are the minority on the board should also publish their opinions and recommendations. The Council will normally require that the offeree company circulate such directors' opinions and recommendations to its shareholders (in addition to that of the majority).'\n\nThe 2026 changes replace 'views' with 'opinions and recommendations', matching amended Rule 24.1(a), extend the Note to splits on an alternative offer, and make clear the minority's position goes out alongside the majority's. A split board is itself important information: two directors who think the price is too low may cause shareholders to look harder at the adviser's valuation."},
    {t:"The circular gives the majority recommendation only; dissenters may issue their own documents", correct:false,
     why:"The Council will normally require the offeree company itself to circulate the minority's opinions and recommendations."},
    {t:"The dissenters must resign before the circular is sent", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in the Note requires resignation. Disagreement is expected on a board and is disclosed, not removed."},
    {t:"The board must reach a unanimous position before the circular can go out", correct:false,
     why:"The Note assumes boards can split and provides for both positions to be published."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1, Note 2", takeaway:"Split board: both sides' opinions and recommendations go out." },

{ id:"tc7-027", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq", amended:true,
  prompt:"Tern's offer for Plover has a cash offer and a share alternative. Under the amended Rule 24.3(a)(iv), what must Plover's circular say about its directors' own shares?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether they'll accept, which alternative they'll elect for, and reasons if required", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.3(a)(iv): the circular must state 'whether the directors of the offeree company intend, in respect of their own beneficial shareholdings, to accept or reject the offer (and, if there are alternative offers, which alternative, if any, they intend to elect for and, if so required by the Council, the reasons for making that election)'.\n\nThe words in brackets are new in 2026. What directors do with their own shares is a strong signal. If the board recommends both alternatives under Note 5 but every director elects for cash, shareholders should know."},
    {t:"Whether they intend to accept or reject; their choice between alternatives is private", correct:false,
     why:"The amended Rule requires disclosure of 'which alternative, if any, they intend to elect for'."},
    {t:"Which alternative they elect for, with reasons in every case", correct:false,
     why:"Reasons are required only 'if so required by the Council'."},
    {t:"Nothing: directors' intentions are disclosed only once the offer closes", correct:false,
     why:"Intentions go in the offeree board circular, so shareholders have them when deciding."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.3(a)(iv)", takeaway:"Directors: accept or reject, and which alternative." },

{ id:"tc7-028", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Every director of Gull Ltd either sits on the offeror's board or will roll over into the offeror's group. The Council exempts all of them from making a recommendation on the offer.",
  prompt:"Who is primarily responsible for the recommendation to shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"The independent financial adviser", correct:true,
     why:"Note 3 on Rule 24.1: 'Directors who have an irreconcilable conflict of interests and those who have been exempted by the Council from making recommendations to shareholders on an offer should not join with the remainder of the board in the expression of its views on the offer. In such cases, the reasons for their exclusion should be clearly explained to shareholders. In the case where all the directors on the offeree company board have been exempted by the Council, the responsibility for making a recommendation to shareholders shall reside primarily with the independent financial adviser.'\n\nThis is common in management buy-outs and take-privates by a controller, where no director is independent. Shareholders still need a recommendation from someone without a stake in the outcome, so the adviser steps up."},
    {t:"The directors with the smallest conflicts", correct:false,
     why:"All have been exempted. There is no ranking of conflicts; the Note moves primary responsibility to the adviser."},
    {t:"The Council", correct:false,
     why:"The Council supervises the process. It doesn't recommend offers."},
    {t:"No one: shareholders decide unadvised", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders aren't left unadvised. The independent financial adviser carries the recommendation."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1, Note 3", takeaway:"Whole board exempt: the IFA recommends." },

{ id:"tc7-029", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Auk Ltd's board rejected an offer at $1.20. The offeror raises its offer to $1.45, and the board now wants to recommend acceptance.",
  prompt:"What does Note 4 on Rule 24.1 say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Normally allowed if justified, giving shareholders time, perhaps by press notice", correct:true,
     why:"Note 4: 'The Council will normally not object to the offeree board issuing an updated recommendation to its shareholders if the circumstances justify it. For example, the offeree board may decide to recommend an offer, after having rejected it initially, when the offeror increases the offer price. It is not acceptable, however, for the offeree board or its adviser to make a recommendation on the offer merely for tactical purposes when such recommendation is not in the best interests of all shareholders. The offeree board must give its shareholders sufficient time to consider the updated recommendation. To ensure prompt and wide dissemination of the updated recommendation, a paid press notice may be needed.'\n\nA price increase is the Note's own example. The timing point matters in practice: an update near the final closing date must leave shareholders time to act on it."},
    {t:"The board is bound by its first recommendation for the rest of the offer", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 permits updated recommendations where the circumstances justify them. A better price is the textbook case."},
    {t:"The board may switch recommendations at any time to keep the offeror negotiating", correct:false,
     why:"The Note says a recommendation made 'merely for tactical purposes' that isn't in the best interests of all shareholders is not acceptable."},
    {t:"Only the independent financial adviser may update a recommendation", correct:false,
     why:"The Note is addressed to the offeree board (and its adviser). The board can update its own recommendation."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1, Note 4", takeaway:"Update for real reasons, with time to consider." },

{ id:"tc7-030", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under Rule 24.3(a), which shareholdings must the offeree board circular state, and where is a nil statement not required?",
  options:[
    {t:"Offeree, director and IFA holdings; the IFA's needs no nil statement", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.3(a) requires: '(i) the shareholdings of the offeree company in the offeror; (ii) the shareholdings in the offeree company and in the offeror in which directors of the offeree company are interested; (iii) the shareholdings in the offeree company and in the offeror (in the case of a securities exchange offer only) owned or controlled by the independent financial adviser to the offeree company, or by funds whose investments are managed by the adviser on a discretionary basis (the funds need not be named); and (iv)' the directors' intentions. Rule 24.3(b): 'If in any of the above categories [with the exception of paragraph (a)(iii)] there are no shareholdings, then this fact should be stated.'\n\nThe adviser's holdings go to independence: an adviser whose funds hold offeree shares has an interest in the price. The offeror holdings matter only in a securities exchange offer, where the adviser is also valuing the offeror's shares."},
    {t:"Only the directors' holdings in the offeree company itself", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.3(a) also covers the offeree's holdings in the offeror and the adviser's holdings."},
    {t:"All of those, with a nil statement for every category that is empty", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.3(b) expressly excepts paragraph (a)(iii), the adviser's category."},
    {t:"All of those, naming every individual fund that the adviser manages", correct:false,
     why:"The Rule says 'the funds need not be named'."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.3(a), (b)", takeaway:"IFA holdings disclosed; no nil statement needed for them." },

{ id:"tc7-031", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Puffin Ltd has two directors' service contracts. Director A's has 18 months to run but Puffin can end it on 6 months' notice without compensation. Director B's has 3 years to run and can only be ended early on payment of compensation. Neither has been changed in the past year.",
  prompt:"Which must the circular disclose under Rule 24.7?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only B's", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.7: the circular 'must contain particulars of all service contracts of any director or proposed director with the offeree company or any of its subsidiaries which have more than 12 months to run and which cannot be terminated by the company within the next 12 months without paying any compensation. If there are no such contracts, this fact should be stated.'\n\nBoth limbs must be met. A's contract has 18 months to run but Puffin can end it within 12 months at no cost, so it fails the second limb. B's meets both. The concern is entrenchment: long contracts that are costly to end can deter a bidder, or reward directors on a change of control. The Note requires the name, expiry date, fixed and variable remuneration (with the formula)."},
    {t:"Both: each has more than 12 months to run", correct:false,
     why:"Both limbs must be met. A's contract can be ended within 12 months without compensation."},
    {t:"Neither, if the annual report already describes them", correct:false,
     why:"The Note on Rule 24.7: 'It is not regarded as sufficient to refer to the latest annual accounts ... or to state that the contracts are open for inspection at a specified place.'"},
    {t:"Only contracts entered into or amended in the 6 months before the announcement", correct:false,
     why:"Those trigger extra disclosure of the earlier contracts they replaced, but qualifying contracts are disclosed whenever they were made."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.7; r 24.7, Note", takeaway:"Over 12 months to run and costly to end: disclose." },

{ id:"tc7-032", lesson:"TC7C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each item in an offeree board circular to the rule that requires it.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"The board's comments on the offeror's plans for the company and its employees", r:"Rule 24.2"},
    {l:"Material contracts with interested persons in the 3 years before the offer period", r:"Rule 24.6"},
    {l:"Directors' service contracts with more than 12 months to run", r:"Rule 24.7"},
    {l:"Compensation for loss of office paid to an offeree director in connection with the offer", r:"Rule 24.8"}
  ],
  why:"Rule 24.2: the board 'should comment ... on statements regarding the offeror's intentions with respect to the offeree company and its employees made pursuant to Rule 23.2'.\nRule 24.6: a summary of 'each material contract with interested persons (not being a contract entered into in the ordinary course of business)' in the three years before the offer period, using the Rule 23.12 definition of interested person.\nRule 24.7: service contracts with more than 12 months to run that can't be ended within 12 months without compensation.\nRule 24.8(a): 'any payment or other benefit ... to any director ... as compensation for loss of office or otherwise in connection with the offer'. Rule 24.8 also covers agreements between a director and anyone else conditional on the offer, and material contracts of the offeror in which an offeree director has a material personal interest.",
  cite:"SG Code r 24.2, 24.6–24.8", takeaway:"Plans, contracts, service terms, director benefits." },

{ id:"tc7x-040", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The founders of Shrike Ltd, who are also its directors, hold 58% between them. They tell the board they will not accept an offer from Kestrel Ltd at any price, and the board circular recommends rejection. Minority shareholders complain that the board's attitude has killed the offer.",
  prompt:"What does Note 1 on Rule 24.1 say about this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its attitude will be decisive, but it must examine its reasons and be ready to explain them", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1 on Rule 24.1: 'A board whose shareholdings confer effective control over a company which is the subject of an offer must carefully examine the reasons behind the advice it gives to shareholders and must be prepared to explain its decisions publicly. Shareholders in companies which are effectively controlled by the directors must accept that in respect of any offer the attitude of their board will be decisive.'\n\nThe Code can't force controlling shareholders to sell. What it can do is require them to justify their advice openly, so that the minority understands why the offer failed. The amended Rule 24.1(a)(i) reinforces this by requiring the board's reasons in the circular."},
    {t:"The Council will require the founders to accept so the minority can sell", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in the Code compels a controlling shareholder to accept an offer. Note 1 accepts that the board's attitude will be decisive."},
    {t:"The founders must abstain from recommending, because they are conflicted as controlling holders", correct:false,
     why:"Holding shares isn't of itself an irreconcilable conflict under Note 3. Note 1 deals with controlling boards and requires them to explain their reasons."},
    {t:"The minority can apply to the Council to have the recommendation set aside", correct:false,
     why:"The Council doesn't set aside a board's recommendation because the minority dislikes it. The protection is transparency of reasons."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1, Note 1; r 24.1(a)(i)", takeaway:"Controlling board: decisive, but must explain itself." },

{ id:"tc7x-041", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Kestrel's offer document quotes the offeree's independent financial adviser as saying the offer is 'fair and reasonable'. What does Rule 24.1(c) require?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the adviser has consented, and not withdrawn consent, to its use in context", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.1(c): 'If any document issued to shareholders of the offeree company in connection with an offer includes a recommendation or an opinion of a financial adviser for or against acceptance of the offer, the document must, unless issued by the financial adviser in question, include a statement that the financial adviser has given and not withdrawn his consent to the issue of the document with the inclusion of his recommendation or opinion in the form and context in which it is included.'\n\nA quotation lifted out of context can change its meaning; a 'fair and reasonable' opinion may have come with important qualifications. The consent statement shows the adviser has approved how its words are used."},
    {t:"Nothing: an adviser's published opinion can be quoted freely by anyone", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.1(c) applies to 'any document issued to shareholders of the offeree company in connection with an offer', including the offeror's documents."},
    {t:"The full text of the adviser's letter must be reproduced alongside it", correct:false,
     why:"The Rule requires a consent statement, not reproduction of the whole letter."},
    {t:"The Council must pre-approve the quotation before the offer document is posted", correct:false,
     why:"The safeguard is the adviser's consent, stated in the document, not Council pre-vetting."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.1(c)", takeaway:"Quote an adviser → state its consent to form and context." },

{ id:"tc7x-042", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Owl Ltd receives a mandatory offer; the offer period began on 1 June. An Owl director bought Owl shares on 10 January and again on 15 March, and sold some on 20 June. Owl's circular is posted in mid-July.",
  prompt:"Which of the director's dealings must the circular detail under Rule 24.3(c)?",
  options:[
    {t:"All three: for a mandatory offer, from 6 months before the offer period to posting", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.3(c): 'If any party whose shareholdings are required by paragraph (a) to be disclosed has dealt for value in the shares in question during the period commencing 6 months (3 months in the case of a voluntary offer) prior to the beginning of the offer period and ending with the latest practicable date prior to the posting of the offeree board circular, the details, including dates and prices, must be stated. If no such deals have been made, this fact should be stated.'\n\nThis is a mandatory offer, so the window runs from 1 December. 10 January, 15 March and 20 June all fall inside it, and the director's interests must be disclosed under paragraph (a)(ii), so his dealings are caught. For a voluntary offer the window would start on 1 March and the January purchase would fall outside. The offeree circular mirrors Rule 23.3 for the offer document."},
    {t:"Only the 20 June sale, made during the offer period itself", correct:false,
     why:"The window starts well before the offer period: 6 months before it for a mandatory offer."},
    {t:"Only the 15 March and 20 June dealings, in the last 3 months", correct:false,
     why:"That would be right for a voluntary offer (3-month window from 1 March). For a mandatory offer the window is 6 months."},
    {t:"None, because the directors' dealings were already announced to SGX at the time", correct:false,
     why:"SGX notifications don't replace Rule 24.3(c). The circular must give dates and prices, or state that there were none."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.3(a)(ii), (c); r 23.3", takeaway:"Circular dealings: 6 months back (3 for voluntary), dates and prices." },

{ id:"tc7x-043", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which statement about the Notes on Rule 24.3 is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Holdings include derivatives; interests follow s 164; say when commitments end", correct:true,
     why:"Note 1: shareholdings in the offeree include securities being offered for or carrying votes, and 'convertible securities, warrants, options and derivatives in respect of' them. Note 2: 'interested' is 'interpreted according to section 164 of the Companies Act'. Note 3: for conversion rights and options, 'the exercise period and exercise price must be given'. Note 4: 'The offeree board circular must specify the circumstances, if any, under which commitments to accept the offer will cease to be binding.' Note 5: directors who resign as part of the transaction are still covered, and their holdings and dealings must be disclosed.\n\nTogether these stop disclosure being avoided by holding economic exposure through derivatives, or by resigning just before the circular."},
    {t:"Only ordinary shares count; derivatives are disclosed under Rule 12 instead", correct:false,
     why:"Note 1 expressly includes 'convertible securities, warrants, options and derivatives'."},
    {t:"Directors who resign before the circular is posted drop out of the disclosure altogether", correct:false,
     why:"Note 5: where directors resign as part of the transaction leading to the offer, 'this Rule applies to them'."},
    {t:"Irrevocable commitments are confidential and need not be described", correct:false,
     why:"Note 4 requires the circular to specify when commitments to accept will cease to be binding."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.3, Notes 1–5", takeaway:"Derivatives count; s 164 interests; resigners covered; say when irrevocables fall away." },

{ id:"tc7x-044", lesson:"TC7C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wren Ltd's shares are not quoted on any exchange. Wren receives an offer and prepares its circular. In the six months before the initial announcement, some shareholders sold shares privately at prices Wren knows of.",
  prompt:"What must Wren's circular disclose about its share capital under Rule 24.5?",
  options:[
    {t:"Capital, recent issues, convertibles and options, and known private sale prices", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.5 requires '(a) the authorised and issued capital, and the rights of the shareholders in respect of capital, dividends and voting; (b) the number of shares issued since the end of the last financial year; (c) details of outstanding instruments convertible into, rights to subscribe for, and options in respect of, securities being offered for or which carry voting rights ...; and (d) if the shares to which the offer relates are not quoted or dealt in on a securities exchange, all the information which the offeree company may have as to the number, amount and price at which any such shares have been sold' from 6 months before the initial announcement until the latest practicable date.\n\nFor an unlisted company there is no market price to compare with the offer. Recent private sale prices are the nearest substitute, so Wren must share what it knows."},
    {t:"Nothing about private sales, because there is no market at all for Wren's shares", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.5(d) exists precisely for unquoted shares, where private sale prices are the best evidence of value."},
    {t:"Only its authorised and issued capital, and nothing else", correct:false,
     why:"That is limb (a). Limbs (b) to (d) add shares issued, convertibles and options, and sale prices for unquoted shares."},
    {t:"The share capital of the offeror, not of Wren itself", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.5 is about the offeree. Offeror information is in the offer document under Rule 23."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.5", takeaway:"Unquoted target: disclose known private sale prices for 6 months back." },

{ id:"tc7x-045", lesson:"TC7C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Unless the Council consents, which of these must the offeree board circular contain about the offeree company under Rule 24.4?",
  options:[
    {t:"Three years of turnover, pre- and post-tax profit, and earnings and dividends per share", correct:true,
     why:"Rule 24.4(c) requires 'details, for the last three financial years, of turnover, exceptional items, net profit or loss before and after tax, minority interests, net earnings per share and net dividends per share'.\n\nThe full list, 'Except with the Council's consent': (a) names, descriptions and addresses of the directors; (b) principal activities; (c) the three-year figures above; (d) 'a statement of the assets and liabilities shown in the last published audited accounts'; (e) 'particulars of all known material changes in the financial position of the company subsequent to the last published audited accounts or a statement that there are no such known material changes'; (f) the (c) figures for any interim statement or preliminary announcement since those accounts; (g) significant accounting policies and points from the notes of major relevance; and (h) disclosure, with the approximate effect, of any change in accounting policy that makes figures not comparable to a material extent.\n\nShareholders get a factual picture of the company they are being asked to sell, alongside the board's view of the offer."},
    {t:"A profit forecast for the current financial year, reported on by its auditors", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.4 doesn't require a forecast. If the board chooses to publish one, Rule 25 governs how it is prepared and reported on."},
    {t:"Full audited accounts for each of the last five financial years, reproduced in full", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.4 asks for three years of key figures and a statement of assets and liabilities from the last published audited accounts, not five years of full accounts."},
    {t:"An independent valuation of every property it owns, at current market value", correct:false,
     why:"Rule 24.4 doesn't require valuations. Where a board does publish an asset valuation, Rule 26 sets the standards."}
  ],
  cite:"SG Code r 24.4", takeaway:"Circular: three years of key figures, latest balance sheet, material changes." }

]);

